     */
    fun get(caller: Caller, block: String): LuaHandle.ProjectBlock?

    /**
     * Every block the calling script can use: its own package's, and those the packages it depends on
     * export.
     */
    fun all(caller: Caller): List<LuaHandle.ProjectBlock>
}

/**
 * The project's recipes: those in `recipes/<id>.json`, and those scripts register. A recipe can take
 * the project's own items as ingredients (`{ item = "ruby" }`), which only a stack of that item fits,
 * however it looks; and a recipe takes a project item only where it names it, so a ruby built on paper
 * never crafts as paper. Players see a recipe in their recipe book once they've discovered it
 * (`player:discover_recipe`), but can craft it either way.
 */
interface NfRecipesApi {
    /**
     * Adds a recipe the server learns at once, sent to everyone online. `definition` says what
     * `recipes/<id>.json` would, in Lua spelling: `type` (`"shaped"`, `"shapeless"`, `"furnace"`,
     * `"blasting"`, `"smoking"`, `"campfire_cooking"`, `"smithing_transform"`, `"stonecutting"` or
     * `"station"`), then what that type takes: `pattern` and `key`, `ingredients`, `ingredient`,
     * `template`, `base` and `addition`, or `materials` (each `{ ingredient = ..., count = n }`),
     * `stations` and `station_range`; `result` (an `Item`), and `experience`, `cooking_time`, `group` and
     * `category`. An ingredient is an item id (`"minecraft:stick"`), a tag (`"#minecraft:planks"`) or a
     * project item (`{ item = "ruby" }`). Registering an id again replaces the recipe. It lasts as long as
     * the script that registered it (so a reloaded script registers it again in its body). An id a
     * `recipes/` file has, or anything that file would have an error for, is an error.
     */
    fun register(caller: Caller, id: String, definition: LuaValue)

    /**
     * Takes away a recipe a script registered, from the server and from everyone's recipe book. `false`
     * when there's no such recipe; one from `recipes/` is the file's, so removing it is an error (delete
     * the file instead).
     */
    fun remove(caller: Caller, id: String): Boolean

    /**
     * A `station` recipe, as `StationRecipe` (its `times` left out), or `nil` when there's no such recipe
     * or it isn't a station recipe. A recipe the game makes (a crafting table's) isn't one: the game
     * crafts those itself.
     */
    fun get(caller: Caller, id: String): StationRecipe?

    /**
     * The `station` recipes a player can make now: every station each needs is within its `station_range`
     * of them (or of `options.near`), and their inventory holds its materials, with `times` how many times
     * over. A material matches as a recipe's ingredient does: an item id or a `#tag` takes any stack of
     * that kind but a project item's, and a project item only its own stacks. A station is a block id, a
     * `#tag` of blocks or a project block, near when it's within the recipe's `station_range` (4 by
     * default) on every axis.
     */
    fun available(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, options: RecipeAvailableOptions?): List<StationRecipe>

    /**
     * Makes a `station` recipe for a player: takes its materials from their inventory and gives them its
     * result (what doesn't fit drops at their feet), `times` over, or as many times as they can when
     * that's fewer. A station it needs that isn't near makes nothing. A material matches as a recipe's
     * ingredient does: an item id or a `#tag` takes any stack of that kind but a project item's, and a
     * project item only its own stacks. A station is a block id, a `#tag` of blocks or a project block,
     * near when it's within the recipe's `station_range` (4 by default) on every axis. A recipe that isn't
     * a station one is an error.
     */
    fun craft(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, id: String, options: RecipeCraftOptions?): Long

    /** Every recipe's id: the project's files', then those scripts registered. */
    fun all(caller: Caller): List<String>
}

/**
 * Loot tables: the project's own (`loot/<id>.json`) and the game's. A roll picks from each of a
 * table's pools, weighing its entries against each other, and gives the items it picked. The same
 * `seed` always gives the same items (the editor rolls the same way). A table the project doesn't
 * have, or that the game doesn't, is an error; so is a game table that needs something the roll
 * doesn't say (a mob's table needs the mob, as `looted`).
 */
interface NfLootApi {
    /**
     * Rolls a loot table and gives the items it picked, in the order it picked them: a stack each, more
     * than one when a pick gives more than a stack holds. Nothing is put anywhere: give them to a player,
     * drop them or fill a chest with them. A game table is rolled by the server where the context says
     * (`location`, else its player's or looted entity's place), and needs one of them.
     */
    fun roll(caller: Caller, table: String, context: LootContext?): List<ItemData>

    /**
     * Rolls a loot table into an inventory the way the game fills a chest: each stack into an empty slot
     * picked at random (from the roll's seed), leaving what's there. In a player's inventory only the
     * hotbar and main inventory take loot. What finds no empty slot is left out. A game table is rolled
     * where the inventory is, unless the context says otherwise.
     */
    fun fill(caller: Caller, table: String, inventory: LuaHandle.Inventory, context: LootContext?): Long?
}

/** Boss bars: bars across the top of players' screens. */
interface NfBossbarsApi {
    /**
     * Makes a boss bar, shown to no one yet (`bar:show_to(player)`). It belongs to this script and goes
     * when it unloads. A key the options don't have is an error.
     */
    fun create(caller: Caller, options: BossBarOptions?): LuaHandle.BossBar
}

/**
 * The project's scoreboard teams: prefixes and suffixes on names, name colours and glow colours,
 * friendly fire, name tags and collision.
 */
interface NfTeamsApi {
    /**
     * Makes a team on the server's scoreboard, with no members yet (`team:add_member`). It belongs to this
     * script and goes when the script unloads, so make it in the script's body. A name that isn't letters,
     * digits and `_`, `-`, `.` or `+`, a name one of the project's teams already has, or a key the options
     * don't have is an error; to share a team between scripts, find it with `nf.teams.get` in the others.
     */
    fun create(caller: Caller, name: String, options: TeamOptions?): LuaHandle.Team

    /** One of the project's teams by name, or `nil` when it has none by that name. */
    fun get(caller: Caller, name: String): LuaHandle.Team?

    /**
     * Every team the project has now, in the order they were made. Teams made with `/team` or by other
     * plugins aren't among them.
     */
    fun all(caller: Caller): List<LuaHandle.Team>
}

/**
 * Runs a function at a time of day, every day or week, or by a cron expression: real time, as opposed
 * to ticks (`nf.after`, `nf.every`). Times are in the server owner's time zone (`schedules.time-zone`
 * in `config.yml`, the server's own zone by default), not the player's. Daylight saving follows the
 * zone's rules. A daily or weekly time the clocks skip runs the same distance after the gap begins
 * (`"02:30"` runs at 03:30 on the day clocks jump from 02:00 to 03:00), and one that happens twice
 * runs the first time only. A cron expression matches the clock as it reads: a time the clocks skip
 * doesn't happen that day, one for a single time of day that happens twice (`"30 1 * * *"`) runs the
 * first time only, and one that matches every half hour keeps matching through an hour that repeats. A
 * schedule belongs to the script that made it and ends when that script unloads (a reload, the server
 * stopping), so the script's body makes it again. It's checked once a tick, so a callback runs within
 * a tick (50 ms) of its time. A run the server missed (it was off at the time) is skipped, unless the
 * schedule has `catch_up = true` and an `id`: then it runs once, on start. What a schedule last ran is
 * kept in the server's store by `id`, per package.
 */
interface NfScheduleApi {
    /**
     * Calls `callback` every day at a time of day. Runs on the main thread, in the tick that finds the
     * time has come, like an `nf.every` callback. One that errors is logged with its file and line; the
     * schedule carries on.
     */
    fun daily(caller: Caller, time: String, callback: LuaFunction, options: ScheduleOptions?): LuaHandle.Schedule

    /**
     * Calls `callback` once a week, on a day at a time of day. Runs on the main thread, in the tick that
     * finds the time has come, like an `nf.every` callback. One that errors is logged with its file and
     * line; the schedule carries on.
     */
    fun weekly(caller: Caller, day: String, time: String, callback: LuaFunction, options: ScheduleOptions?): LuaHandle.Schedule

    /**
     * Calls `callback` whenever a cron expression matches. Runs on the main thread, in the tick that finds
     * the time has come, like an `nf.every` callback. One that errors is logged with its file and line;
     * the schedule carries on.
     */
    fun cron(caller: Caller, expression: String, callback: LuaFunction, options: ScheduleOptions?): LuaHandle.Schedule
}

/**
 * Players' money, as the server's economy plugin keeps it, through
 * [Vault](https://www.spigotmc.org/resources/vault.34315/): whichever economy is registered with it
 * (EssentialsX, CMI, …) answers, so a project never names one. Needs Vault and an economy plugin. The
 * plugin has to be on the server and enabled: a package that declares it (`requires`) while it isn't
 * is a problem on the project's problem list until it is, and calling without it is an error. A plugin
 * that is enabled after NetherForge counts from then on. The economy is looked up when it's asked, so
 * one that registers late (after NetherForge starts) works from then on. Amounts are plain numbers in
 * the economy's own currency; a player who is offline works as well as one who is online.
 */
interface NfEconomyApi {
    /** How much money the player has: `0` for someone the economy has no account for. */
    fun balance(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player): Double

    /**
     * Gives the player money. Returns their new balance, or `nil` when the economy refused (it has no
     * account for them, or can't hold that much). An amount that isn't positive and finite is an error.
     */
    fun deposit(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, amount: Double): Double?

    /**
     * Takes money from the player. Returns their new balance, or `nil` when the economy refused, usually
     * because they don't have that much (nothing is taken then). An amount that isn't positive and finite
     * is an error.
     */
    fun withdraw(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, amount: Double): Double?
}

/**
 * Placeholders, through [PlaceholderAPI](https://www.spigotmc.org/resources/placeholderapi.6245/):
 * fill in the `%player_name%`-style placeholders every plugin offers, and offer your own as
 * `%<namespace>_<key>%` to every plugin that reads them (scoreboards, chat formats, holograms). Needs
 * PlaceholderAPI. The plugin has to be on the server and enabled: a package that declares it
 * (`requires`) while it isn't is a problem on the project's problem list until it is, and calling
 * without it is an error. A plugin that is enabled after NetherForge counts from then on.
 */
interface NfPlaceholdersApi {
    /**
     * Replaces every placeholder in the text with its value, as PlaceholderAPI does: `"Hello
     * %player_name%"` becomes `"Hello Alex"`. A placeholder nobody answers is left as written.
     * Placeholders registered with `nf.placeholders.register` are answered too. A value is plain text, not
     * MiniMessage: if it comes from players, `nf.text.escape` the result before sending it as MiniMessage.
     */
    fun parse(caller: Caller, text: String, player: LuaHandle.Player?): String

    /**
     * Makes `%<namespace>_<key>%` a placeholder for the whole server: PlaceholderAPI asks `callback` for
     * the value whenever any plugin parses it. `callback` is called with the `key` (everything after the
     * first `_`, so `%shop_price_apple%` is `"price_apple"`) and the player it's for, if there is one, and
     * returns the text (or a number) to put there, or `nil` when it has no value for that key (the
     * placeholder is then left as written). It runs on the main thread when the server asks from the main
     * thread; PlaceholderAPI is also asked from other threads (async chat, scoreboard plugins), where
     * scripts can't run: there the placeholder answers the value `callback` last gave for that same key
     * and player, and `nil` before it ever has. The placeholder belongs to the script that registered it
     * and goes when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping). A namespace another script or
     * plugin already uses is an error.
     */
    fun register(caller: Caller, namespace: String, callback: LuaFunction)
}

/**
 * The project's particle effects (`particles/<id>/effect.json`): timelines of particles authored in
 * the editor, played at a place by scripts.
 */
interface NfParticlesApi {
    /**
     * Starts playing an effect at a place, from its first tick. It plays for its `duration` (or, looping,
     * until it's stopped) and belongs to the script that played it: when that script unloads, the effect
     * ends (reason `"unloaded"`). Only players within 32 blocks see it (128 for an emitter with `force`),
     * and of those only `viewers` when given. An effect the project doesn't have, a key `options` doesn't
     * take, or an option out of range is an error, and so is an effect whose file has errors and never had
     * a good version (one that did plays its last good version). More than 1024 effects playing on the
     * server at once is an error too: stop the ones you're done with.
     */
    fun play(caller: Caller, effect: String, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3, options: ParticlePlayOptions?): LuaHandle.Effect?
}

/**
 * The project's cutscenes (`cutscenes/<id>.json`): camera paths keyed in the editor, played for one
 * player at a time.
 */
interface NfCutscenesApi {
    /**
     * Starts a cutscene for a player, from its first moment. While it plays, the player spectates a camera
     * that follows the cutscene's path, and can't move or interact. Their game mode, position, flying
     * state and anything riding are put back when it ends, however it ends (including when they leave, a
     * reload or the server stopping); someone who was riding something is dismounted and isn't put back on
     * it. The camera's chunk is kept loaded while it's there, so a path may start and go anywhere in the
     * world, however far from the player. It belongs to the script that played it: when that script
     * unloads, it ends (reason `"unloaded"`). A player who is already watching one has it end (reason
     * `"replaced"`) and the new one start without putting them back first, so cutscenes chain without a
     * flash of the world they were in. A cutscene the project doesn't have, a key `options` doesn't take,
     * or one whose file has errors and never had a good version (one that did plays its last good version)
     * is an error. A player who is offline, or a dead one, gets `nil`.
     */
    fun play(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, cutscene: String, options: CutscenePlayOptions?): LuaHandle.Cutscene?

    /** Ends the cutscene a player is watching now, with reason `"stopped"`, and puts them back. */
    fun stop(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** The cutscene a player is watching now. */
    fun current(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.Cutscene?
}

/**
 * Screens: walls of maps, 128 by 128 pixels each, that scripts draw on (pixels, shapes, text,
 * pictures) and play videos on. The project's own are its `screens/<id>/screen.json` files; a script
 * can make more. What a screen shows is sent to each player who can see it, a change at a time, so
 * drawing every tick costs only what changed.
 */
interface NfScreensApi {
    /**
     * One of the project's screens (`screens/<id>/screen.json`). A screen the project doesn't have, or one
     * whose file has errors and never had a good version, is an error.
     */
    fun get(caller: Caller, screen: String): LuaHandle.Screen

    /** Every one of the project's screens, and every screen a script made that's still there. */
    fun all(caller: Caller): List<LuaHandle.Screen>

    /**
     * Makes a screen. It belongs to the script that made it: when that script unloads, the screen goes,
     * its frames taken down. With a `key`, the screen's maps keep their numbers for good (a map item
     * handed out, or a frame someone hung, still shows it after a restart, once a script makes it again
     * with the same key); without one, they're new each time. A size outside 1 to 16 maps is an error.
     */
    fun create(caller: Caller, options: ScreenOptions): LuaHandle.Screen

    /**
     * The fonts text can be drawn in: `default` (the game's own map font) and every font of the project's
     * (`screen_fonts/<id>/`).
     */
    fun fonts(caller: Caller): List<String>

    /**
     * How big `text` would be drawn with `options`, in pixels, without drawing it. An unknown font is an
     * error.
     */
    fun measureText(caller: Caller, text: String, options: ScreenTextOptions?): ScreenTextSize

    /**
     * The colour a map would really draw for `color`: the nearest it can show, as `#rrggbb`, or `"clear"`
     * for `"clear"`. A colour that isn't written `#rrggbb` is an error.
     */
    fun mapColor(caller: Caller, color: String): String

    /**
     * The screen a player is looking at and the pixel at the middle of their view, within `distance`
     * blocks; `nil` when they look at none, or something solid is in the way. Screens hidden from the
     * player are looked through.
     */
    fun lookingAt(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, distance: Double?): ScreenPoint?
}

/**
 * Files in the one directory scripts may keep things in: the only state a script has that survives a
 * restart.
 */
interface NfFilesApi {
    /**
     * A file or folder in the data directory, which survives restarts and reloads. A handle is a path, not
     * an open file: nothing is read until you ask. Paths that would leave that directory (`..`,
     * backslashes, names starting with a dot) give `nil` rather than an error. No argument is the
     * directory itself.
     */
    fun get(caller: Caller, path: String?): LuaHandle.File?
}

/**
 * Helpers for the MiniMessage text players see: escaping what players typed, stripping tags, resource
 * pack glyphs, moving the cursor, and measuring.
 */
interface NfTextApi {
    /**
     * Escapes text so MiniMessage shows it as typed rather than reading tags in it. Use it on anything a
     * player wrote (a name they chose, a dialog answer, a chat line) before putting it into MiniMessage:
     * otherwise they can add colours, or worse, `<click:run_command:...>`.
     */
    fun escape(caller: Caller, text: String): String

    /** MiniMessage with its tags taken out: the plain text a player would read, for logs and comparisons. */
    fun strip(caller: Caller, text: String): String

    /**
     * The character a resource pack glyph is drawn as, for building text by hand: a glyph wider than 256
     * pixels is drawn in pieces, so it's those pieces' characters and the moves between them. In
     * MiniMessage text the tag `<glyph:ui/coin>` is usually simpler: it works anywhere text does (names,
     * lore, chat, titles, dialogs, text displays) and draws the picture in its own colours, never bold,
     * while this text is tinted by the text colour around it like any other (wrap it in `<white>` to avoid
     * that, and keep it out of `<bold>`, which pulls a wide glyph's pieces apart). `nil` while the
     * project's resource packs have errors (nothing is built then); a glyph no resource pack has is an
     * error.
     */
    fun glyph(caller: Caller, glyph: String): String?

    /**
     * Characters that move the cursor `pixels` GUI pixels (negative is left) and draw nothing, for
     * building text by hand: what the tag `<space:N>` inserts. In MiniMessage the tag is usually simpler,
     * and `<at:N>…</at>` draws something N pixels away without moving what follows
     * (`<at:-30><glyph:ui/head></at>King Slime`; add `from=center`, `from=start` or `from=end` to count N
     * from the middle, start or end of the line, and `align=right` or `align=center` to put that edge of
     * what it holds there). The characters are the resource pack's, so they need one: `nil` while there's
     * none (the project has no resource packs or blocks). Inside `<bold>` each character is a pixel wider,
     * which the tag isn't. Held to ±2047.
     */
    fun space(caller: Caller, pixels: Long): String?

    /**
     * How wide a line of MiniMessage text draws in the game's default font, in pixels (GUI pixels: the
     * font is 8 tall), for laying out menu titles and text displays. Tags draw nothing, except `<bold>` (a
     * pixel more per character), `<glyph:ui/coin>` (the glyph's own width) and `<space:N>` (N, which may
     * be negative); what an `<at:N>` holds takes no width. Text with line breaks measures as its widest
     * line. It reads the default font's widths from `fonts/default.json`, which the editor writes into the
     * project from your Minecraft install. `nil` when it can't be measured exactly: that file is missing
     * (open the project in the editor with Minecraft imported), or the text uses a character it doesn't
     * cover.
     */
    fun width(caller: Caller, text: String): Long?
}

/**
 * Resource pack looks (item models, glyphs, skins, tooltips, equipment looks, sounds) handed from one
 * package's code to another's. A look is a value, not a use: any script can show any look the server
 * has, by its full name `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>`, whether or not its package exports it or is one
 * the script's package depends on. That's how a project hands a package it uses looks of its own to
 * draw with (see [Packages](packages.md#looks-are-values)).
 */
interface NfLooksApi {
    /**
     * A look's full name, `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>`, as the calling script's package names it:
     * `"nether/pawn"` in the project `chess_server` is `"chess_server:nether/pawn"`, which means the same
     * in any package's code. Hand that to a package, never the bare name: a bare name means the package's
     * own look wherever it's used. A name that already has a namespace comes back as it is. It's a plain
     * string, so it can be kept in saved data (`this:data()`). Whether the look is there isn't checked
     * here, but where it's shown; a name that isn't shaped like a look is an error.
     */
    fun qualify(caller: Caller, look: String): String
}

/** JSON text to and from Lua values. */
interface NfJsonApi {
    /**
     * A value as JSON text, written the way a `data()` table is saved: tables numbered from 1 become
     * arrays, other tables objects (with string keys, in order), and the API's own values one-key objects
     * that `nf.json.decode` turns back into them: `vec3(1, 2, 3)` is `{"$vec3":[1,2,3]}`, and `Location`s
     * and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles have tags of their own. A key of yours that
     * would read as a tag (`"$vec3"`) is written with one more `$`; any other key (`"$ref"`) is written as
     * it is. A value JSON can't hold (a function, another kind of handle, a table that contains itself, a
     * number key outside a list) is an error naming where it was (`value.callback: a function can't be
     * written as JSON`).
     */
    fun encode(caller: Caller, value: LuaValue): String

    /**
     * JSON text as a Lua value: objects and arrays become tables, and what `nf.json.encode` tags becomes a
     * `Vec3`, `Location` or handle again. `nil` for text that isn't JSON (and for `null`), so text from a
     * player or a file can be tried without guarding.
     */
    fun decode(caller: Caller, text: String): Any?
}

/**
 * Requests to web services (a Discord webhook, a status API). A package declares the hosts it talks to
 * in `netherforge.json`'s `requires` (`"http": ["discord.com"]`, or `"*.discord.com"` for every name
 * under it), and the server owner sees every host the project and its packages declare when it starts
 * (`/nf requires`); a request to any other host is an error. Every request is held to the package
 * whose code makes it. The server looks a name up once, checks the addresses it leads to and connects
 * to exactly the address it checked: a loopback, private, link-local, shared (`100.64.0.0/10`),
 * unique-local or IPv4-mapped address is refused even when its name is declared, unless the server
 * owner turned on `http.allow-private-addresses` in `config.yml` (for a service on the same machine or
 * network). Redirects (at most 5) are followed, and each one is held to the same rules, host
 * declaration included. The server's proxy settings are never used. Only `http` and `https` URLs work.
 * The server owner also limits requests in `config.yml`'s `http:` section: how large a request and a
 * response may be, how long a request may take, and how many a package may make a minute. Connections
 * that stay open (`stream`, `websocket`) are held to the same hosts and addresses, count as a request
 * when they open, and have limits of their own in `http.connections`.
 */
interface NfHttpApi {
    /**
     * Sends an HTTP request and gives the response. Any response is a result, whatever its status: a `404`
     * or `500` is `response.status`, not an error, so check it. `nil, err` is for a request that couldn't
     * be made or finished: a name that doesn't resolve, an address that isn't allowed, a refused
     * connection, a timeout, a response over the size limit, too many requests this minute, a redirect to
     * a host the package didn't declare, or more than 5 redirects. A mistake in the call itself (a URL
     * that isn't `http` or `https`, a host the package didn't declare, a body over the limit, a header
     * that can't be sent) is an error at the script's line. It works off the main thread, so its response
     * arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `response, err` once it's done, or call it
     * inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `response, err`. Without a callback
     * outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `response` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun request(caller: Caller, options: HttpRequestOptions): CompletionStage<HttpResponse>

    /**
     * Opens a request whose response keeps coming (an NDJSON feed, Server-Sent Events, any chunked stream)
     * and hands it over piece by piece as the stream's `message` events, on the main thread, as the server
     * sends it. It returns at once: the connection is made off the main thread, and its `open` event says
     * when the response has begun (with its status, which may be an error's: check it). It ends once, with
     * its `end` event saying why: the server finished, `close()` was called, it failed, an owner's limit
     * was reached, nothing came for too long, or the script that opened it unloaded. Nothing reconnects by
     * itself: open it again from `end` if you want to, waiting longer each time. Redirects are followed as
     * for `request`. The hosts, addresses and request rules are `request`'s, and opening counts as a
     * request against the package's rate; a mistake in the call is an error at the script's line, and a
     * limit the owner set (too many open, the request rate) ends the stream at the next tick with reason
     * `"limit"`.
     */
    fun stream(caller: Caller, options: HttpStreamOptions): LuaHandle.HttpStream

    /**
     * Opens a WebSocket (a `ws://` or `wss://` URL): a connection both sides send text messages over, for
     * as long as it stays open (a chat bridge, a game feed, Discord's gateway). It returns at once: the
     * connection is made off the main thread, and the socket's `open` event says when it's ready (what
     * `send` sends before then waits for it). Each text message from the other side is a `message` event
     * on the main thread, in order. It ends once, with its `end` event saying why. Nothing reconnects by
     * itself. The host must be one the package declares in `requires.http`, as for `request` (the same
     * declaration covers both), and the address rules are `request`'s; redirects aren't followed. A
     * mistake in the call is an error at the script's line, and a limit the owner set (too many open, the
     * request rate) ends it at the next tick with reason `"limit"`.
     */
    fun websocket(caller: Caller, options: WebSocketOptions): LuaHandle.WebSocket
}

/**
 * Web requests to the server: webhooks from stores, CI and chat services, "log in with…" callbacks,
 * small pages and JSON for a companion site. A package declares `"requires": { "web": true }` in its
 * `netherforge.json`, and its routes live under its own path, `/<namespace>/`, so packages can't
 * collide. They're served only once the server owner turns web routes on (`web.enabled` in
 * `config.yml`), on the port the resource pack is served from, usually behind a reverse proxy that
 * adds HTTPS; until then a package that declares them has a warning (`runtime.web-disabled`). Every
 * request waits for the main thread: handlers run there, under the script's budget, like any handler,
 * and the owner limits how large a request may be, how many each address may make a minute and how
 * many may wait (`web:` in `config.yml`). `/nf requires` lists every route.
 */
interface NfWebApi {
    /**
     * Answers requests for `method` at `path`, under the package's own `/<namespace>/`:
     * `nf.web.route("POST", "/hooks/store", …)` in package `shop` answers `POST /shop/hooks/store`. A
     * segment `:name` matches any one segment and hands it over as `request.params.name`; a last segment
     * `*name` matches the rest of the path, slashes and all. `handler` is called with the request and a
     * response to answer it with, now (`response:send`) or later, from a task or a callback (an
     * `nf.http.request`, a database query); one not answered within `web.handler-timeout-seconds` is
     * answered `504`. A `GET` route answers `HEAD` too. The route belongs to the script that made it and
     * goes when it unloads; requests still waiting then are answered `503`. Two routes of one package for
     * the same method and path, a path that isn't one, and a path a login (`nf.web.oauth`) uses are
     * errors.
     */
    fun route(caller: Caller, method: String, path: String, handler: LuaFunction): LuaHandle.Route

    /**
     * The address the world reaches the calling package's `path` at: `web.public-url` in `config.yml` (the
     * server's own address when it's empty), then `/<namespace>`, `path` and the query. For a link to put
     * in chat, a webhook to register with another service, a login's address. `nil` while the server owner
     * hasn't turned web routes on.
     */
    fun url(caller: Caller, path: String?, query: Map<String, String>?): String?

    /**
     * Whether `signature` is the HMAC of `body` under `options.secret`, as webhook senders sign what they
     * send (GitHub's `X-Hub-Signature-256`, Stripe's and Shopify's): compared in constant time, so the
     * answer leaks nothing about the right one. Check it before acting on a webhook. Keep the secret in a
     * setting (`nf.config`), not in a script. A signature that isn't in `options.encoding` is `false`, not
     * an error.
     */
    fun verifySignature(caller: Caller, body: String, signature: String, options: SignatureOptions): Boolean

    /**
     * A "log in with…" flow (OAuth 2 with PKCE, the authorization code grant): `flow:begin(player)` gives
     * the address to send the player to, the service sends them back to the flow's own route
     * (`/<namespace>/oauth/<name>/callback`, which you register with the service as its redirect URI:
     * `nf.web.url("/oauth/<name>/callback")`), and the server trades the code for a token itself and
     * raises `complete` (or `fail`) on the flow. NetherForge keeps the state and the code verifier, ties
     * each to the player who began, uses each once and lets it expire after 10 minutes, so a forged or
     * replayed callback is refused. The token endpoint's host must be one the package declares in
     * `requires.http`. The flow belongs to the script that made it and goes when it unloads; logins begun
     * before a reload are forgotten. Two flows of one package with the same `name` are an error.
     */
    fun oauth(caller: Caller, options: OAuthOptions): LuaHandle.OAuthFlow
}

/**
 * Heavy computation in WebAssembly: a chess engine, pathfinding over a big grid, noise, compression, a
 * library compiled from C, C++ or Rust. A module is `wasm/<id>/module.wasm` in the project (or a
 * package's, exported), built for WASI preview 1 and single-threaded (`wasm32-wasip1`); every other
 * file in its folder is data it can read, never write, as files from its root (`/`). It runs off the
 * main thread, with no access to the server, the network or any file but its own data: it can only
 * answer the script that asked. A package declares it runs modules with `"requires": { "wasm": true
 * }`, and the server owner sees that (`/nf requires`) and limits it in `config.yml`'s `wasm:` section:
 * how many threads run modules, how much memory each may use, how long a call may take, and how many
 * instances and programs a package may keep. There are three ways to run one: `nf.wasm.instantiate`
 * for a library whose functions you call (`instance:call`), `nf.wasm.run` for a program that reads its
 * input and prints its answer, and `nf.wasm.start` for a program that keeps running and talks line by
 * line (a UCI chess engine). The server compiles every module off the main thread as the project
 * loads; one that couldn't be compiled fails when it's run, saying why (and the problem `runtime.wasm`
 * says so too). A module the project doesn't have is an error at the script's line. A script that runs
 * a module restarts when the module changes.
 */
interface NfWasmApi {
    /**
     * The functions a module exports, with their parameters' and results' types, in the order it exports
     * them: a program (a WASI command) exports `_start`. Empty until the server has compiled it, and for
     * one that couldn't be.
     */
    fun functions(caller: Caller, module: String): List<WasmFunction>

    /**
     * Makes an instance of a library module: its own memory, kept between calls, which `instance:call`
     * calls functions in. Its start function (and a reactor's `_initialize`) runs first. It fails when the
     * module couldn't be compiled, it needs more memory than `wasm.max-memory-mb`, the server's modules
     * together would hold more than `wasm.max-memory-total-mb`, or the package already holds
     * `wasm.max-instances-per-package` instances. The instance belongs to the script that made it. It
     * works off the main thread, so its instance arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with
     * `instance, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and
     * returns `instance, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `instance`
     * is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun instantiate(caller: Caller, module: String): CompletionStage<LuaHandle.WasmInstance>

    /**
     * Runs a program to its end with `options.stdin` as its input, and gives what it printed and its exit
     * code: for a tool that reads a question and prints the answer. It's stopped (`nil, err`) past
     * `wasm.max-call-seconds`, and what it prints is kept up to `wasm.max-output-bytes` of each stream. A
     * program that exits with a code other than 0 has still run: check `result.exit_code`. It works off
     * the main thread, so its result arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `result, err`
     * once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `result,
     * err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `result` is `nil` and `err`
     * says why.
     */
    fun run(caller: Caller, module: String, options: WasmRunOptions?): CompletionStage<WasmRunResult>

    /**
     * Starts a program that keeps running: each `send` is a line of its input, each line it prints is an
     * `output` event, on the main thread, in order, and it ends once, with its `end` event. It returns at
     * once; the program starts off the main thread, on a thread of its own while it runs, and belongs to
     * the script that started it. A limit the owner set (`wasm.max-processes-per-package`,
     * `wasm.max-processes-total`, memory) ends it at the next tick with reason `"limit"`; a module that
     * couldn't be compiled ends it `"failed"`.
     */
    fun start(caller: Caller, module: String, options: WasmStartOptions?): LuaHandle.WasmProcess
}

/**
 * The other servers of the network this one is part of: a proxy (Velocity) running NetherForge's proxy
 * plugin, which is the network's hub, and the servers behind it, each running NetherForge and
 * connected to the hub (`network:` in their `config.yml`). Scripts publish messages on channels every
 * server can listen to (`nf.network.channel`), ask one server and get its answer (`channel:request`),
 * keep values every server sees in shared maps (`nf.network.map`), know who's online on which server
 * (`players`, the `network_player_*` events), and send a player to another server with data that
 * arrives with them (`send_player`). A package declares it with `"requires": { "network": true }`, and
 * the server owner sees that (`/nf requires`). Channels and maps are named in the calling package's
 * namespace, so packages never see each other's unless one depends on the other. Everything goes
 * through the hub: a server that isn't connected (`is_connected()`) misses what's published meanwhile,
 * and what it sends returns `false` or fails. Delivery is at most once, and in order between any two
 * servers. On the editor's dev server, unless its `config.yml` puts it in a network, the server is a
 * network of its own, named `"dev"`, so channels and maps work there with no proxy (sending players
 * doesn't). A server whose owner hasn't put it in a network has none: its packages that declare it
 * have the warning `runtime.network-disabled`.
 */
interface NfNetworkApi {
    /**
     * This server's name in the network, as the proxy names it (`network.server-name` in `config.yml`);
     * `"dev"` on the editor's dev server when it's a network of its own, `"test"` in a script test. `nil`
     * on a server that isn't part of a network.
     */
    fun serverName(caller: Caller): String?

    /**
     * Whether this server is connected to its network's hub now. `network_connect` and
     * `network_disconnect` say when that changes.
     */
    fun isConnected(caller: Caller): Boolean

    /**
     * Every server of the network, as the proxy names them, with whether NetherForge on each is connected
     * and how many players are on it. Empty while this server isn't connected.
     */
    fun servers(caller: Caller): List<NetworkServer>

    /**
     * Everyone online in the network, on any server, this one included, as the proxy last said. Empty
     * while this server isn't connected.
     */
    fun players(caller: Caller): List<NetworkPlayer>

    /**
     * A player online anywhere in the network, by name (any case) or UUID; `nil` when nobody by that name
     * or UUID is.
     */
    fun findPlayer(caller: Caller, player: String): NetworkPlayer?

    /**
     * A channel: what's `publish`ed on it reaches every server whose scripts listen to its `message` event
     * (this one included), and a `request` on it reaches one server's `request` handlers. Getting it
     * subscribes nothing: a server hears a channel's messages while any of its scripts has a handler on
     * it.
     */
    fun channel(caller: Caller, name: String): LuaHandle.NetworkChannel

    /**
     * A shared map: keys (strings) and values the hub keeps for the whole network, which every server
     * reads at once from its own copy and writes through the hub. The hub saves them, so they outlive any
     * server and the proxy restarting. This server keeps a copy from the first time any script gets the
     * map until the project reloads: `is_ready()` once the hub has sent it, and `change` events after.
     */
    fun map(caller: Caller, name: String): LuaHandle.NetworkMap

    /**
     * Sends a player to another server of the network, through the proxy, with `data` for that server's
     * scripts: its `player_arrive` handlers hear it once they've joined there. The player may be on any
     * server, this one or another. It fails (`nil, err`) when this server isn't connected, the player
     * isn't online, the proxy has no such server, they're already there, or the proxy couldn't move them
     * (the server is down or full, a proxy plugin cancelled it). It works off the main thread, so its
     * moved arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `moved, err` once it's done, or call it
     * inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `moved, err`. Without a callback
     * outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `moved` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun sendPlayer(caller: Caller, player: PlayerOrString, server: String, data: LuaValue): CompletionStage<Boolean>
}

/**
 * Dates and durations as text, in the same unit as `nf.server.unix_time()`: milliseconds. For timers,
 * use ticks (`nf.after`, `nf.wait`); this is for showing and reading clock times and lengths of time.
 */
interface NfTimeApi {
    /** A moment as clock time, in English, written the way a pattern says. */
    fun format(caller: Caller, time: Long, pattern: String, options: TimeOptions?): String

    /**
     * Reads a clock time written the way a pattern says, as milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC. A pattern
     * with no time of day reads as midnight; one with an offset (`XXX`) in it uses the offset it reads
     * rather than `zone`. `nil` when the text doesn't match the pattern (it may come from a player); a
     * pattern without a year, month and day, or with a letter that isn't a pattern letter, is an error.
     */
    fun parse(caller: Caller, text: String, pattern: String, options: TimeOptions?): Long?

    /**
     * A length of time as short text, largest unit first and units that are 0 left out: `"1h 2m 5s"`, `"3d
     * 4h"`, `"45s"`. Less than a second is `"0s"`; a negative length starts with `-`.
     */
    fun duration(caller: Caller, milliseconds: Long): String

    /**
     * Reads a length of time written as numbers with units, as milliseconds: `"1h30m"`, `"1h 30m"`,
     * `"90s"`, `"2d"`, `"1.5h"`, `"250ms"`. The units are `d`, `h`, `m`, `s` and `ms`, in any order, each
     * at most once; spaces between them are fine. `nil` for text that isn't a duration (it may come from a
     * player).
     */
    fun parseDuration(caller: Caller, text: String): Long?
}

/**
 * Randomness `math.random` doesn't give: generators of your own that repeat for a seed, smooth noise,
 * and random ids.
 */
interface NfRandomApi {
    /**
     * Smooth 2D noise (simplex): a number from -1 to 1 that changes gradually as `x` and `z` do, the same
     * every time for the same arguments. For terrain heights, patches of blocks, wobble over time.
     */
    fun noise2(caller: Caller, x: Double, z: Double, options: NoiseOptions?): Double

    /**
     * Smooth 3D noise (simplex), like `noise2` with a third coordinate: caves, clouds, or 2D noise that
     * drifts over time (`y` as the tick).
     */
    fun noise3(caller: Caller, x: Double, y: Double, z: Double, options: NoiseOptions?): Double

    /** A new random UUID, like `"3f2b8c1e-7a4d-4e0b-9c55-0e8a6f1d2b7c"`: an id no one else will make. */
    fun uuid(caller: Caller): String
}

/**
 * What a test file runs on. A project's tests are the `*_test.lua` files anywhere in it
 * (`tests/shop_test.lua`), run by `netherforge test` (or the editor's Run tests) on a fake server: no
 * Minecraft, no network, a world that holds only what the test puts in it. A test file runs like a
 * module (it can `require` the project's modules, and files beside it) and registers its tests with
 * `nf.test.case`; each test runs alone, on a fresh server with the project loaded and its scripts
 * started, so one can never leave something behind for the next. The file's own code runs once for
 * every test, before it, so it's where to `require` and build what the tests share. Nothing here
 * exists on a real server: `nf.test` is `nil` there.
 */
interface NfTestApi {
    /**
     * Registers a test. It passes when `callback` returns, and fails at the line where an `assert` or
     * `error` in it (or in something it calls) went wrong. A script of the project that errors while the
     * test runs fails it too, naming that script and line. Two tests in one file can't have the same name.
     */
    fun case(caller: Caller, name: String, callback: LuaFunction)

    /**
     * Runs the server forward `ticks` ticks (20 are a second), whole ticks at a time: timers fall due,
     * `tick` handlers run, centities tick and mobs think, exactly as on a server, and everything scripts
     * started on other threads finishes first. When it returns, the last tick is done. Each call in those
     * ticks (a handler, a timer, a centity's tick) has its own budget and time limit, as on a server: none
     * of it counts against the test's own, which is for the test's code. One that runs out is reported as
     * on a server, at its file and line. Script errors during it are collected and fail the test when it
     * ends.
     */
    fun advance(caller: Caller, ticks: Long)

    /**
     * Joins a fake player and returns them: the server raises the join events (`player_join`) as it does
     * for a real one, and the player can then be sent messages, given items, moved and clicked at like any
     * other `Player`. Names are unique: a name already joined gives that player.
     */
    fun player(caller: Caller, name: String): LuaHandle.Player

    /**
     * Raises a server-wide event as the server would, to the scripts: whoever listens to `event` with
     * `nf.on` hears it, and, for an event the server raises about a player, an entity or a world, the
     * handlers on that handle (`player:on("chat")`) hear it first, in the order the server uses. Each can
     * `stop()` or `cancel()` it or set its writable fields. The few events the runtime stages itself (a
     * death, damage, a click on a centity) reach `nf.on` handlers only. `payload` is what the event
     * carries, the same table its handlers read (`event.player`, `event.message`): a field the event does
     * not have is an error naming the fields. What the server would then do with a cancelled event is not
     * simulated: read the result.
     */
    fun raise(caller: Caller, event: String, payload: LuaValue?): RaisedEvent

    /**
     * Clicks for a player as their client would, with what they hold filled in, and raises what the server
     * would for it: `player_interact` (the held project item's `interact` first, and a click on one of the
     * project's blocks reaches it), then, for a right click with an item that nothing cancelled,
     * `player_use_item` (the project item's `use` first). A left click is the attack button at a block or
     * the air: it doesn't break the block (`world:break_block` does that). A click on an entity is
     * `nf.test.raise("player_interact_entity", ...)`. Nothing the game would then do (an item thrown, a
     * door opened) is simulated: read what the handlers did.
     */
    fun click(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, button: String, options: TestClickOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Lets go of the right button for a player who was using an item (drawing a bow, raising a shield,
     * eating) before it finished, as their client would: raises `player_stop_using_item` with the item in
     * their main hand, or in their off hand when the main one is empty. Like the server, it is heard by
     * the player before `nf.on`. An error when both hands are empty.
     */
    fun release(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, ticks: Long)

    /**
     * The movement keys a player holds now, as their client says when they change: raises `player_input`
     * (the player's `input` first, then `nf.on`). A key left out is not held, so `nf.test.input(player,
     * {})` lets go of every key. It only raises the event: the player doesn't move, sneak or sprint for
     * it.
     */
    fun input(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, held: TestInputKeys)

    /**
     * A stack as a player's game was last sent it, after the `view` handlers: what their tooltip says,
     * where the server keeps the real stack (read that from `player:inventory()` or `menu:item`). The fake
     * server sends what changed at the end of each tick, as a real one does, and what
     * `player:refresh_items` finds looks different, so `nf.test.advance(1)` after a change. `nil` for an
     * empty slot, or one their game hasn't been sent.
     */
    fun shown(caller: Caller, player: LuaHandle.Player, slot: IntegerOrChoice, options: TestShownOptions?): ItemData?

    /**
     * The connections scripts opened with `nf.http.stream` and `nf.http.websocket` in this test, oldest
     * first, ended ones included. In a test nothing reaches the network: each connection is held by the
     * test, which answers it with `nf.test.accept`, talks over it with `nf.test.receive` and
     * `nf.test.sent`, and ends it with `nf.test.disconnect`. Everything else is as on a server: the
     * package must declare the host, the owner's limits apply (the defaults, in a test), and what the test
     * does reaches the scripts at the next tick, so `nf.test.advance(1)` after it.
     */
    fun connections(caller: Caller, url: String?): List<LuaHandle>

    /**
     * Answers a connection that's still connecting, as its server would: its `open` event follows at the
     * next tick. A stream opens with `options.status` (default `200`). Accepting one that isn't connecting
     * any more is an error.
     */
    fun accept(caller: Caller, connection: LuaHandle, options: TestAcceptOptions?)

    /**
     * Sends `text` from the far end. A WebSocket hears it as one `message`; a stream reads it as more of
     * its response, split by its `format` exactly as a server's would be (so `"a\nb\n"` is two lines, and
     * half a line waits for the rest). It accepts a connection that's still connecting first. An error
     * when the connection has ended, or when as many messages as `connections.max-queued-messages` are
     * already waiting for the scripts (advance to let them in).
     */
    fun receive(caller: Caller, connection: LuaHandle, text: String)

    /**
     * Every message scripts sent on a WebSocket so far, in order, whether or not it had opened (a stream
     * sends nothing: its request body is in its options).
     */
    fun sent(caller: Caller, connection: LuaHandle.WebSocket): List<String>

    /**
     * Makes a web request to the server, as a browser or a webhook sender would, and gives its answer: it
     * goes through the routes scripts made with `nf.web.route` and the owner's limits (the defaults, in a
     * test) exactly as a real one does, without a network, whether or not web routes are turned on. When
     * the handler answers later (from a task, a callback), the server runs forward tick by tick until it
     * does, as `nf.test.advance` would, up to `web.handler-timeout-seconds` (then it's `504`).
     */
    fun request(caller: Caller, method: String, path: String, options: TestRequestOptions?): TestWebResponse

    /**
     * What a login flow's token endpoint answers in this test: in a test the server doesn't call it, and
     * trades every code for this instead (by default `{ access_token = "test-token", token_type = "Bearer"
     * }`). A table without `access_token` makes the login `fail`, with its `error_description` or `error`.
     */
    fun oauthToken(caller: Caller, flow: LuaHandle.OAuthFlow, response: LuaValue)

    /**
     * Ends a connection from the far end, after what it already received: with `options.error` it breaks
     * (reason `"failed"`, `detail` the error), otherwise the server finishes it (`"finished"`, a WebSocket
     * with `options.code` and `options.reason`). Its `end` event follows at the next tick. Disconnecting
     * one that has ended does nothing.
     */
    fun disconnect(caller: Caller, connection: LuaHandle, options: TestDisconnectOptions?)

    /**
     * Stands in for a WebAssembly module for the rest of this test, so a test needn't run a heavy one (a
     * chess engine) to check the script around it. In a test modules really run, as on a server; once
     * stubbed, nothing of the module runs: an instance's `call` and `call_text` answer with
     * `stub.functions` (a function the stub doesn't have fails, `nil, err`), `run` with `stub.run`, and a
     * program `start`ed answers each line it's sent with the lines `stub.input` returns, as `output`
     * events at the next tick (closing its input ends it, exit code 0). Each stub function runs on the
     * main thread as the test's code.
     */
    fun stubWasm(caller: Caller, module: String, stub: TestWasmStub)

    /**
     * Adds another server to the test's network. In a test this server is part of a network of its own,
     * named `"test"`, whose hub is a real one in the test: every other server is one the test adds and
     * plays, with `network_publish`, `network_received`, `network_answer`, `network_write`, `network_join`
     * and `network_send`. Adding one that's there does nothing; the network starts with none.
     */
    fun networkServer(caller: Caller, name: String)

    /**
     * Another server publishes `value` on a channel, to every server listening: this one's `message`
     * handlers hear it at the next tick (`nf.test.advance(1)`).
     */
    fun networkPublish(caller: Caller, server: String, channel: String, value: LuaValue)

    /**
     * What another server has heard of this test's network so far: every message published on a channel it
     * listens to (it listens to every channel `network_received` or `network_answer` named for it, from
     * when it's named), oldest first.
     */
    fun networkReceived(caller: Caller, server: String, channel: String?): List<TestNetworkMessage>

    /**
     * How another server answers requests on a channel: `callback` gets what it was asked and who asked,
     * and returns the answer (an error in it is the asker's `err`). Requests are answered at the next
     * tick, as a real server's would be.
     */
    fun networkAnswer(caller: Caller, server: String, channel: String, callback: LuaFunction)

    /**
     * Another server asks this one on a channel, as `channel:request` there would, and gives this server's
     * answer: the server runs forward tick by tick until its `request` handlers have answered (they answer
     * the tick after, as a real server's would). A request that fails (nothing here handles requests on
     * the channel, a handler errored) is an error at the test's line, saying why.
     */
    fun networkRequest(caller: Caller, server: String, channel: String, value: LuaValue): Any?

    /**
     * Another server sets a key of a shared map (`nil` deletes it): every server with the map hears its
     * `change` at the next tick.
     */
    fun networkWrite(caller: Caller, server: String, map: String, key: String, value: LuaValue)

    /**
     * A player joins the network on another server, as the proxy would say: `nf.network.players()` lists
     * them and `network_player_join` is heard at the next tick. Joining them on a server they're not on
     * moves them there (`network_player_switch`). A player on this server is joined with `nf.test.player`,
     * which the network hears of too.
     */
    fun networkJoin(caller: Caller, server: String, name: String): NetworkPlayer

    /** A player on another server leaves the network: `network_player_quit` is heard at the next tick. */
    fun networkQuit(caller: Caller, name: String)

    /**
     * Another server sends a player here with `data`, as `nf.network.send_player` there would: they join
     * this server (`player_join`) and, the tick after, its `player_arrive` handlers hear `data`. They
     * needn't be in the network yet.
     */
    fun networkSend(caller: Caller, server: String, name: String, data: LuaValue): LuaHandle.Player
}

/**
 * One spawned centity: `this` in the centity's script, and what `nf.centities.spawn`,
 * `nf.centities.get` and `nf.centities.all` return. A handle stays valid as long as the instance
 * exists; after it's removed its methods return `nil` or `false` rather than erroring.
 */
interface CentityApi {
    /**
     * The instance's id, unique and stable across restarts. Keep this rather than the handle if you need
     * to find it later.
     */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.Centity): String

    /** Which centity it is: its folder name under `centities/`. `nil` once removed. */
    fun kind(self: LuaHandle.Centity): String?

    /** Whether the instance is still in the world. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it appeared by itself, from its `spawning` rules in `centity.json`, and is still temporary:
     * it isn't saved, it's removed when no player is near, and it counts against its cap. `false` for one
     * a script spawned, one `keep` has kept, and once it's removed.
     */
    fun isNatural(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether a script spawned it temporary (`nf.centities.spawn`'s `temporary` or `lifetime`), and it
     * still is: it isn't saved, and it goes when the script that spawned it unloads, or when its lifetime
     * is up. `false` for one `keep` has kept, and once it's removed.
     */
    fun isTemporary(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * How many ticks a temporary centity with a `lifetime` has left before it's removed, or `nil` for one
     * without (or once it's removed).
     */
    fun lifetime(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Long?

    /**
     * Gives it this many ticks more before it's removed, from now. A centity with a lifetime is temporary,
     * so one that wasn't becomes so: it's no longer saved, and goes when its lifetime is up (one spawned
     * temporary still goes with its script too). Leave it out (or `nil`) to take its lifetime away; it
     * stays temporary until `keep`.
     */
    fun setLifetime(self: LuaHandle.Centity, ticks: Long?): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes a natural or temporary centity a normal one: it is saved from now on (it comes back after a
     * restart), is never removed for being far from players or because a script unloaded or a lifetime ran
     * out, and no longer counts against a cap. Nothing happens to one that is already normal.
     */
    fun keep(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /** One of its nodes by name, or `nil` if it has none by that name. */
    fun node(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String): LuaHandle.Node?

    /** Every node, parents before their children. */
    fun nodes(self: LuaHandle.Centity): List<LuaHandle.Node>

    /** Its root nodes (those with no parent), in the file's order. Every other node hangs from one of them. */
    fun roots(self: LuaHandle.Centity): List<LuaHandle.Node>

    /**
     * Adds a root node: one with no parent, whose translation is in the centity's own space, as if
     * `centity.json` had one more. A node a script adds is **runtime state**, like a rest pose a script
     * sets: it takes part in everything a declared node does (its transform follows its parent, a display
     * and an interaction entity are spawned for it and sent to players, a hitbox takes clicks and `click`
     * bubbles up through its parents, a `physics` body is simulated and collides, `raycast` hits it)
     * except what a file names: an animation clip can't drive it, because clips are written against the
     * file's nodes, though it follows its animated parent. It is **not saved**: after a restart, and when
     * its centity is reloaded, it's gone, and the script's body, which runs again, is where to add it.
     * Only nodes a script added can be removed; the nodes `centity.json` declares can't be. A name that's
     * already a node's, or isn't a usable node name (letters, digits, `_` and `-`, at most 64 characters),
     * is an error, and so is a definition the file's rules would refuse. Returns `nil` once the centity is
     * removed.
     */
    fun addNode(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String, definition: LuaValue?): LuaHandle.Node?

    /**
     * Its `properties` from its `centity.json`: the project's own facts about it, any JSON (`{ damage =
     * 12, class = "melee" }`), as a table. A new table each call, so keep it rather than ask in a loop. An
     * empty table when the file has none, `nil` once it's gone.
     */
    fun properties(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Any?

    /**
     * The box around all its hitboxes as they're shown now (each shown node's boxes, turned and placed),
     * in the world: what `world:overlap_box` and the other spatial queries test against. `nil` for a
     * centity with no hitboxes showing, or once it's removed.
     */
    fun bounds(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Bounds?

    /**
     * Where it stands, in which world, and which way it faces, or `nil` once it's removed. The location's
     * `yaw` is the centity's (`centity:yaw()`); its `pitch` is `nil`, because a centity only turns about
     * the vertical.
     */
    fun location(self: LuaHandle.Centity): LuaLocation?

    /** Where it stands in its world: the anchor every node is placed relative to. `nil` once it's removed. */
    fun position(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Vec3?

    /** The world it's in, or `nil` once it's removed. */
    fun world(self: LuaHandle.Centity): LuaHandle.World?

    /**
     * Which way it faces, in degrees, or `nil` once it's removed. Minecraft's convention: 0 faces south
     * (+z), 90 west (−x), 180 north, −90 east, always given back between −180 and 180. Its +Z axis points
     * that way, and every node is turned with it about its position.
     */
    fun yaw(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Double?

    /**
     * Turns the whole centity to face a yaw, about the vertical through its position: every node, its
     * hitboxes and its physics bodies turn with it (a moving body keeps going, turned by as much). The
     * turn is saved. Pitch and roll aren't a centity's: rotate a root node for those. Does nothing once
     * it's removed.
     */
    fun setYaw(self: LuaHandle.Centity, degrees: Double)

    /**
     * Turns it to face a world position, as `set_yaw` does: only about the vertical, so how far above or
     * below the point is makes no difference. A point straight above or below its position leaves it as it
     * is.
     */
    fun lookAt(self: LuaHandle.Centity, point: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * A world position in the centity's own space: what a root node's translation would be to stand there.
     * The centity's own space has its origin at the centity's position (`centity:position()`) and is
     * turned by its yaw (`centity:yaw()`): its +Z is the way the centity faces, its Y is the world's. It's
     * the space a root node's translation is in. `nil` once it's removed.
     */
    fun toLocal(self: LuaHandle.Centity, point: Vec3): Vec3?

    /**
     * A position in the centity's own space as a world position. The centity's own space has its origin at
     * the centity's position (`centity:position()`) and is turned by its yaw (`centity:yaw()`): its +Z is
     * the way the centity faces, its Y is the world's. It's the space a root node's translation is in.
     * `nil` once it's removed.
     */
    fun toWorld(self: LuaHandle.Centity, point: Vec3): Vec3?

    /**
     * Moves the whole centity: to a `Location` (turning to face its `yaw`, when it has one), or to a
     * position in its current world. It keeps its own facing when the destination has none; a location's
     * `pitch` is ignored.
     */
    fun teleport(self: LuaHandle.Centity, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes it ride a player: it moves with them and turns with their body, its displays and hitboxes at
     * the anchor coming along, until `stop_following`, a `teleport`, or a `move_to` ends it. Not for a
     * centity with physics bodies.
     */
    fun follow(self: LuaHandle.Centity, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Stops it following its player, where it is now. `false` when it wasn't following, or once it's
     * removed.
     */
    fun stopFollowing(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * One of it's stats. A stat is a number by name (`"defense"`, `"move"`, `"damage_melee"`): the sum of
     * what every buff it has adds (its file's `stats`) and every modifier a script set (`set_modifiers`),
     * 0 when nothing gives it any. What a stat means is the scripts': the engine only adds them up.
     */
    fun stat(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String): Double

    /**
     * Every stat it has that isn't 0, by name. A stat is a number by name (`"defense"`, `"move"`,
     * `"damage_melee"`): the sum of what every buff it has adds (its file's `stats`) and every modifier a
     * script set (`set_modifiers`), 0 when nothing gives it any. What a stat means is the scripts': the
     * engine only adds them up.
     */
    fun stats(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Map<String, Double>

    /** What a script's `source` adds to it's stats (`set_modifiers`), or `nil` when it adds nothing. */
    fun modifiers(self: LuaHandle.Centity, source: String): Map<String, Double>?

    /**
     * Sets what `source` adds to it's stats, replacing what it added before: one call per source, each
     * time it changes (a piece of armour put on), and `nil` takes it all away. Buffs and modifiers are
     * runtime state: a restart clears them; a player's last while the server runs, offline too, and count
     * down only while they're online.
     */
    fun setModifiers(self: LuaHandle.Centity, source: String, modifiers: Map<String, Double>?): Boolean

    /**
     * Gives it a buff (`buffs/<id>.json`) for a number of ticks: it takes away the buffs it `cures`, and
     * doesn't take hold while one it's `blockedBy` lasts. Given again while it lasts, its file's
     * `stacking` says what happens to the time. Its `buff_add` handlers may change the ticks or cancel it.
     * While it lasts its `stats` are added to it's; when it runs out, `buff_end` hears it. Buffs and
     * modifiers are runtime state: a restart clears them; a player's last while the server runs, offline
     * too, and count down only while they're online. A buff the project doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun addBuff(self: LuaHandle.Centity, buff: String, ticks: Long): Boolean

    /** Takes a buff away from it (`buff_end` hears `"removed"`). */
    fun removeBuff(self: LuaHandle.Centity, buff: String): Boolean

    /** Whether it has a buff now. */
    fun hasBuff(self: LuaHandle.Centity, buff: String): Boolean

    /** How many ticks a buff it has has left, or `nil` when they don't have it. */
    fun buffTicks(self: LuaHandle.Centity, buff: String): Long?

    /** Every buff it has now, with what a HUD shows them by, the first given first. */
    fun buffs(self: LuaHandle.Centity): List<ActiveBuff>

    /**
     * How much health it has left: from its `centity.json`'s `health` (its `max` when it spawns), or what
     * `set_max_health` gave it. `nil` for one without health, or once it's removed. Not saved: after a
     * restart it's whole again.
     */
    fun health(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Double?

    /** Its health when whole, or `nil` without health (or once it's removed). */
    fun maxHealth(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Double?

    /**
     * Sets how much health it has, between 0 and its `max_health`: no `damage` or `death` event, and 0
     * doesn't kill it (`damage` does). Its bar shows the change. `false` without health, or once it's
     * removed.
     */
    fun setHealth(self: LuaHandle.Centity, health: Double): Boolean

    /**
     * Changes its health when whole (more than 0), keeping what it has but no more than the new most. One
     * without `health` in its file gets health this way (full, and no bar). `false` once it's removed.
     */
    fun setMaxHealth(self: LuaHandle.Centity, maxHealth: Double): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes health from it: its `damage` handlers may change the amount or cancel it, its health goes down
     * by what's left, its bar shows, and at 0 it dies (`death`, then it's removed unless its file says
     * otherwise). Damage to one already dead does nothing.
     */
    fun damage(self: LuaHandle.Centity, amount: Double, options: CentityDamageOptions?): Double?

    /** Gives it back health, up to its `max_health`. Its bar shows the change. */
    fun heal(self: LuaHandle.Centity, amount: Double): Double?

    /**
     * Whether its health has reached 0 through `damage`. `false` for one without health. A dead one its
     * file keeps (`removeOnDeath: false`) stays dead until `set_health` gives it health again.
     */
    fun isDead(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it has a body: a `body` in its `centity.json`, or one `set_body` gave it. Without one the
     * body functions do nothing (their getters answer `false`, `nil` or `vec3.zero`). `false` once it's
     * removed.
     */
    fun hasBody(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Its body's settings as they are now: what its `centity.json`'s `body` says, every field filled in,
     * with what `set_body` changed. `nil` without a body, or once it's removed.
     */
    fun body(self: LuaHandle.Centity): BodySettings?

    /**
     * Changes its body's settings: only the fields given, the rest as they are. One without a body gets
     * one, the fields left out as `centity.json`'s defaults. A body's settings are runtime state, like a
     * node's pose: they last until its centity is reloaded or the server restarts, when it's back to its
     * file's. A value out of range is clamped as in the file; a width or height that isn't more than 0 is
     * an error.
     */
    fun setBody(self: LuaHandle.Centity, settings: BodySettings): Boolean

    /**
     * How fast its body is moving, in blocks per second along the world's axes: `vec3.zero` without a
     * body, `nil` once it's removed.
     */
    fun velocity(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Vec3?

    /**
     * Replaces its body's velocity: a knock back, a dash, a launch. The body carries on from it under
     * gravity and drag, and walking eases it back to its walk.
     */
    fun setVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Centity, velocity: Vec3): Boolean

    /** Adds to its body's velocity. */
    fun addVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Centity, velocity: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * The velocity its body is walking at (`set_walk_velocity`), or `nil` when it isn't walking, has no
     * body, or is removed.
     */
    fun walkVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Vec3?

    /**
     * Walks its body: every tick it speeds up or slows down toward this velocity by its `acceleration`
     * (`air_acceleration` while it's off the ground), so it reaches it smoothly and keeps to it, round
     * whatever pushes it about, until it's changed. Only the sideways part counts, unless it flies, when
     * it steers it in any direction. Leave it out (or `nil`) to stop walking: on the ground it then slows
     * to a stop by its `friction`. Walking doesn't turn it: `set_yaw` or `look_at` does.
     */
    fun setWalkVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Centity, velocity: Vec3?): Boolean

    /**
     * Sends its body up fast enough to rise `height` blocks, worked out from its gravity and drag: what a
     * jump over a block (1.2) needs. Whether it's on the ground is for the script to check
     * (`is_on_ground`), so a double jump is one more call.
     */
    fun jump(self: LuaHandle.Centity, height: Double): Boolean

    /** Whether its body is standing on something: a block, as of its last move. `false` without a body. */
    fun isOnGround(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /** Whether its body is in water or lava (the block at its middle). `false` without a body. */
    fun isInLiquid(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * The face of what stopped its body sideways on its last move, as the direction straight out of it
     * (`vec3(-1, 0, 0)` for a wall to its east), or `nil` when nothing did, or without a body. What a
     * walker turns round at, or jumps over.
     */
    fun wall(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Vec3?

    /**
     * Makes it a projectile: from now on the server flies the whole centity every tick at its velocity,
     * under its gravity and drag, and sweeps its path (a sphere of `radius`) for what it hits: centities'
     * hitboxes, players and entities (`hit`), and blocks (it ends, bounces or sticks: `flight_end`,
     * `bounce`, `stick`). It flies until it ends, then is removed unless told not to. Nothing about a
     * flight is saved. A launch over a flight replaces it. One with a `body` stops walking while it flies,
     * and walks again after. Every value is in blocks and seconds, as everywhere.
     */
    fun launch(self: LuaHandle.Centity, options: LaunchOptions): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it's flying as a projectile (`launch`), stuck in a block or lying where it came to rest
     * included: until its flight ends. `false` once it's removed.
     */
    fun isLaunched(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Ends its flight where it is, as if it had ended by itself: `flight_end` hears `"stopped"`, and it's
     * removed unless `remove_on_end` was `false`.
     */
    fun stopFlight(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes the whole centity walk somewhere, finding its way round the blocks in the way: to a place in
     * its own world, or after an entity or another centity, looking for a new path to wherever that is
     * every 10 ticks. When it stops, its `path_end` handlers hear whether it got there. A walk goes by the
     * centity's **feet**: the middle of the bottom of its hitboxes as they're placed when it sets off (its
     * position, for a centity without any). The way is found over the blocks' collision shapes, sized to
     * its hitboxes (other centities aren't in the way), and walked a little each tick, before physics and
     * scripts: it steps up and drops down as the options allow, falls when nothing holds it up, and finds
     * another way when a block appears across its path. With `fly = true` it flies instead, through the
     * air in any direction. A new `move_to`, `stop_pathing` and `teleport` end the walk it was on, and so
     * does a reload of its centity (its script's body can set off again). Walks aren't saved, and a
     * centity whose chunk unloads waits where it is until it loads again. Its physics bodies are carried
     * along with it.
     */
    fun moveTo(self: LuaHandle.Centity, target: LocationOrVec3OrEntityOrCentity, options: CentityPathOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Stops it where it is, ending its walk without a `path_end`. One that was falling stays in the air: a
     * centity only falls while it walks.
     */
    fun stopPathing(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /** Whether it's walking somewhere now (`move_to`). `false` once it's removed. */
    fun hasPath(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Where the path it's walking ends, as where its feet will be: where it was sent, or as close as it
     * can get when there's no way all the way there. A walk goes by the centity's **feet**: the middle of
     * the bottom of its hitboxes as they're placed when it sets off (its position, for a centity without
     * any). `nil` when it isn't walking, or once it's removed.
     */
    fun pathTarget(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Vec3?

    /**
     * This instance's own saved table, kept with the instance across restarts and removed with it. Every
     * script that holds the instance (its own, a module's) gets the same table. It may hold strings,
     * numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own
     * values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles,
     * which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles
     * do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with
     * a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). The table is
     * live: change it and the change is saved, at the next autosave (every five minutes), when the project
     * reloads and when the server stops. It stays in memory across hot reloads rather than being read
     * again, so a script that reloads finds it as it left it. A table that would take more than 1 MiB
     * isn't saved (the last save stands), and the log says so. `nil` once it's removed.
     */
    fun data(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Any?

    /** Takes it out of the world for good. Its `remove` handlers hear it, then its script unloads. */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.Centity)

    /**
     * Starts one of its animations, from the beginning (or `from_tick`), restarting it if it's already
     * playing. Its `animation_start` handlers hear it before this returns. An animation it doesn't have is
     * an error.
     */
    fun playAnimation(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String, options: AnimationOptions?): Boolean

    /** Stops one animation, or all of them. The nodes it was moving go back to rest. */
    fun stopAnimation(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String?)

    /**
     * Holds a playing animation where it is: its nodes keep the pose it had reached, until
     * `resume_animation`. An animation it doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun pauseAnimation(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String): Boolean

    /** Carries on with a paused animation from where it stopped. An animation it doesn't have is an error. */
    fun resumeAnimation(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether an animation is playing (a paused one still is, and so is a `hold` animation that reached
     * its end).
     */
    fun isAnimationPlaying(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String): Boolean

    /** The animations playing now, oldest first. */
    fun playingAnimations(self: LuaHandle.Centity): List<String>

    /** Every animation the centity has. */
    fun animations(self: LuaHandle.Centity): List<String>

    /**
     * How far into its clip a playing animation is, in ticks (20 a second): within the clip's length, so a
     * looping one starts again from 0 each time round. Not always whole, at a speed other than 1. `nil`
     * when it isn't playing; an animation the centity doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun animationTick(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String): Double?

    /**
     * Jumps a playing animation to a point in its clip, and poses the nodes there at once. Past the end, a
     * looping clip wraps round, and any other ends on its next tick. An animation the centity doesn't have
     * is an error.
     */
    fun seekAnimation(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String, tick: Double): Boolean

    /**
     * How fast a playing animation runs: 1 is as authored, 2 twice as fast. `nil` when it isn't playing;
     * an animation the centity doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun animationSpeed(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String): Double?

    /**
     * Changes how fast a playing animation runs, from where it is now. An animation the centity doesn't
     * have is an error.
     */
    fun setAnimationSpeed(self: LuaHandle.Centity, name: String, multiplier: Double): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether every node that draws something glows (`set_glowing`). `false` once it's removed, or when it
     * draws nothing.
     */
    fun isGlowing(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Boolean

    /**
     * Outlines every node that draws something, seen through walls, the way the Glowing effect outlines a
     * mob. The colour is `set_glow_color`'s. The same as `set_glowing` on each node.
     */
    fun setGlowing(self: LuaHandle.Centity, glowing: Boolean): Boolean

    /**
     * The colour its block and item displays glow in, as `#RRGGBB`: `nil` when they don't share one, when
     * none was set (they glow white), or once it's removed.
     */
    fun glowColor(self: LuaHandle.Centity): String?

    /**
     * Sets the colour every block and item display node glows in (the same as `set_glow_color` on each).
     * Minecraft draws no glow colour on text displays, so they're left as they are. It doesn't turn the
     * glow on: `set_glowing` does.
     */
    fun setGlowColor(self: LuaHandle.Centity, color: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * Hides the whole centity from one player: they don't see its nodes and can't click its hitboxes.
     * Everyone else still does. It stays hidden from them when they leave and come back, and when its
     * chunk unloads and loads again, until `show_to`, a server restart or the centity's removal. Hiding it
     * from someone offline counts for when they join.
     */
    fun hideFrom(self: LuaHandle.Centity, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Shows it to a player it was hidden from. */
    fun showTo(self: LuaHandle.Centity, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Whether `hide_from` hid it from a player. */
    fun isHiddenFrom(self: LuaHandle.Centity, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * How far away its displays are drawn, in blocks: 64 unless `set_view_range` changed it. `nil` once
     * it's removed.
     */
    fun viewRange(self: LuaHandle.Centity): Double?

    /**
     * How far away its displays are drawn, in blocks. Each player's own entity distance setting scales it
     * further, so it's the distance at 100%. Not saved, like everything a script sets on a node.
     */
    fun setViewRange(self: LuaHandle.Centity, blocks: Double): Boolean
}

/**
 * One node of one centity instance. Its transform (`translation`, `rotation`, `scale`) is in its
 * parent's space, in the same units as `centity.json`: blocks, degrees (X then Y then Z) and scale
 * factors. A root node's parent space is the centity's own: origin at the centity's position, turned
 * by its yaw. `world_position`, `to_world`, `to_local` and `world_direction` convert to and from the
 * world; physics (velocities, impulses and the point an impulse lands at) is always in world space.
 * What a script sets is the node's rest pose; an animation driving the same channel overrides it while
 * it plays. Nothing a script sets is saved: the script's body, which runs every time it starts, is
 * where to set it up. A node with `physics` is moved by it: each tick physics writes its translation
 * and rotation, after animations and before scripts, so a script's write has the last word (setting
 * the rotation re-seeds the body's orientation).
 */
interface NodeApi {
    /** The node's name. */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.Node): String

    /** The instance it belongs to. */
    fun centity(self: LuaHandle.Node): LuaHandle.Centity

    /** Its parent node, or `nil` for a root. */
    fun parent(self: LuaHandle.Node): LuaHandle.Node?

    /** Its child nodes, by name. */
    fun children(self: LuaHandle.Node): List<LuaHandle.Node>

    /**
     * Adds a node that hangs from this one, so it moves, turns and scales with it. A node a script adds is
     * **runtime state**, like a rest pose a script sets: it takes part in everything a declared node does
     * (its transform follows its parent, a display and an interaction entity are spawned for it and sent
     * to players, a hitbox takes clicks and `click` bubbles up through its parents, a `physics` body is
     * simulated and collides, `raycast` hits it) except what a file names: an animation clip can't drive
     * it, because clips are written against the file's nodes, though it follows its animated parent. It is
     * **not saved**: after a restart, and when its centity is reloaded, it's gone, and the script's body,
     * which runs again, is where to add it. Only nodes a script added can be removed; the nodes
     * `centity.json` declares can't be. The name is unique among the centity's nodes, not only this node's
     * children. A name that's taken, or isn't a usable node name, is an error, and so is a definition the
     * file's rules would refuse. Returns `nil` once its centity or this node is gone.
     */
    fun addChild(self: LuaHandle.Node, name: String, definition: LuaValue?): LuaHandle.Node?

    /**
     * Removes this node and every node below it, with their displays and hitboxes (players see them go at
     * the end of the tick), their physics bodies and every handler on them; the handles answer `nil` and
     * `false` after that. Only a node a script added can be removed: removing one `centity.json` declares
     * is an error, since animations, physics and other scripts are written against it. Returns whether it
     * was removed: `false` if it is already gone, or its centity is.
     */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Its offset from its parent (for a root node, from the centity's position), as shown now: animation
     * and physics included. `nil` once its centity is removed.
     */
    fun translation(self: LuaHandle.Node): Vec3?

    /**
     * Its rotation relative to its parent, in degrees, applied X then Y then Z. `nil` once its centity is
     * removed.
     */
    fun rotation(self: LuaHandle.Node): Vec3?

    /** Its scale on each of its own axes. `nil` once its centity is removed. */
    fun scale(self: LuaHandle.Node): Vec3?

    /** Moves it relative to its parent (a root node, relative to the centity's position). */
    fun setTranslation(self: LuaHandle.Node, translation: Vec3)

    /**
     * Turns it relative to its parent. Displays ease to the new pose over a tick, so setting it every tick
     * looks smooth.
     */
    fun setRotation(self: LuaHandle.Node, rotation: Vec3)

    /** Scales it along its own axes. */
    fun setScale(self: LuaHandle.Node, scale: Vec3)

    /**
     * Lays it from one point of the world to another: its origin halfway, its Z pointing from `from` to
     * `to`, and its Z scale the distance between them. For a node whose display is one block long along Z
     * and centred on its origin (a beam, a string, a link), that's the whole way from one to the other.
     * Its X and Y scale stay as they are, or are `thickness`.
     */
    fun stretchBetween(self: LuaHandle.Node, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, options: StretchOptions?): Boolean

    /** Whether it's a rope node (`"rope"` in `centity.json`). */
    fun isRope(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Strings a rope node between two ends, and keeps it strung each tick as they move, until
     * `release_rope` or another `rope_between`: its links laid along a line from one to the other, sagging
     * with `length`'s slack, as many shown as the distance needs (up to its `links`; the rest hidden). An
     * end is a point, a node (its origin, as shown), or an entity (half a block above its feet; a player's
     * at their hands' height, 1.1 blocks up). An end that's gone (a removed centity, an entity that left)
     * releases the rope. A node that isn't a rope is an error.
     */
    fun ropeBetween(self: LuaHandle.Node, from: Vec3OrNodeOrEntity, to: Vec3OrNodeOrEntity, options: RopeOptions?): Boolean

    /** Stops keeping a rope strung: its links stay where they were last laid. */
    fun releaseRope(self: LuaHandle.Node)

    /**
     * Eases its translation, rotation or scale from where they are to new values over some ticks, as
     * `set_translation`, `set_rotation` and `set_scale` would each tick: and with `back`, eases them back
     * again. A tween of a channel this node is already tweening replaces it, from where it is; `set_*` on
     * a channel doesn't stop its tween. Animations drive what they drive over it, as they do over `set_*`;
     * an `additive` one changes it.
     */
    fun tween(self: LuaHandle.Node, options: TweenOptions): Boolean

    /** Whether a tween is easing any of its channels. */
    fun isTweening(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /** Stops its tweens where they are now. */
    fun stopTween(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Where its origin is in the world, as shown now: animation and physics included. `nil` once its
     * centity is removed.
     */
    fun worldPosition(self: LuaHandle.Node): Vec3?

    /**
     * Its rotation relative to the world, in degrees applied X then Y then Z, as shown now: its own and
     * every ancestor's together. Scale doesn't count. `nil` once its centity is removed.
     */
    fun worldRotation(self: LuaHandle.Node): Vec3?

    /**
     * A world position in this node's own space: the space its children's translations are in, which
     * moves, turns and scales with it, as shown now. `nil` once its centity is removed, or while it's
     * scaled to nothing on some axis.
     */
    fun toLocal(self: LuaHandle.Node, point: Vec3): Vec3?

    /**
     * A position in this node's own space (where a child at that translation would be) as a world
     * position, as shown now. `nil` once its centity is removed.
     */
    fun toWorld(self: LuaHandle.Node, point: Vec3): Vec3?

    /**
     * A direction in this node's own space as a world direction: turned with the node, but not moved or
     * scaled. `node:world_direction(vec3.south)` is where its +Z points. `nil` once its centity is
     * removed.
     */
    fun worldDirection(self: LuaHandle.Node, direction: Vec3): Vec3?

    /**
     * Turns it so its +Z axis points at a world position, keeping its X axis level (no roll), by setting
     * its rotation as `set_rotation` does. Exact unless an ancestor is scaled unevenly. Pointing at its
     * own origin leaves it as it is.
     */
    fun lookAt(self: LuaHandle.Node, point: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Turns it further, by an angle about an axis in its parent's space, by the right-hand rule (as
     * `Vec3:rotated` does), by setting its rotation as `set_rotation` does.
     */
    fun rotate(self: LuaHandle.Node, axis: Vec3, degrees: Double): Boolean

    /** Whether it's set visible (it can still be hidden by a hidden parent). */
    fun isVisible(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /** Shows or hides it and everything under it. A hidden node can't be clicked. */
    fun setVisible(self: LuaHandle.Node, visible: Boolean)

    /**
     * Whether its hitbox takes clicks (`set_clickable`). `true` for a node without one, which has nothing
     * to click anyway.
     */
    fun isClickable(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Turns its hitbox off or back on without hiding the node: an unclickable node is still drawn, but
     * clicks pass through it as if it had no hitbox. Its children keep their own. Physics still treats the
     * hitbox as solid. Not saved.
     */
    fun setClickable(self: LuaHandle.Node, clickable: Boolean)

    /**
     * What it draws: `"block"`, `"item"`, `"model"` (a resource pack look) or `"text"`, or `nil` for a
     * node that draws nothing (a pivot, a hitbox alone) or once its centity is removed.
     */
    fun displayKind(self: LuaHandle.Node): String?

    /**
     * The block state a block display shows, like `"minecraft:oak_slab[type=top]"`, or `nil` when the node
     * isn't a block display.
     */
    fun displayBlock(self: LuaHandle.Node): String?

    /**
     * Changes the block a block display shows. A hitbox with `shape: "collision"` follows the new block's
     * shape.
     */
    fun setDisplayBlock(self: LuaHandle.Node, block: String): Boolean

    /** What a text display says, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when the node isn't a text display. */
    fun displayText(self: LuaHandle.Node): String?

    /** Changes what a text display says. */
    fun setDisplayText(self: LuaHandle.Node, text: String): Boolean

    /** The item an item display shows, or `nil` when the node isn't an item display. */
    fun displayItem(self: LuaHandle.Node): ItemData?

    /**
     * Changes the item an item display shows: any `Item`, so a name, `item_model` or `glint` shows as it
     * would in a hand. Its `count` makes no difference. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item
     * the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun setDisplayItem(self: LuaHandle.Node, item: ItemData): Boolean

    /**
     * The resource pack look a model display shows (`<pack>/<key>`, as the calling script names it), or
     * `nil` when the node isn't a model display.
     */
    fun model(self: LuaHandle.Node): String?

    /**
     * Changes the look a model display shows: another entry of a resource pack's `items`, a model with
     * elements or not. The display entity stays and only its item changes, which costs one small update to
     * each player who sees it, so swapping models every few ticks animates a node frame by frame (a
     * flipbook), alongside the transform tracks. A look the server's packages don't have is an error, and
     * so is a namespace no package on the server has.
     */
    fun setModel(self: LuaHandle.Node, model: String): Boolean

    /**
     * How a text display's text is drawn, every field filled in (`background` is `nil` for Minecraft's own
     * grey), or `nil` when the node isn't a text display. Change a field and hand it to
     * `set_display_text_style`.
     */
    fun displayTextStyle(self: LuaHandle.Node): TextStyle?

    /**
     * Changes how a text display's text is drawn. The whole style is replaced: a field left out goes back
     * to its default, so to change one thing, read `display_text_style()`, change it and write it back. A
     * bad value (an alignment that isn't one, a background that isn't `#AARRGGBB`) is an error.
     */
    fun setDisplayTextStyle(self: LuaHandle.Node, style: TextStyle): Boolean

    /** Whether its display glows (`set_glowing`). `false` for a node that draws nothing. */
    fun isGlowing(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Outlines its display, seen through walls, the way the Glowing effect outlines a mob. Block and item
     * displays glow in `set_glow_color`'s colour (white by default). Not saved.
     */
    fun setGlowing(self: LuaHandle.Node, glowing: Boolean): Boolean

    /**
     * The colour its block or item display glows in, as `#RRGGBB`, or `nil` for the default (white), for a
     * text display, or for a node that draws nothing.
     */
    fun glowColor(self: LuaHandle.Node): String?

    /**
     * Sets the colour a block or item display glows in. Minecraft draws no glow colour on a text display,
     * so there it does nothing and returns `false`. It doesn't turn the glow on: `set_glowing` does.
     */
    fun setGlowColor(self: LuaHandle.Node, color: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * The light its display is drawn with, when `set_brightness` fixed it; `nil` when it's lit by where it
     * stands (the default), or for a node that draws nothing.
     */
    fun brightness(self: LuaHandle.Node): Brightness?

    /**
     * Draws its display as if lit by fixed light levels, whatever the light where it stands: `{
     * block_light = 15, sky_light = 15 }` is full brightness in a dark cave. Leave it out (or `nil`) to go
     * back to the light around it. A level outside 0 to 15, or a missing one, is an error.
     */
    fun setBrightness(self: LuaHandle.Node, brightness: Brightness?): Boolean

    /**
     * Which way its display turns to face whoever looks at it: `"fixed"` (it doesn't), `"vertical"` (about
     * the vertical, like a sign on a post), `"horizontal"` or `"center"` (all the way). `nil` for a node
     * that draws nothing.
     */
    fun billboard(self: LuaHandle.Node): String?

    /**
     * Makes its display turn to face each viewer (or stop). A turning display ignores its rotation (its
     * parents' included) about the axes it turns on. A text display starts as its `billboard` in
     * `centity.json` says (`"center"` by default), a block or item display `"fixed"`. Anything else is an
     * error.
     */
    fun setBillboard(self: LuaHandle.Node, billboard: String): Boolean

    /**
     * How many ticks its display takes to ease to a new pose: 1 unless `set_interpolation_ticks` changed
     * it. `nil` once its centity is removed.
     */
    fun interpolationTicks(self: LuaHandle.Node): Long?

    /**
     * How many ticks its display takes to ease to each new pose (its translation, rotation and scale, and
     * its parents'). The client does the easing, so a pose set once with `set_interpolation_ticks(20)`
     * glides there over a second without the script doing anything more. When the whole centity moves
     * (`teleport`, physics carrying it along), its display glides there over as many ticks, up to 59. 0
     * jumps. Not saved.
     */
    fun setInterpolationTicks(self: LuaHandle.Node, ticks: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * How fast a physics body is moving, in blocks per second along the world's axes: `vec3.zero` for a
     * node without `physics`, `nil` once its centity is removed.
     */
    fun velocity(self: LuaHandle.Node): Vec3?

    /** Replaces a physics body's velocity, and wakes it. */
    fun setVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Node, velocity: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Adds to a physics body's velocity whatever it weighs ("go this much faster", which is what a jump
     * means), and wakes it.
     */
    fun addVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Node, velocity: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * How fast a physics body is turning about each world axis, in degrees per second: `vec3.zero` for a
     * node without `physics`, `nil` once its centity is removed.
     */
    fun angularVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Node): Vec3?

    /** Replaces a physics body's angular velocity, and wakes it. */
    fun setAngularVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Node, angularVelocity: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * A physics body's mass: what its `physics` says, or its collider's volume in blocks. 0 for a node
     * without `physics`. `speed * node:mass()` is the impulse that sends it off at `speed`.
     */
    fun mass(self: LuaHandle.Node): Double

    /**
     * Pushes a physics body through its centre of mass: mass times a change in velocity, so the same push
     * moves a light body further than a heavy one. Through the centre, so it doesn't turn it. Wakes it.
     */
    fun applyImpulse(self: LuaHandle.Node, impulse: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * The same push, landing at a point instead of through the middle, so it turns the body as well: the
     * further off centre, the more of it becomes angular velocity.
     */
    fun applyImpulseAt(self: LuaHandle.Node, impulse: Vec3, point: Vec3): Boolean

    /** Whether a physics body is resting on something: `not node:is_on_ground()` reliably means falling. */
    fun isOnGround(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether a physics body has settled and stopped being simulated. It wakes when something hits it,
     * when what holds it up goes (checked about once a second), or when a script pushes it.
     */
    fun isAsleep(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Puts a settled physics body back to work, for when a script changed something it can't feel (a block
     * placed beside it).
     */
    fun wake(self: LuaHandle.Node)

    /**
     * Pushes a physics body through its centre of mass for the next tick: a force in mass times blocks per
     * second squared, so calling it every tick is a steady push (and `vec3(0, 32, 0) * node:mass()` holds
     * a body up against default gravity). The same as `apply_impulse(force / 20)`. Wakes it.
     */
    fun applyForce(self: LuaHandle.Node, force: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether gravity is on for a physics body: `true` unless `set_gravity(false)` turned it off. How
     * strong it is is the body's `physics.gravity`, which may be 0. `false` for a node without `physics`.
     */
    fun hasGravity(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Turns gravity off for a physics body, so it floats where it's put and drifts at the speed it has, or
     * back on. Wakes it. Not saved.
     */
    fun setGravity(self: LuaHandle.Node, gravity: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Whether a physics body is kinematic (`set_kinematic`). */
    fun isKinematic(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes a physics body kinematic: moved only by scripts, never by gravity or what it hits. It keeps
     * moving at the velocity and angular velocity a script gives it (`set_velocity`), and its centity's
     * other bodies collide with it as if it were a wall: a moving platform, a piston. Back to a normal
     * body with `false`. Not saved.
     */
    fun setKinematic(self: LuaHandle.Node, kinematic: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Whether a physics body is simulated (`set_physics_enabled`). `false` for a node without `physics`. */
    fun isPhysicsEnabled(self: LuaHandle.Node): Boolean

    /**
     * Freezes a physics body in place, or lets it go again. A frozen body doesn't move at all (scripts can
     * still set its pose), and its centity's other bodies collide with it as with a wall. It keeps its
     * velocity, so it carries on where it left off when it's let go. Not saved.
     */
    fun setPhysicsEnabled(self: LuaHandle.Node, enabled: Boolean): Boolean
}

/**
 * Something in a world that isn't a block: an arrow, a minecart, a dropped item, a mob, a player. A
 * handle is its UUID, so keeping one is fine: once the entity has gone (killed, removed) or while its
 * chunk is unloaded, its methods answer `nil` or `false` rather than erroring. Its handle is the class
 * it is: a `Living` (health, effects, equipment), a `Mob` (a target, AI, walking, goals), a `Player`
 * or a `DroppedItem`, each with every `Entity` method too, and taken wherever an `Entity` is. A handle
 * to an entity that wasn't in the world when it was handed out becomes its class once it's back. The
 * entities NetherForge draws centities with are never handed out as `Entity`. Positions are world
 * space.
 */
interface EntityApi {
    /**
     * Its UUID, as a string: the thing to store. A player's gives the same handle back from
     * `nf.players.get(id)`.
     */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.Entity): String

    /**
     * What it is, like `"minecraft:zombie"` or `"minecraft:player"`, or `nil` once it has gone (or while
     * its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun kind(self: LuaHandle.Entity): String?

    /** Whether it's in the world now: alive, and in a loaded chunk. For a player, whether they're online. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /**
     * Where it is, in which world, and which way it faces, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk
     * is unloaded).
     */
    fun location(self: LuaHandle.Entity): LuaLocation?

    /** Where it is (its feet), or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun position(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Vec3?

    /** The world it's in, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun world(self: LuaHandle.Entity): LuaHandle.World?

    /**
     * Where its eyes are, which is where it looks from, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is
     * unloaded).
     */
    fun eyePosition(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Vec3?

    /**
     * Which way it faces horizontally, in degrees, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is
     * unloaded). Minecraft's convention: 0 faces south (+z), 90 west (−x), 180 north, −90 east.
     */
    fun yaw(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Double?

    /**
     * How far up or down it looks, in degrees, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded):
     * −90 is straight up, 90 straight down.
     */
    fun pitch(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Double?

    /** The unit direction it looks in, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun direction(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Vec3?

    /** Turns it to face a horizontal direction, keeping its pitch. */
    fun setYaw(self: LuaHandle.Entity, degrees: Double): Boolean

    /** Tilts it to look up or down, keeping its yaw. */
    fun setPitch(self: LuaHandle.Entity, degrees: Double): Boolean

    /** Turns it to look at a point: its yaw and pitch both. */
    fun lookAt(self: LuaHandle.Entity, point: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Moves it: to a `Location` (facing its way, when it has a facing), or to a position in its current
     * world. It keeps its own facing when the destination has none, and its passengers come along.
     */
    fun teleport(self: LuaHandle.Entity, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3): Boolean

    /** How fast it moves, in blocks per tick, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun velocity(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Vec3?

    /** Sets how fast it moves, in blocks per tick: a push. Friction and gravity carry on from there. */
    fun setVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Entity, velocity: Vec3): Boolean

    /** Adds to how fast it moves, in blocks per tick: a knock. */
    fun addVelocity(self: LuaHandle.Entity, velocity: Vec3): Boolean

    /** Whether it stands on something. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun isOnGround(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes it out of the world for good, without dying: no drops, no death event. Its handlers and its
     * saved table go with it. A player can't be removed: they leave through `kick`.
     */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /**
     * What players see it called, as MiniMessage: its custom name, or its kind's name (`"Zombie"`). `nil`
     * once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.Entity): String?

    /**
     * Its custom name, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when it has none, and once it has gone (or while its chunk
     * is unloaded).
     */
    fun customName(self: LuaHandle.Entity): String?

    /**
     * Names it, or takes its custom name away. Whether players see the name above it without looking at it
     * is `set_custom_name_visible`.
     */
    fun setCustomName(self: LuaHandle.Entity, text: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether its custom name shows above it all the time, not only when a player looks at it. `false`
     * once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun isCustomNameVisible(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Shows its custom name above it all the time, or only when a player looks at it. */
    fun setCustomNameVisible(self: LuaHandle.Entity, visible: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Its scoreboard tags (what `/tag` adds), sorted. Empty once it has gone. */
    fun tags(self: LuaHandle.Entity): List<String>

    /** Whether it has a scoreboard tag. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun hasTag(self: LuaHandle.Entity, tag: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Adds a scoreboard tag, as `/tag <entity> add` does. Tags are saved with the entity and selectors can
     * find them (`@e[tag=boss]`).
     */
    fun addTag(self: LuaHandle.Entity, tag: String): Boolean

    /** Takes a scoreboard tag away. */
    fun removeTag(self: LuaHandle.Entity, tag: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Its own table for scripts to keep things in, saved on the entity itself (its persistent data
     * container), so it goes where the entity goes and is gone when the entity is: the same table every
     * time, shared by every script. Change it in place; it's saved when the server autosaves, when the
     * entity's chunk unloads and when the server stops. It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of
     * them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s,
     * `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same
     * types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a
     * function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log
     * naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). `nil` while the entity isn't
     * in the world.
     */
    fun data(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Any?

    /**
     * Whether it glows: an outline seen through walls. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is
     * unloaded).
     */
    fun isGlowing(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Makes it glow, or stops it. The outline is white, or its team's colour (`team:set_color`). */
    fun setGlowing(self: LuaHandle.Entity, glowing: Boolean): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether players can see it at all (`set_visible`), whoever they are. `false` once it has gone (or
     * while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun isVisible(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /**
     * Hides it from every player, or shows it again. Saved with the entity. To hide it from some players
     * only, use `hide_from`.
     */
    fun setVisible(self: LuaHandle.Entity, visible: Boolean): Boolean

    /**
     * Hides it from one player: they stop seeing it (and can't click it), while everyone else still does.
     * It stays hidden from them when they leave and come back, and when the entity's chunk unloads and
     * loads again, until `show_to`.
     */
    fun hideFrom(self: LuaHandle.Entity, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Shows it to a player it was hidden from with `hide_from`. */
    fun showTo(self: LuaHandle.Entity, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Whether `hide_from` hid it from this player. */
    fun isHiddenFrom(self: LuaHandle.Entity, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * The project's team it's in (`team:add_member`), or `nil` when it's in none, or in a team the project
     * didn't make (one made with `/team` or by another plugin). For a player it answers while they're
     * offline too; for any other entity, `nil` once it has gone.
     */
    fun team(self: LuaHandle.Entity): LuaHandle.Team?

    /** Whether it makes no sounds. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun isSilent(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Silences it, or lets it make sounds again. */
    fun setSilent(self: LuaHandle.Entity, silent: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Whether it falls. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun hasGravity(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Lets it fall, or keeps it floating where it is. */
    fun setGravity(self: LuaHandle.Entity, gravity: Boolean): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether nothing but creative players and the void can hurt it. `false` once it has gone (or while
     * its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun isInvulnerable(self: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Makes it invulnerable, or not. */
    fun setInvulnerable(self: LuaHandle.Entity, invulnerable: Boolean): Boolean

    /** What rides on it, in order. Empty when nothing does, or once it has gone. */
    fun passengers(self: LuaHandle.Entity): List<LuaHandle.Entity>

    /**
     * Puts something on it to ride: a player on a horse, a zombie on a chicken. It can't ride itself, nor
     * something riding it.
     */
    fun addPassenger(self: LuaHandle.Entity, entity: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Takes a rider off it. */
    fun removePassenger(self: LuaHandle.Entity, entity: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /**
     * What it rides, or `nil` when it rides nothing, and once it has gone (or while its chunk is
     * unloaded).
     */
    fun vehicle(self: LuaHandle.Entity): LuaHandle.Entity?

    /**
     * What it carries, as an `Inventory`: a player's own inventory, a chest boat's or a donkey's chest, a
     * villager's. `nil` for an entity without one, or once it has gone.
     */
    fun inventory(self: LuaHandle.Entity): LuaHandle.Inventory?

    /**
     * The block it's looking at: a ray from its eyes along its direction, the first block whose shape it
     * hits (fluids don't stop it). `nil` when it looks at nothing within reach, or once it has gone.
     */
    fun targetBlock(self: LuaHandle.Entity, maxDistance: Double?): LuaHandle.Block?

    /**
     * The entity it's looking at: a ray from its eyes along its direction, the first entity it hits,
     * unless a block is in the way. Never itself. `nil` when there's none within reach, or once it has
     * gone.
     */
    fun targetEntity(self: LuaHandle.Entity, maxDistance: Double?): LuaHandle.Entity?

    /**
     * The centity it's looking at: a ray from its eyes along its direction, the first centity hitbox it
     * hits (tested with the same shapes clicks are), unless a block is in the way. `nil` when there's none
     * within reach, or once it has gone.
     */
    fun targetCentity(self: LuaHandle.Entity, maxDistance: Double?): LuaHandle.Centity?
}

/**
 * A living entity: a mob, an animal, an armour stand, a player. An `Entity` with health, attributes,
 * status effects and equipment.
 */
interface LivingApi {
    /**
     * How much health it has, from 0 to `max_health()` (2 is one heart), or `nil` once it has gone (or
     * while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun health(self: LuaHandle.Living): Double?

    /** Sets its health, up to `max_health()`. 0 kills it. */
    fun setHealth(self: LuaHandle.Living, health: Double): Boolean

    /** The most health it can have, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun maxHealth(self: LuaHandle.Living): Double?

    /**
     * Hurts it, as an attack would: armour and effects count, it flinches, and its `damage` handlers hear
     * it.
     */
    fun damage(self: LuaHandle.Living, amount: Double, source: LuaHandle.Entity?): Boolean

    /** Gives it health back, up to `max_health()`. */
    fun heal(self: LuaHandle.Living, amount: Double): Boolean

    /**
     * The value of one of its attributes now: its base with every modifier applied (its equipment's, its
     * effects', scripts'), within the attribute's range. `nil` for an attribute it doesn't have (`scale`
     * on a dropped item), or once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). An attribute the server
     * doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun attribute(self: LuaHandle.Living, attribute: String): Double?

    /**
     * One of its attributes' base value, before modifiers. `nil` for an attribute it doesn't have, or once
     * it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun attributeBase(self: LuaHandle.Living, attribute: String): Double?

    /**
     * Sets one of its attributes' base value. It's saved with the entity. An attribute the server doesn't
     * have is an error.
     */
    fun setAttributeBase(self: LuaHandle.Living, attribute: String, value: Double): Boolean

    /**
     * Everything changing one of its attributes: the modifiers scripts added, and those from its
     * equipment, effects and the game itself (sprinting), each with its full id. `nil` for an attribute it
     * doesn't have, or once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). An attribute the server doesn't
     * have is an error.
     */
    fun attributeModifiers(self: LuaHandle.Living, attribute: String): List<AttributeModifier>?

    /**
     * Adds a modifier to one of its attributes, replacing the project's modifier of the same id. Modifiers
     * are saved with the entity unless `saved` is `false`, so give each a fixed id and remove it when it's
     * done, rather than making new ones. The id is in the project's namespace (`netherforge.json`'s
     * `namespace`): `"slow_zone"` is `"<namespace>:slow_zone"`, so nothing else's modifiers can be
     * replaced or removed. An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun addAttributeModifier(self: LuaHandle.Living, attribute: String, id: String, amount: Double, operation: String?, saved: Boolean?): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes one of the project's modifiers off one of its attributes. An attribute the server doesn't have
     * is an error.
     */
    fun removeAttributeModifier(self: LuaHandle.Living, attribute: String, id: String): Boolean

    /** Its status effects, like speed or poison. Empty once it has gone. */
    fun effects(self: LuaHandle.Living): List<StatusEffect>

    /**
     * Whether it has a status effect. An effect the server doesn't have is an error. `false` once it has
     * gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun hasEffect(self: LuaHandle.Living, effect: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Gives it a status effect, replacing one of the same kind it has. An effect the server doesn't have
     * is an error.
     */
    fun addEffect(self: LuaHandle.Living, effect: String, ticks: Long, options: EffectOptions?): Boolean

    /** Takes a status effect away. An effect the server doesn't have is an error. */
    fun removeEffect(self: LuaHandle.Living, effect: String): Boolean

    /**
     * What it holds or wears in a slot, or `nil` for nothing, and once it has gone (or while its chunk is
     * unloaded).
     */
    fun equipment(self: LuaHandle.Living, slot: String): ItemData?

    /**
     * Puts an item in one of its slots, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item
     * the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun setEquipment(self: LuaHandle.Living, slot: String, item: ItemData?): Boolean
}

/**
 * A mob: a `Living` with AI, like a zombie, a cow or a bat. It has a target, walks where it's sent
 * (`move_to`), and its AI runs goals, the game's and any written in Lua.
 */
interface MobApi {
    /**
     * What it is after (to attack it, or follow it), or `nil` for nothing, and once it has gone (or while
     * its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun target(self: LuaHandle.Mob): LuaHandle.Entity?

    /** Sets what it is after, or calms it. */
    fun setTarget(self: LuaHandle.Mob, entity: LuaHandle.Entity?): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it thinks for itself: moves, looks around, attacks. `false` once it has gone (or while its
     * chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun hasAi(self: LuaHandle.Mob): Boolean

    /** Lets it think for itself, or freezes it in place (it can still be pushed and hurt). */
    fun setAi(self: LuaHandle.Mob, ai: Boolean): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes it walk somewhere, finding its way round what's in the way: to a place in its own world, or
     * after an entity, looking for a new path to wherever that entity is every 10 ticks. When it stops,
     * its `path_end` handlers hear whether it got there. A new `move_to` replaces the walk it was on. Its
     * own AI still runs, and can send it elsewhere (a zombie that sees a player goes for them), which ends
     * the walk as given up; with `set_ai(false)` it doesn't walk at all.
     */
    fun moveTo(self: LuaHandle.Mob, target: LocationOrVec3OrEntity, options: PathOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Stops it walking where it was going, whoever sent it: a `move_to`, or its own AI. A walk `move_to`
     * started ends without a `path_end`.
     */
    fun stopPathing(self: LuaHandle.Mob): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it is walking somewhere now, whoever sent it: a `move_to`, or its own AI. `false` once it
     * has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun hasPath(self: LuaHandle.Mob): Boolean

    /**
     * Where the path it is walking ends, whoever sent it: as close as it can get to where it was sent,
     * which is short of it when there's no way all the way there. `nil` when it isn't walking, or once it
     * has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun pathTarget(self: LuaHandle.Mob): Vec3?

    /**
     * Every goal its AI has, the game's and any added: its regular goals (walking about, attacking,
     * looking at players) and its targeting goals (what it picks a fight with), each with its key, its
     * priority and the controls it claims. In no particular order. `nil` once it has gone (or while its
     * chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun goals(self: LuaHandle.Mob): List<MobGoal>?

    /**
     * Takes goals off it: every one with the key, the game's or one a script added. Goals aren't saved, so
     * a mob that unloads and loads again has the game's goals back.
     */
    fun removeGoal(self: LuaHandle.Mob, key: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes every goal off it, its targeting goals too, but those `options.keep` names. A mob with no
     * goals stands where it is but for what moves it (`move_to`, being pushed); goals added afterwards run
     * as usual. Goals aren't saved, so a mob that unloads and loads again has the game's goals back.
     */
    fun clearGoals(self: LuaHandle.Mob, options: GoalClearOptions?): Boolean
}

/**
 * An item lying on the ground, which players pick up by walking over it: an `Entity` holding one
 * stack.
 */
interface DroppedItemApi {
    /** The stack it is, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun item(self: LuaHandle.DroppedItem): ItemData?

    /**
     * Changes the stack it is. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have,
     * is an error.
     */
    fun setItem(self: LuaHandle.DroppedItem, item: ItemData): Boolean

    /**
     * How many ticks until it can be picked up, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is
     * unloaded).
     */
    fun pickupDelay(self: LuaHandle.DroppedItem): Long?

    /** Sets how many ticks until it can be picked up. */
    fun setPickupDelay(self: LuaHandle.DroppedItem, ticks: Long): Boolean
}

/**
 * A figure that looks like a player, for shopkeepers, quest givers and statues: a `Living` with a
 * player's skin, a line of text under its name, and a pose, that isn't a player (it's on no player
 * list, counts towards nothing and loads no chunks) and costs no more than an armour stand. Spawn one
 * with `world:spawn_entity("minecraft:mannequin", …)`, and hear players click it with its `interact`
 * event. It stands where it's put unless pushed or hurt (`set_immovable` keeps it there), holds and
 * wears what it's given (`set_equipment`), and turns with `look_at`. For a figure that walks, fights
 * and plays as a player does, join a `Bot`.
 */
interface MannequinApi {
    /**
     * Its skin: a player's name (their skin, which the server looks up), a `Skin` table, or `nil` for the
     * default skin, and once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun skin(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin): StringOrSkin?

    /** Gives it a skin. Players' clients draw it as soon as the server has it. */
    fun setSkin(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin, skin: StringOrSkin?): Boolean

    /**
     * The line of text under its name, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when it shows none (a new one shows
     * "NPC"), and once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun description(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin): String?

    /** Sets the line of text under its name. */
    fun setDescription(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin, text: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether nothing pushes it: players, mobs, water, explosions. `false` once it has gone (or while its
     * chunk is unloaded).
     */
    fun isImmovable(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin): Boolean

    /** Keeps it where it is whatever pushes it, or lets it be pushed again. */
    fun setImmovable(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin, immovable: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Which hand it holds things in, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun mainHand(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin): String?

    /** Sets which hand it holds things in. */
    fun setMainHand(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin, side: String): Boolean

    /** How it stands, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). */
    fun pose(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin): String?

    /** Sets how it stands. */
    fun setPose(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin, pose: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Which parts of its skin's outer layer show, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is
     * unloaded).
     */
    fun skinParts(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin): SkinParts?

    /** Shows or hides parts of its skin's outer layer: a part left out of the table keeps how it is. */
    fun setSkinParts(self: LuaHandle.Mannequin, parts: SkinParts): Boolean
}

/**
 * Someone on the server: a `Living`, with everything living entities have, and more. A handle is a
 * UUID, so keeping one is fine: if they leave, its methods answer `nil` or `false` rather than
 * erroring, and work again when they come back. `nf.players.get` hands out handles to people who are
 * offline too. Their real inventory is numbered: the hotbar 0 to 8, the rest of the main inventory 9
 * to 35, armour 36 (feet), 37 (legs), 38 (chest) and 39 (head), the offhand 40, then body armour 41
 * and a saddle 42 (what the game keeps there for any entity).
 */
interface PlayerApi {
    /**
     * One of they's stats. A stat is a number by name (`"defense"`, `"move"`, `"damage_melee"`): the sum
     * of what every buff it has adds (its file's `stats`) and every modifier a script set
     * (`set_modifiers`), 0 when nothing gives it any. What a stat means is the scripts': the engine only
     * adds them up.
     */
    fun stat(self: LuaHandle.Player, name: String): Double

    /**
     * Every stat they has that isn't 0, by name. A stat is a number by name (`"defense"`, `"move"`,
     * `"damage_melee"`): the sum of what every buff it has adds (its file's `stats`) and every modifier a
     * script set (`set_modifiers`), 0 when nothing gives it any. What a stat means is the scripts': the
     * engine only adds them up.
     */
    fun stats(self: LuaHandle.Player): Map<String, Double>

    /** What a script's `source` adds to they's stats (`set_modifiers`), or `nil` when it adds nothing. */
    fun modifiers(self: LuaHandle.Player, source: String): Map<String, Double>?

    /**
     * Sets what `source` adds to they's stats, replacing what it added before: one call per source, each
     * time it changes (a piece of armour put on), and `nil` takes it all away. Buffs and modifiers are
     * runtime state: a restart clears them; a player's last while the server runs, offline too, and count
     * down only while they're online.
     */
    fun setModifiers(self: LuaHandle.Player, source: String, modifiers: Map<String, Double>?): Boolean

    /**
     * Gives they a buff (`buffs/<id>.json`) for a number of ticks: it takes away the buffs it `cures`, and
     * doesn't take hold while one it's `blockedBy` lasts. Given again while it lasts, its file's
     * `stacking` says what happens to the time. Its `buff_add` handlers may change the ticks or cancel it.
     * While it lasts its `stats` are added to they's; when it runs out, `buff_end` hears it. Buffs and
     * modifiers are runtime state: a restart clears them; a player's last while the server runs, offline
     * too, and count down only while they're online. A buff the project doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun addBuff(self: LuaHandle.Player, buff: String, ticks: Long): Boolean

    /** Takes a buff away from they (`buff_end` hears `"removed"`). */
    fun removeBuff(self: LuaHandle.Player, buff: String): Boolean

    /** Whether they has a buff now. */
    fun hasBuff(self: LuaHandle.Player, buff: String): Boolean

    /** How many ticks a buff they has has left, or `nil` when they don't have it. */
    fun buffTicks(self: LuaHandle.Player, buff: String): Long?

    /** Every buff they has now, with what a HUD shows them by, the first given first. */
    fun buffs(self: LuaHandle.Player): List<ActiveBuff>

    /** Their name, as they have it now (a player can change theirs between visits). */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.Player): String

    /**
     * What chat and other plugins call them, as MiniMessage: their name unless something changed it. `nil`
     * when they're offline.
     */
    fun displayName(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /** Changes what chat calls them, until they leave. Their real name, and what's above their head, stay. */
    fun setDisplayName(self: LuaHandle.Player, text: String): Boolean

    /** Sends them a chat line only they see. */
    fun sendMessage(self: LuaHandle.Player, text: String): Boolean

    /** Shows a line above their hotbar for a couple of seconds. */
    fun sendActionbar(self: LuaHandle.Player, text: String): Boolean

    /** Shows a big title in the middle of their screen, with a smaller line under it. */
    fun sendTitle(self: LuaHandle.Player, title: String, subtitle: String?, options: TitleOptions?): Boolean

    /** Takes a title off their screen. */
    fun clearTitle(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Plays a sound only they hear, where they are, following them. The same sounds `world:play_sound`
     * takes: an unknown one is an error.
     */
    fun playSound(self: LuaHandle.Player, sound: String, options: SoundOptions?): Boolean

    /** Stops a sound playing for them, or every sound when it's left out. An unknown sound is an error. */
    fun stopSound(self: LuaHandle.Player, sound: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * Spawns particles only they see, in their world: `world:spawn_particle` with `viewers = { player }`,
     * so `viewers` isn't an option here.
     */
    fun spawnParticle(self: LuaHandle.Player, particle: String, position: Vec3, options: ParticleOptions?): Boolean

    /** Runs a command as them, with their permissions. The leading slash is optional. */
    fun run(self: LuaHandle.Player, command: String): Boolean

    /** Whether they have a permission node. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun hasPermission(self: LuaHandle.Player, permission: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Grants them a permission node (`true`), or denies it (`false`), alongside whatever a permissions
     * plugin gives them. It's kept with the project's other grants and given back every time they join,
     * until `unset_permission`; it works while they're offline too. A package (every project is one) may
     * only grant nodes its own `netherforge.json` lists under `allow.permissions`, held to the package
     * whose code makes the call as `requires` is (each entry allows itself and everything under it:
     * `"shop"` allows `shop` and `shop.vip`): anything else is an error, and grants a later edit no longer
     * allows stop applying.
     */
    fun setPermission(self: LuaHandle.Player, permission: String, value: Boolean)

    /**
     * Takes back a node `set_permission` granted or denied: they have it again only if something else
     * gives it to them.
     */
    fun unsetPermission(self: LuaHandle.Player, permission: String): Boolean

    /**
     * The nodes the project has set for them with `set_permission`, each to `true` (granted) or `false`
     * (denied). Not every permission they have: `has_permission` answers that.
     */
    fun permissions(self: LuaHandle.Player): Map<String, Boolean>

    /** Whether they're a server operator, online or not. */
    fun isOperator(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Their game mode, or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun gameMode(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /** Changes their game mode. */
    fun setGameMode(self: LuaHandle.Player, gameMode: String): Boolean

    /** How full they are, 0 to 20 (20 is a full bar), or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun food(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /** Sets how full they are. */
    fun setFood(self: LuaHandle.Player, food: Long): Boolean

    /** How long until they get hungry again, 0 up to their food level, or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun saturation(self: LuaHandle.Player): Double?

    /** Sets their saturation, up to their food level. */
    fun setSaturation(self: LuaHandle.Player, saturation: Double): Boolean

    /** Their experience level, or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun level(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /** Sets their experience level. */
    fun setLevel(self: LuaHandle.Player, level: Long): Boolean

    /** How far the experience bar is towards the next level, 0 to 1, or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun experienceProgress(self: LuaHandle.Player): Double?

    /** Moves the experience bar, keeping their level. */
    fun setExperienceProgress(self: LuaHandle.Player, progress: Double): Boolean

    /** Gives them experience points, as orbs would: their level goes up when the bar fills. */
    fun giveExperience(self: LuaHandle.Player, points: Long): Boolean

    /** Whether they're flying. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun isFlying(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Starts or stops them flying. They have to be able to fly (`set_can_fly`) to start. */
    fun setFlying(self: LuaHandle.Player, flying: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Whether they may fly (double-tapping jump), as in creative. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun canFly(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Lets them fly, or not. */
    fun setCanFly(self: LuaHandle.Player, canFly: Boolean): Boolean

    /** How fast they walk, 0 to 1 (0.2 is normal), or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun walkSpeed(self: LuaHandle.Player): Double?

    /** Sets how fast they walk. */
    fun setWalkSpeed(self: LuaHandle.Player, speed: Double): Boolean

    /** How fast they fly, 0 to 1 (0.1 is normal), or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun flySpeed(self: LuaHandle.Player): Double?

    /** Sets how fast they fly. */
    fun setFlySpeed(self: LuaHandle.Player, speed: Double): Boolean

    /** Whether they're sneaking. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun isSneaking(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Whether they're sprinting. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun isSprinting(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * The language their game is set to, like `"en_us"`, or `nil` when they're offline. Right after they
     * join it can still be the default until their game says.
     */
    fun locale(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /**
     * How long a message takes to reach them and come back, in milliseconds, as the server estimates it;
     * `nil` when they're offline.
     */
    fun ping(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /** Disconnects them from the server. */
    fun kick(self: LuaHandle.Player, reason: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * When they first joined this server, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`), or
     * `nil` for someone who never has (a handle from `nf.players.whitelisted()`, say).
     */
    fun firstPlayed(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /**
     * When they were last on this server, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`): now
     * while they're online, `nil` for someone who never has been.
     */
    fun lastSeen(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /**
     * Whether they're banned from the server (by `ban`, `/ban` or anything else), and the ban hasn't run
     * out.
     */
    fun isBanned(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Bans them from the server: they can't join until it runs out or `unban`. Kicks them, with the
     * reason, when they're online. Banning someone already banned replaces their ban.
     */
    fun ban(self: LuaHandle.Player, options: BanOptions?)

    /** Lifts their ban. */
    fun unban(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether they're on the server's whitelist (which only keeps people out while it's on:
     * `nf.server.is_whitelist_enabled()`).
     */
    fun isWhitelisted(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Puts them on the server's whitelist, or takes them off. Taking someone online off doesn't kick them. */
    fun setWhitelisted(self: LuaHandle.Player, whitelisted: Boolean)

    /** Their ender chest, as an `Inventory`, or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun enderChest(self: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.Inventory?

    /**
     * Their own sky (`player:set_sky`), as the calling script names it, or `nil` for none: then they see
     * their world's (`world:sky()`).
     */
    fun sky(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /**
     * Shows them a sky of the project's, whatever world they're in, over their world's own
     * (`world:set_sky`), until it's cleared: as `world:set_sky` does for everyone in a world, and the same
     * errors. Nothing for someone offline, and it ends when they leave.
     */
    fun setSky(self: LuaHandle.Player, sky: String, options: SkyOptions?)

    /**
     * Takes their own sky away (`player:set_sky`): they see their world's again, fading over
     * `options.fade_ticks`.
     */
    fun clearSky(self: LuaHandle.Player, options: SkyOptions?)

    /**
     * A set of item slots of the project's own that they keep: extra equipment, a backpack, a bank. It's
     * saved in the player's own save data, with their inventory, in the same file and the same write, so a
     * crash or a restart can't duplicate an item between the two or lose one. Shown in a menu by binding
     * slots to it (`slot:bind`), where what players move in and out is saved at once. Dying doesn't drop
     * it. An `Inventory` of kind `"storage"`: its item methods work as on any other, and it answers `nil`
     * and `false` while they're offline (it can't be changed then). `nil` when they're offline. A name
     * that isn't an id is an error, and so is a size outside 1 to 256.
     */
    fun storage(self: LuaHandle.Player, name: String, size: Long): LuaHandle.Inventory?

    /**
     * Their HUD: the resource packs' HUD elements, filled in for them (bars, icons, text). A handle like
     * this one: fine to keep, and it answers `false` or `nil` while they're offline.
     */
    fun hud(self: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.Hud

    /** What's in their main hand (the selected hotbar slot), or `nil` for nothing or when they're offline. */
    fun heldItem(self: LuaHandle.Player): ItemData?

    /**
     * Puts an item in their main hand, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item
     * the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun setHeldItem(self: LuaHandle.Player, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /** What's in their offhand, or `nil` for nothing or when they're offline. */
    fun offHandItem(self: LuaHandle.Player): ItemData?

    /** Puts an item in their offhand, or empties it. */
    fun setOffHandItem(self: LuaHandle.Player, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /**
     * What they carry on their cursor with an inventory screen open (their own, a vanilla one or one of
     * the project's menus), or `nil` for nothing or when they're offline.
     */
    fun cursorItem(self: LuaHandle.Player): ItemData?

    /**
     * Puts an item on their cursor, replacing what they carry, or empties it: for `click_inventory` and
     * `drag_inventory` handlers, say, taking what they hold. What's on the cursor goes back to their
     * inventory (or drops) when they close the screen. The server can't tell whether their own inventory
     * is open, so with no screen open it's carried out of sight until they next open and close it.
     */
    fun setCursorItem(self: LuaHandle.Player, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /** Which hotbar slot they have selected, 0 to 8, or `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun heldSlot(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /** Selects a hotbar slot for them. */
    fun setHeldSlot(self: LuaHandle.Player, index: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * Puts an item into their inventory, topping up matching stacks first, and drops what doesn't fit at
     * their feet. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun giveItem(self: LuaHandle.Player, item: ItemData): Long?

    /**
     * Puts a recipe in their recipe book (with the toast that says so). `false` when they're offline,
     * already had it, or the server has no such recipe; a project recipe id the project doesn't have is an
     * error.
     */
    fun discoverRecipe(self: LuaHandle.Player, recipe: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes a recipe out of their recipe book. They can still craft it. `false` when they're offline or
     * didn't have it.
     */
    fun undiscoverRecipe(self: LuaHandle.Player, recipe: String): Boolean

    /** Whether a recipe is in their recipe book. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun hasDiscoveredRecipe(self: LuaHandle.Player, recipe: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Meets an advancement's criteria for them, as if they'd earned them: every criterion, or just
     * `criterion`. Once the advancement is complete they get the toast, the chat line and its rewards, as
     * when the game completes it.
     */
    fun grantAdvancement(self: LuaHandle.Player, advancement: String, criterion: String?): Boolean

    /** Takes an advancement back: every criterion of it, or just `criterion`. */
    fun revokeAdvancement(self: LuaHandle.Player, advancement: String, criterion: String?): Boolean

    /** Whether they've completed an advancement. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun hasAdvancement(self: LuaHandle.Player, advancement: String): Boolean

    /**
     * How far they are with an advancement: the criteria they've met and those left. `nil` when they're
     * offline.
     */
    fun advancementProgress(self: LuaHandle.Player, advancement: String): AdvancementProgress?

    /** How many ticks are left before they can use an item again, or 0. `nil` when they're offline. */
    fun itemCooldown(self: LuaHandle.Player, key: String): Long?

    /**
     * Makes them wait before using an item again, as an ender pearl does: every item of that kind, or
     * every item in that cooldown group. Items whose own `cooldown` names another group aren't affected by
     * their kind's.
     */
    fun setItemCooldown(self: LuaHandle.Player, key: String, ticks: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * Opens a menu for them: the shared window for a `shared` menu, otherwise a new window of their own,
     * filled from the file (or from the template). Its script's body has run (for a new window) and its
     * `open` handlers have heard it by the time this returns. A menu the project doesn't have is an error;
     * a template that's gone opens nothing (`nil`).
     */
    fun openMenu(self: LuaHandle.Player, menu: StringOrMenuTemplate, options: MenuOpenOptions?): LuaHandle.Menu?

    /**
     * The project menu window they're looking at, or `nil` (nothing open, or a chest that isn't one of
     * ours).
     */
    fun menu(self: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.Menu?

    /**
     * Sends their game every stack it shows again where it would now look different to them: their
     * inventory, the window they have open and their cursor, each worked out again by the `view` handlers
     * (and `nf.on("item_view")`). Stacks are sent by themselves when they change, but not when only what a
     * `view` handler works its look out from does, so call this then: when a shop menu opens or closes,
     * say. It's done once at the end of the tick however often it's called, and only stacks that look
     * different from what their game was last sent are sent.
     */
    fun refreshItems(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Closes the project menu they have open. Anything else on their screen (a chest, their own inventory)
     * stays open.
     */
    fun closeMenu(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Shows them a real inventory, as if they'd opened it: a chest's, another player's, their ender chest.
     * Whatever was on their screen closes. A storage (`player:storage`) is shown in a menu instead, by
     * binding slots to it (`slot:bind`): opening one is `false`.
     */
    fun openInventory(self: LuaHandle.Player, inventory: LuaHandle.Inventory): Boolean

    /** Closes whatever container screen they have open: a chest, a project menu, their own inventory. */
    fun closeInventory(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Puts a dialog on their screen: the same as `dialog:open_for(player, options)`. One of the project's
     * dialogs already on their screen is closed (its `close` handlers hear it). A dialog the project
     * doesn't have is an error; a `Dialog` from `nf.dialogs.create` that's gone opens nothing (`false`).
     */
    fun openDialog(self: LuaHandle.Player, dialog: StringOrDialog, options: DialogOpenOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes whatever dialog is on their screen away. When it's one of the project's, its `close` handlers
     * hear it.
     */
    fun closeDialog(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Their sidebar: the panel of lines at the right of their screen. Always the same handle for the same
     * player.
     */
    fun sidebar(self: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.Sidebar

    /**
     * A border of their own, which only they see: the first call gives them one, a copy of their world's,
     * and they see it instead of their world's until `reset_border()` or they leave (it follows them from
     * world to world). The server draws it for them but doesn't enforce it: it doesn't hurt them. `nil`
     * when they're offline.
     */
    fun border(self: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.WorldBorder?

    /**
     * Whether they see a border of their own (`player:border()`) rather than their world's. `false` when
     * they're offline.
     */
    fun hasOwnBorder(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes their own border away: they see their world's again, and the handle `border()` gave answers
     * `nil`.
     */
    fun resetBorder(self: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * The text above the player list (what tab shows), as MiniMessage, or `nil` when there's none or
     * they're offline.
     */
    fun tabHeader(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /** Sets the text above the player list. */
    fun setTabHeader(self: LuaHandle.Player, text: String): Boolean

    /** The text below the player list, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when there's none or they're offline. */
    fun tabFooter(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /** Sets the text below the player list. */
    fun setTabFooter(self: LuaHandle.Player, text: String): Boolean

    /**
     * What the player list (tab) calls them, as MiniMessage: their name unless `set_tab_name` changed it.
     * Their team's prefix and suffix go around it. `nil` when they're offline.
     */
    fun tabName(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /**
     * Changes what the player list calls them, until they leave. Their real name, what chat calls them and
     * what's over their head stay.
     */
    fun setTabName(self: LuaHandle.Player, text: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * Where they come in the player list: higher comes first, and equal ones are sorted as Minecraft sorts
     * them (by team, then name). 0 unless `set_tab_order` changed it; `nil` when they're offline.
     */
    fun tabOrder(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /** Moves them up or down the player list, until they leave. */
    fun setTabOrder(self: LuaHandle.Player, order: Long): Boolean

    /** Whether they're in another player's player list (`set_listed_for`). `false` when either is offline. */
    fun isListedFor(self: LuaHandle.Player, viewer: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes them out of another player's player list, or puts them back. Only the list's entry goes: the
     * viewer still sees them in the world and hears them in chat (to hide them altogether, `hide_from`).
     * It stays that way when either of them leaves and comes back, until it's set back.
     */
    fun setListedFor(self: LuaHandle.Player, viewer: LuaHandle.Player, listed: Boolean): Boolean

    /**
     * The line under their name tag (`set_below_name`), as MiniMessage, or `nil` when they have none (or
     * they're offline).
     */
    fun belowName(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /**
     * Puts a line of text under their name tag, which every player sees: a rank, their health, a level. It
     * goes when they leave. Minecraft draws this line for every player as soon as anyone has one, so while
     * someone does, players without one show an empty line. It's shown through the scoreboard's below-name
     * slot, which it takes over from anything else showing there.
     */
    fun setBelowName(self: LuaHandle.Player, text: String?): Boolean

    /** The project's boss bars they're among the viewers of (`bossbar:show_to`), offline or not. */
    fun bossbars(self: LuaHandle.Player): List<LuaHandle.BossBar>

    /**
     * What became of the project's resource pack on their game: `"loaded"`, `"declined"`, `"failed"`,
     * `"pending"` (accepted, still downloading), or `nil` before their game has answered (and when they're
     * offline, or there's no resource pack).
     */
    fun resourcePackStatus(self: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /**
     * Shows them a block that isn't there: only their game draws it, and the world doesn't change. It
     * lasts until the real block updates (something changes it, or their game reloads the chunk), or
     * `reset_block`. They can't walk through or stand on it any differently from the real block. A block
     * state the server doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun sendBlockChange(self: LuaHandle.Player, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3, state: String): Boolean

    /** Shows them the real block again, after `send_block_change`. */
    fun resetBlock(self: LuaHandle.Player, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Shows them another entity (or player) wearing or holding something it isn't: only their game draws
     * it. It lasts until that entity's equipment really changes, or it goes out of their sight and comes
     * back. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun sendEquipmentChange(self: LuaHandle.Player, entity: LuaHandle.Entity, slot: String, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /**
     * The entity they're watching through, as a spectator (`set_camera`), or `nil` when they're looking
     * through their own eyes (or offline).
     */
    fun camera(self: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.Entity?

    /**
     * Makes them watch through another entity's eyes, as a spectator does when they click one: they see
     * what it sees until they sneak, or this is called without one. They have to be in spectator mode
     * (`set_game_mode("spectator")`).
     */
    fun setCamera(self: LuaHandle.Player, entity: LuaHandle.Entity?): Boolean

    /**
     * Where their compasses point (those that aren't tied to a lodestone): the world's spawn unless
     * `set_compass_target` changed it. `nil` when they're offline.
     */
    fun compassTarget(self: LuaHandle.Player): LuaLocation?

    /** Points their compasses somewhere: a quest, a base, a meeting point. It lasts until they leave. */
    fun setCompassTarget(self: LuaHandle.Player, location: LuaLocation): Boolean

    /** Opens a book on their screen, as if they'd opened a written book, without giving them one. */
    fun openBook(self: LuaHandle.Player, pages: List<String>): Boolean

    /**
     * How many chunks around them the server is sending: the server's own view distance, or what
     * `set_view_distance` asked for, either of them lowered by their own game's setting. A change shows
     * here from the next tick. `nil` when they're offline.
     */
    fun viewDistance(self: LuaHandle.Player): Long?

    /**
     * Changes how many chunks around them the server sends, until they leave, from the next tick. Their
     * own game's setting still limits it: `view_distance()` says what they get.
     */
    fun setViewDistance(self: LuaHandle.Player, distance: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * Their saved table: one per player, by id, kept across restarts and shared by every script. By
     * convention each module keeps its own part of it under its name (`player:data().shop`), so modules
     * don't trip over each other. It works for someone offline too (`nf.players.get` finds anyone who has
     * joined). It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list
     * numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`,
     * `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something
     * gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a
     * `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was
     * (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). The table is live: change it and the change is
     * saved, at the next autosave (every five minutes), when the project reloads and when the server
     * stops. It stays in memory across hot reloads rather than being read again, so a script that reloads
     * finds it as it left it. A table that would take more than 1 MiB isn't saved (the last save stands),
     * and the log says so. It's also saved when they leave.
     */
    fun data(self: LuaHandle.Player): Any?

    /**
     * Their table for this time online: one per player, the same table every time, shared by every script,
     * and never saved. It's made empty the first time a script asks after they join, kept while they stay
     * (through a module's reload too: it's the player's, not the script's), and dropped once they've left,
     * after their `player_quit` handlers, so the next time they join it's empty again. A whole-project
     * reload (`netherforge.json` changing, or `/nf reload` with no files) empties it, as it does every
     * script's state. Anything a script holds goes in it (handles, functions, a `Task`), so a module
     * needn't keep a map keyed by player and clear it in `player_quit`. By convention each module keeps
     * its own part of it under its name, as with `data()`. `nil` for a player who isn't online.
     */
    fun session(self: LuaHandle.Player): Any?
}

/**
 * A fake player a script drives (see `nf.bots`): a `Player` with everything players have, and the
 * controls a player's client has. Every action is done as the client would do it, so what it causes is
 * heard like anyone's (`player_move`, `player_interact`, `entity_damage`, …) and a handler that
 * cancels it stops it. Actions that take ticks (`walk_to`, `break_block`) are asynchronous; a new one
 * ends the one before it with an error, as `stop()` does. Every bot online is a `Bot`, those the
 * editor's agents and tests drive included. Once it has left, every action answers `false` (and an
 * asynchronous one `nil, err`).
 */
interface BotApi {
    /**
     * Disconnects it, as a player quitting does: `player_quit` is heard, and it's gone for good (a bot
     * never comes back: join a new one).
     */
    fun leave(self: LuaHandle.Bot): Boolean

    /**
     * Walks in a straight line to a position's x and z, as a player holding forward would: a step a tick,
     * jumping up a block in its way, falling down drops and swimming. It doesn't find a way around a wall:
     * it stops, stuck, when it hasn't come closer for a second, and when it's still short after `timeout`
     * ticks. It takes as many ticks as the walk does, so its position arrives later: give `callback`,
     * which is called with `position, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without
     * one, and it waits and returns `position, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error.
     * When it fails, `position` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun walkTo(self: LuaHandle.Bot, position: Vec3, options: BotWalkOptions?): CompletionStage<Vec3>

    /**
     * Stops what it is doing over several ticks (walking, mining): that action ends with `nil` and an
     * error saying it was stopped. Nothing to stop is fine.
     */
    fun stop(self: LuaHandle.Bot): Boolean

    /**
     * Turns its head to look at a point, as its player would with the mouse: its next click aims there at
     * once. (An `Entity`'s `look_at` turns it from the server's side, which a bot's client hears a tick
     * later.)
     */
    fun lookAt(self: LuaHandle.Bot, point: Vec3): Boolean

    /** Jumps, once it stands on something. */
    fun jump(self: LuaHandle.Bot): Boolean

    /** Holds sneak, or lets go. */
    fun setSneaking(self: LuaHandle.Bot, sneaking: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Sprints, or stops: it sprints as it walks. */
    fun setSprinting(self: LuaHandle.Bot, sprinting: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Says something in chat, as its player typing it. A message starting with `/` is a command it types. */
    fun chat(self: LuaHandle.Bot, message: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Hits an entity or a centity's node (a left click), turning to its middle first, and swings its arm.
     * It must be within reach (3 blocks for an entity) and its client must have been sent it: an entity
     * hidden from the bot, or too far away to be sent, can't be clicked.
     */
    fun attack(self: LuaHandle.Bot, target: LuaHandle): Boolean

    /**
     * Right-clicks an entity or a centity's node, turning to its middle first (or to `options.at`). It
     * must be within reach (3 blocks for an entity) and its client must have been sent it: an entity
     * hidden from the bot, or too far away to be sent, can't be clicked.
     */
    fun interact(self: LuaHandle.Bot, target: LuaHandle, options: BotInteractOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Right-clicks with what it holds, at nothing: eats, draws a bow, throws a pearl. `release_item` lets
     * go.
     */
    fun useItem(self: LuaHandle.Bot, offHand: Boolean?): Boolean

    /** Lets go of right-click while using an item: a drawn bow shoots, a raised shield lowers. */
    fun releaseItem(self: LuaHandle.Bot): Boolean

    /** Swings its arm at nothing (a left click at the air). */
    fun swing(self: LuaHandle.Bot): Boolean

    /**
     * Right-clicks a face of a block, turning to it first: opens a door or a chest, places what it holds
     * against it. It must be within reach (4.5 blocks).
     */
    fun useBlock(self: LuaHandle.Bot, position: Vec3, face: String?, offHand: Boolean?): Boolean

    /**
     * Mines a block, turning to it first, for as long as that takes with what it holds, as a player
     * holding the button does. `nil, err` when there's no block there, it's out of reach (4.5 blocks), or
     * the server didn't let it break. It takes as many ticks as the block takes to break, so its broken
     * arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `broken, err` once it's done, or call it inside
     * a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `broken, err`. Without a callback outside a
     * task, it's an error. When it fails, `broken` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun breakBlock(self: LuaHandle.Bot, position: Vec3): CompletionStage<Boolean>

    /** Swaps what it holds in its hands (F). */
    fun swapHands(self: LuaHandle.Bot): Boolean

    /** Drops one of what it holds (Q), or the whole stack. */
    fun dropItem(self: LuaHandle.Bot, stack: Boolean?): Boolean

    /**
     * Clicks a slot of the menu it has open, or of its inventory with none: the menu's own slots first,
     * then its inventory's, as `slot` numbers them.
     */
    fun clickSlot(self: LuaHandle.Bot, slot: Long, click: String?): Boolean

    /** Presses Respawn on the death screen. `false` when it isn't dead. */
    fun respawn(self: LuaHandle.Bot): Boolean
}

/**
 * Whoever ran a command: a player or the console. What `event.sender` is in a command handler. For a
 * player, `event.player` is the same person as a `Player`.
 */
interface SenderApi {
    /** Their name: a player's name, or `"CONSOLE"`. */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.Sender): String

    /** Sends them a line: a chat line for a player, a log line for the console. */
    fun sendMessage(self: LuaHandle.Sender, text: String): Boolean

    /** Whether they have a permission node. The console has every one. */
    fun hasPermission(self: LuaHandle.Sender, permission: String): Boolean

    /** Whether a player ran it. */
    fun isPlayer(self: LuaHandle.Sender): Boolean

    /** Whether the console ran it. */
    fun isConsole(self: LuaHandle.Sender): Boolean
}

/**
 * A world on the server (the overworld, the nether, the end, or another the server loads). A handle is
 * its name, so keeping one is fine: if the world is unloaded its methods answer `nil` or `false`.
 * Positions are world space, in blocks.
 */
interface WorldApi {
    /** Its name, as `nf.worlds.get` takes it: `"world"`, `"world_nether"`. */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.World): String

    /** Whether the server has it loaded now. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.World): Boolean

    /**
     * What kind of world it is, or `nil` once it has gone. A world of a custom dimension type says
     * `"normal"`.
     */
    fun environment(self: LuaHandle.World): String?

    /**
     * The time of day in ticks, 0 to 23999: 0 is sunrise, 6000 noon, 12000 sunset, 18000 midnight. `nil`
     * once the world has gone. It's the game's clock, not the wall clock (`nf.server.unix_time()`).
     */
    fun timeOfDay(self: LuaHandle.World): Long?

    /**
     * Sets the time of day, keeping the day count. Taken modulo 24000. Doesn't stop the clock (that's the
     * daylight cycle game rule).
     */
    fun setTimeOfDay(self: LuaHandle.World, ticks: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it is night here: the time of day is 13000 to 22999, from dusk to just before sunrise, as
     * `/time set night` and a centity's `"night"` spawn time mean it. `false` once the world has gone.
     */
    fun isNight(self: LuaHandle.World): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it is day here: the time of day is 23000 to 12999, the rest of `is_night`'s. `false` once
     * the world has gone.
     */
    fun isDay(self: LuaHandle.World): Boolean

    /** How many whole days have gone by in this world, or `nil` once it has gone. */
    fun dayCount(self: LuaHandle.World): Long?

    /**
     * Tonight's moon, or `nil` once the world has gone. It moves on a phase each day: full, waning
     * gibbous, last quarter, waning crescent, new, waxing crescent, first quarter, waxing gibbous, then
     * full again.
     */
    fun moonPhase(self: LuaHandle.World): String?

    /** The weather now, or `nil` once the world has gone. Thunder always comes with rain. */
    fun weather(self: LuaHandle.World): String?

    /**
     * Changes the weather, for `options.ticks` or for as long as the game would have kept it. A `ticks`
     * below 1 is an error, as is a key `options` doesn't take.
     */
    fun setWeather(self: LuaHandle.World, weather: String, options: WeatherOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * The sky set over the world (`world:set_sky`), as the calling script names it, or `nil` for none (or
     * once the world has gone). A player's own (`player:set_sky`) is theirs alone.
     */
    fun sky(self: LuaHandle.World): String?

    /**
     * Sets a sky of the project's over the world (`skies/<id>.json`): everyone in it sees its colours in
     * place of the biomes' own, the sky, fog, clouds and water, fading over `options.fade_ticks`, until
     * it's cleared or another is set. Only what players are shown changes: the world, its biomes, its mobs
     * and its weather are as they were, and a restart clears it. A player's own sky (`player:set_sky`) is
     * shown them instead. A sky the project has no file for is an error, and so is one added since the
     * server started (its colours are biomes, which the game learns as it starts: restart the server).
     */
    fun setSky(self: LuaHandle.World, sky: String, options: SkyOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Takes the world's sky away (`world:set_sky`): its biomes' own colours come back over
     * `options.fade_ticks`. Nothing when it has none.
     */
    fun clearSky(self: LuaHandle.World, options: SkyOptions?)

    /** Where players appear in this world when nothing else decides, or `nil` once the world has gone. */
    fun spawnLocation(self: LuaHandle.World): LuaLocation?

    /** Moves the world's spawn. A `Location` in another world is an error. */
    fun setSpawnLocation(self: LuaHandle.World, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3): Boolean

    /** The lowest block y there can be, or `nil` once the world has gone. */
    fun minHeight(self: LuaHandle.World): Long?

    /** One above the highest block y there can be, or `nil` once the world has gone. */
    fun maxHeight(self: LuaHandle.World): Long?

    /**
     * The block at a position: a handle that reads the world live, so it sees later changes. The position
     * is floored to the block it's in. `nil` once the world has gone.
     */
    fun block(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3): LuaHandle.Block?

    /**
     * Every block in a box, read in one call: for code that needs many blocks at once (a mob's collision,
     * a flood fill of a room), where a `world:block` and a `:kind()` per block would cost each one. The
     * corners are floored to the blocks they're in and both are included, in any order. What each block is
     * given as is `options.what`. At most 32,768 blocks (32 × 32 × 32): a bigger box is an error. `nil`
     * when any of the box is in a chunk that isn't loaded, or the world has gone.
     */
    fun blocks(self: LuaHandle.World, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, options: BlockBoxOptions?): BlockBox?

    /**
     * Fills outward from a block through every block `options.through` names, one neighbour at a time, as
     * far as it reaches: the blocks of a tree (`through = { "#minecraft:logs" }`), the air of a room
     * (`through = { "passable" }`), a lake. It answers every block it filled (`positions`, nearest first
     * by steps) and every block it stopped at (`edges`: a room's walls), with what each is when asked
     * (`kinds`), and `stopped` says why it didn't finish when it didn't: it hit its `limit`, a chunk that
     * isn't loaded or the world's top or bottom (`"unloaded"`: a room open to the sky or the unknown), or
     * its `bounds`. It runs in the server, reading the blocks a box at a time, so a fill of thousands of
     * blocks costs a script one call. A start block it can't fill through answers no positions, and the
     * start as an edge. Blocks in chunks that aren't loaded can't be read or changed: that answers `nil`
     * or `false`, and never loads the chunk (`world:load_chunk` does).
     */
    fun flood(self: LuaHandle.World, start: Vec3, options: FloodOptions): FloodResult?

    /**
     * Changes the block at a position. A block state the server doesn't have (an unknown block, property
     * or value) is an error. Blocks in chunks that aren't loaded can't be read or changed: that answers
     * `nil` or `false`, and never loads the chunk (`world:load_chunk` does).
     */
    fun setBlock(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3, state: String, options: BlockSetOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Sets every block in the box between two corners (both included) to one state. One call changes at
     * most 32768 blocks: a bigger box is an error (`fill_gradually` spreads a big fill over several
     * ticks). A block state the server doesn't have is an error. Blocks in unloaded chunks are skipped.
     */
    fun fillBlocks(self: LuaHandle.World, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, state: String, options: BlockSetOptions?): Long

    /**
     * Sets every block in the box between two corners (both included) to one state, like `fill_blocks`,
     * but over as many ticks as it takes, for a box too big for one call: up to 16777216 blocks (256 × 256
     * × 256). It goes a few thousand blocks a tick, shared with every other gradual placement and fill on
     * the server (the owner's `structures.blocks-per-tick` in `config.yml`), so even a big one never
     * stalls the server; each package may have a few going at once (`structures.max-jobs-per-package`),
     * past which one fails at once. It goes a chunk's column at a time, from the bottom up, loading the
     * chunks the box covers as it reaches them (`fill_blocks` skips unloaded ones). A bigger box, a block
     * state the server doesn't have, or a `per_tick` below 1 is an error at once. The world going, or this
     * script stopping, stops it part way, and what was set stays. It takes as many ticks as the fill does,
     * so its changed arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `changed, err` once it's done,
     * or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `changed, err`. Without a
     * callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `changed` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun fillGradually(self: LuaHandle.World, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, state: String, options: GradualFillOptions?): CompletionStage<Long>

    /**
     * The highest block at a column that isn't air, ignoring the position's `y`. `nil` in an unloaded
     * chunk, or a world that has gone.
     */
    fun highestBlock(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3): LuaHandle.Block?

    /** Whether the chunk a position is in is loaded: whether its blocks can be read and changed now. */
    fun isChunkLoaded(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Loads the chunk a position is in, now, generating it if it's new: that can take a while, so do it
     * rarely. The server unloads it again when nobody is near, as it does any chunk.
     */
    fun loadChunk(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Finds the nearest place where the world's generator puts a biome, as `/locate biome` does: it asks
     * the generator, so it finds places in chunks nobody has been to, and never loads or generates a
     * chunk. It looks out from `options.near` in a square spiral, every 32 blocks across and 64 up and
     * down, so the place it gives is in the biome but may be up to 32 blocks past its nearest edge, and a
     * biome narrower than that can be missed. A biome the world's generator never places (a nether biome
     * in the overworld) is found nowhere at once. An id that isn't a biome on this server (a bare id that
     * isn't one of the project's is an error saying how to write the game's), or a `radius` out of range,
     * is an error at once. Finding nothing within the radius, or a world that has gone, fails. It works
     * off the main thread, so its position arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `position,
     * err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns
     * `position, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `position` is
     * `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun locateBiome(self: LuaHandle.World, id: String, options: BiomeSearchOptions?): CompletionStage<Vec3>

    /** Everyone in this world now. Empty once the world has gone. */
    fun players(self: LuaHandle.World): List<LuaHandle.Player>

    /**
     * The live centity instances in this world: the same as `nf.centities.all` with this world as the
     * filter's `world`.
     */
    fun centities(self: LuaHandle.World, filter: CentityFilter?): List<LuaHandle.Centity>

    /**
     * The entities in this world's loaded chunks, players included (as `Player` handles), or those the
     * filter picks. Never the entities centities are drawn with. Empty once the world has gone. A filter
     * key that isn't one of `EntityFilter`'s is an error.
     */
    fun entities(self: LuaHandle.World, filter: EntityFilter?): List<LuaHandle.Entity>

    /**
     * Spawns an entity, set up as `options` say before anything sees it (its `entity_spawn` handlers
     * included). A kind the server doesn't have, or one that can't be spawned (a player), is an error.
     */
    fun spawnEntity(self: LuaHandle.World, kind: String, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3, options: EntitySpawnOptions?): LuaHandle.Entity?

    /**
     * Drops an item stack at a position, as if a block broke there. An item table with a misspelled field,
     * or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun spawnItem(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3, item: ItemData): LuaHandle.DroppedItem?

    /**
     * Spawns particles at a position. The particle's id comes from the server (`"minecraft:flame"`), and
     * which options it takes depends on its kind: `color` and `size` for dust, `color` for coloured
     * particles, `block_state` for block particles, `item` for item particles. An unknown particle, an
     * option it doesn't take, or one it needs left out is an error. Particles that need data NetherForge
     * can't send (vibrations, trails) are an error too. Players see them within 32 blocks, or 128 with
     * `force`.
     */
    fun spawnParticle(self: LuaHandle.World, particle: String, position: Vec3, options: ParticleOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Plays a sound at a position, for everyone near enough to hear it. A Minecraft sound
     * (`"minecraft:block.note_block.pling"`, checked against the server's sounds) or a resource pack sound
     * (`"<pack>/<key>"`, like `"ui/click"`, your package's own, or another loaded package's as
     * `"<namespace>:<pack>/<key>"`: [looks are values](packages.md#looks-are-values)). An unknown sound is
     * an error.
     */
    fun playSound(self: LuaHandle.World, sound: String, position: Vec3, options: SoundOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes an explosion at a position, as TNT would (TNT's power is 4): it damages and knocks back
     * entities, and breaks blocks unless `break_blocks` is `false`.
     */
    fun explode(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3, power: Double, options: ExplosionOptions?): Boolean

    /** Strikes lightning at a position: it sets fire and hurts what it hits, unless `effect_only`. */
    fun strikeLightning(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3, options: LightningOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Casts a ray from a point and answers the first thing it hits within `max_distance`: a block's
     * collision shape, an entity's hitbox, or a centity's hitbox (tested with the same shapes clicks are).
     * `nil` when it hits nothing, and in a world that has gone. Blocks in unloaded chunks aren't hit.
     */
    fun raycast(self: LuaHandle.World, from: Vec3, direction: Vec3, maxDistance: Double, options: RaycastOptions?): RaycastHit?

    /**
     * Everything whose hitbox is inside or touches a box, nearest the box's middle first. It finds
     * centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden
     * node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A
     * centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or
     * `nil`) in a world that has gone.
     */
    fun overlapBox(self: LuaHandle.World, min: Vec3, max: Vec3, options: QueryOptions?): List<QueryHit>

    /**
     * Everything whose hitbox comes within `radius` of a point, nearest first: what an explosion or an
     * aura reaches. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and
     * physics see them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options`
     * allows: everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded
     * chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
     */
    fun overlapSphere(self: LuaHandle.World, center: Vec3, radius: Double, options: QueryOptions?): List<QueryHit>

    /**
     * Everything whose hitbox reaches into a cone from a point, nearest first: what a swing or a breath in
     * front of someone reaches. A hitbox counts when its point nearest the cone's axis is within `length`
     * of the tip and within `angle` of the axis, or the tip is inside it. It finds centities by their
     * hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden node has none),
     * players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A centity is
     * found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in
     * a world that has gone.
     */
    fun overlapCone(self: LuaHandle.World, origin: Vec3, direction: Vec3, length: Double, angle: Double, options: QueryOptions?): List<QueryHit>

    /**
     * Moves a sphere of `options.radius` (0, a point, by default) from one point to another and answers
     * everything it touches on the way, in the order it meets them: what a fast or piercing shot passes
     * through in a tick, which a ray (stopping at the first thing) can't say. Blocks stop it unless
     * `blocks = false`: the block it meets is then the last hit, with its `normal`, and nothing past it is
     * found. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see
     * them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows:
     * everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks
     * is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
     */
    fun sweep(self: LuaHandle.World, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, options: SweepOptions?): List<QueryHit>

    /**
     * The nearest thing whose hitbox comes within `radius` of a point, or `nil` when there's nothing: what
     * a homing shot or a mob looking for a target wants. With `line_of_sight = true`, only something a ray
     * from the point reaches without a block in the way. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown
     * node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities
     * by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest
     * node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
     */
    fun nearest(self: LuaHandle.World, position: Vec3, radius: Double, options: QueryOptions?): QueryHit?

    /**
     * A game rule's value: a `boolean` or an `integer`, depending on the rule. `nil` once the world has
     * gone. A rule the server doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun gameRule(self: LuaHandle.World, rule: String): Any?

    /**
     * Sets a game rule in this world. A rule the server doesn't have, or a value of the wrong type for it
     * (a `boolean` rule given a number), is an error.
     */
    fun setGameRule(self: LuaHandle.World, rule: String, value: LuaValue): Boolean

    /**
     * How many mobs of a spawn category the game keeps around each player here before it stops spawning
     * more (the mob cap, per player, scaled by how many chunks are loaded around them), or `nil` once the
     * world has gone. It's this world's own setting, or the server's (`bukkit.yml`) when the world has
     * none.
     */
    fun spawnLimit(self: LuaHandle.World, category: String): Long?

    /**
     * Sets this world's mob cap for a spawn category (see `world:spawn_limit`). `0` stops that category
     * spawning naturally. A negative number goes back to the server's own setting. Mobs already there
     * stay.
     */
    fun setSpawnLimit(self: LuaHandle.World, category: String, limit: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * How many ticks pass between the game's attempts to spawn mobs of a spawn category here, or `nil`
     * once the world has gone. It's this world's own setting, or the server's (`bukkit.yml`) when the
     * world has none.
     */
    fun spawnInterval(self: LuaHandle.World, category: String): Long?

    /**
     * Sets how many ticks pass between spawn attempts for a spawn category in this world (see
     * `world:spawn_interval`): lower is faster. `0` stops that category spawning naturally. A negative
     * number goes back to the server's own setting.
     */
    fun setSpawnInterval(self: LuaHandle.World, category: String, ticks: Long): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether this project may unload it (`world:unload`): it's one the project's scripts created
     * (`nf.worlds.create`, `nf.worlds.copy`) or named in `netherforge.json`'s `managedWorlds`, and it
     * isn't the server's main world. Any script can find, load and use any world; only managed ones can be
     * taken away.
     */
    fun isManaged(self: LuaHandle.World): Boolean

    /**
     * Unloads the world: players in it are moved out first (to the spawn of `options.move_players_to`,
     * else of the main world), then the server saves it (unless `save` is `false`) and lets it go. With
     * `delete`, its files are deleted too, off the main thread, and the project forgets it. Only a world
     * this project manages can be unloaded (see `world:is_managed`): any other, the server's main world
     * included, is an error, as is a `move_players_to` in this same world. A world it created stays the
     * project's after a restart; find it again with `nf.worlds.load`.
     */
    fun unload(self: LuaHandle.World, options: WorldUnloadOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * The world's border: the wall players can't pass, shown to everyone in the world (except a player
     * given a border of their own with `player:border()`). `nil` once the world has gone.
     */
    fun border(self: LuaHandle.World): LuaHandle.WorldBorder?

    /**
     * Saves the box between two corners (both included) as a structure named `id`, to place later with
     * `place_structure`: an arena to reset, a prefab to stamp. It's saved in the server's data folder, not
     * the project, and kept across restarts; saving the same id again replaces it. An id that isn't an id
     * (lowercase letters, digits and `_`), or that one of the project's structures (`structures/<id>.nbt`)
     * or builds (`builds/<id>/`) already has, is an error, as is a box over 48 blocks along any side. Air
     * is saved too, so placing it puts back the empty space as well; it loads the chunks the box covers,
     * as `/place` does.
     */
    fun saveStructure(self: LuaHandle.World, id: String, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, options: StructureSaveOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Places a structure with its smallest corner at `position` (before it's turned): one of the project's
     * (`structures/<id>.nbt`) or one a script saved (`save_structure`). Loads the chunks it covers, as
     * `/place` does, all in this tick: `place_gradually` spreads a big one over several. A build
     * (`builds/<id>/`) is always placed that way, so naming one here is an error, as is a structure
     * there's none of, one whose file can't be read, a `rotation` that isn't a multiple of 90 or an
     * `integrity` outside 0 to 1.
     */
    fun placeStructure(self: LuaHandle.World, id: String, position: Vec3, options: StructurePlaceOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Places a structure or a build (`builds/<id>/`, a structure too big for one file) like
     * `place_structure`, its smallest corner at `position` before it's turned, but over as many ticks as
     * it takes. It goes a few thousand blocks a tick, shared with every other gradual placement and fill
     * on the server (the owner's `structures.blocks-per-tick` in `config.yml`), so even a big one never
     * stalls the server; each package may have a few going at once (`structures.max-jobs-per-package`),
     * past which one fails at once. Its blocks go in from the bottom up (a build's a part at a time),
     * loading the chunks it covers as it reaches them, then its entities, once every block is in. Blocks
     * go in as they were saved, with no updates to their neighbours, as `/place` puts them. A structure or
     * build there's none of, a `rotation` that isn't a multiple of 90, an `integrity` outside 0 to 1 or a
     * `per_tick` below 1 is an error at once. A part that can't be read, the world going, or this script
     * stopping stops it part way, and what was placed stays. It takes as many ticks as the placing does,
     * so its placed arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `placed, err` once it's done, or
     * call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `placed, err`. Without a
     * callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `placed` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun placeGradually(self: LuaHandle.World, id: String, position: Vec3, options: GradualPlaceOptions?): CompletionStage<Long>
}

/**
 * A border: the wall at the edge of a square area, which players can't walk through and which hurts
 * them outside it. Either a world's (`world:border()`), which everyone in the world sees, or one
 * player's own (`player:border()`), which only they see and which the server draws for them but
 * doesn't enforce: it doesn't hurt them, and doesn't stop what isn't them. Sizes and positions are
 * blocks; times are ticks (20 a second). Its methods answer `nil` or `false` once its world has gone,
 * or for a player's own, once they've left or gone back to their world's (`player:reset_border()`).
 */
interface WorldBorderApi {
    /** Its centre, with `y` 0, or `nil` once it has gone. */
    fun center(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder): Vec3?

    /** Moves its centre. `y` is ignored. More than 29,999,984 blocks out is an error. */
    fun setCenter(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder, center: Vec3): Boolean

    /**
     * How wide it is, side to side, in blocks: while it's growing or shrinking, how wide it is now. `nil`
     * once it has gone.
     */
    fun size(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder): Double?

    /**
     * Changes how wide it is: at once, or moving steadily there over `options.ticks`. A size below 1 or
     * over 59,999,968, or negative `ticks`, is an error.
     */
    fun setSize(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder, size: Double, options: BorderSizeOptions?): Boolean

    /** How it hurts players outside it, or `nil` once it has gone. */
    fun damage(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder): BorderDamage?

    /**
     * Changes how it hurts players outside it; what `damage` leaves out stays as it was. A negative value
     * is an error. A player's own border is only drawn, so this changes nothing for them.
     */
    fun setDamage(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder, damage: BorderDamage): Boolean

    /** When players' screens turn red as it comes near, or `nil` once it has gone. */
    fun warning(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder): BorderWarning?

    /**
     * Changes when players' screens turn red; what `warning` leaves out stays as it was. A negative value
     * is an error.
     */
    fun setWarning(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder, warning: BorderWarning): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether a place is inside it. A `Location` in another world is outside a world's border; a player's
     * own border has no world, so only the position counts. `false` once it has gone.
     */
    fun contains(self: LuaHandle.WorldBorder, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3): Boolean
}

/**
 * One block position in one world. The handle is the position, not what's there: it reads the world
 * live, so a `Block` kept from earlier sees later changes. Blocks in chunks that aren't loaded can't
 * be read or changed: that answers `nil` or `false`, and never loads the chunk (`world:load_chunk`
 * does).
 */
interface BlockApi {
    /** The world it's in. */
    fun world(self: LuaHandle.Block): LuaHandle.World

    /** Its corner with the smallest x, y and z: whole numbers, in the world. */
    fun position(self: LuaHandle.Block): Vec3

    /** Its corner as a `Location`, with no facing. */
    fun location(self: LuaHandle.Block): LuaLocation

    /** What block it is, like `"minecraft:stone"`, or `nil` in an unloaded chunk. */
    fun kind(self: LuaHandle.Block): String?

    /**
     * Its whole block state, like
     * `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,shape=straight,waterlogged=false]"` (properties
     * sorted), or `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
     */
    fun state(self: LuaHandle.Block): String?

    /**
     * One property's value, like `"east"` for `facing`, or `nil` when the block has no such property or
     * its chunk isn't loaded.
     */
    fun property(self: LuaHandle.Block, name: String): String?

    /** Every property and its value; empty for a block without any, and `nil` in an unloaded chunk. */
    fun properties(self: LuaHandle.Block): Map<String, String>?

    /** Whether it's air (any kind). `false` in an unloaded chunk. */
    fun isAir(self: LuaHandle.Block): Boolean

    /** Whether it's solid: something players stand on and can't walk through. `false` in an unloaded chunk. */
    fun isSolid(self: LuaHandle.Block): Boolean

    /** Whether it's water or lava. `false` in an unloaded chunk. */
    fun isLiquid(self: LuaHandle.Block): Boolean

    /** Changes it, as `world:set_block` does. A block state the server doesn't have is an error. */
    fun setState(self: LuaHandle.Block, state: String, options: BlockSetOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Changes one property, keeping the rest of its state. A property the block doesn't have, or a value
     * it can't take, is an error.
     */
    fun setProperty(self: LuaHandle.Block, name: String, value: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Breaks it as a player would: it drops what it would drop (for that tool, when given) and turns to
     * air.
     */
    fun breakNaturally(self: LuaHandle.Block, tool: ItemData?): Boolean

    /** The block at an offset from this one. */
    fun relative(self: LuaHandle.Block, offset: Vec3OrChoice): LuaHandle.Block

    /**
     * What a container block holds (a chest, a barrel, a hopper, a furnace, a shulker box), as an
     * `Inventory`: the same one players see. A double chest's is both halves. `nil` for a block that isn't
     * a container, and in an unloaded chunk.
     */
    fun inventory(self: LuaHandle.Block): LuaHandle.Inventory?

    /**
     * The project block that's here, as a `CustomBlock` (the same handle: one position, one block), or
     * `nil` when what's here isn't one of the project's blocks, or its chunk isn't loaded.
     */
    fun custom(self: LuaHandle.Block): LuaHandle.CustomBlock?

    /**
     * How bright it is there, 0 to 15: the brighter of block light and sky light. `nil` in an unloaded
     * chunk.
     */
    fun lightLevel(self: LuaHandle.Block): Long?

    /** How much sky light reaches it, 0 to 15, whatever the time of day. `nil` in an unloaded chunk. */
    fun skyLightLevel(self: LuaHandle.Block): Long?

    /**
     * The biome here, named as `locate_biome` takes one: a biome of the project reads as its id
     * (`"ruby_grove"`), a package's as `"acme:grove"` (to a package's own scripts, its own is bare), the
     * game's as `"minecraft:plains"`. The game keeps biomes per 4×4×4 blocks, so neighbouring blocks share
     * one. `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
     */
    fun biome(self: LuaHandle.Block): String?

    /**
     * This position's own table for scripts to keep things in, saved with the chunk: the same table every
     * time, shared by every script. Change it in place; it's saved when the server autosaves, when the
     * chunk unloads and when the server stops. It stays with the position, whatever happens to the block.
     * It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from
     * 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity`
     * and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or
     * `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped
     * when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be
     * saved`). `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
     */
    fun data(self: LuaHandle.Block): Any?
}

/**
 * A block of the project's own at a position, which is a `Block` like any other: it reads the world
 * live, so one kept from earlier turns into whatever is there now (`block:custom()` says whether it
 * still is one). The game holds it as a state of its carrier (a note block's, tripwire's or a leaves
 * block's) its resource pack draws as the block, so `kind()` and `state()` answer that; `id()` and
 * `project()` say which block it is. `data()` is its own table: it starts empty when the block is
 * placed and is gone when the block is, whatever replaced it. `break_naturally` rolls its loot table;
 * replacing or filling over it with `set_state` or `world:fill_blocks` removes it without drops.
 */
interface CustomBlockApi {
    /**
     * Its id: its project block's folder name under `blocks/`, or `namespace:id` for one a package it
     * depends on exports, as the calling script names it. `nil` once what's here isn't a block of the
     * project's.
     */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.CustomBlock): String?

    /** The project block it's one of, or `nil` once what's here isn't one. */
    fun project(self: LuaHandle.CustomBlock): LuaHandle.ProjectBlock?

    /**
     * The way its front faces, for a block that turns (`rotation` in its `block.json`): what it was placed
     * facing, or turned to since. `nil` for a block that doesn't turn, or once what's here isn't one.
     */
    fun facing(self: LuaHandle.CustomBlock): String?

    /**
     * Turns it to face another way, keeping everything else about it: its table, its centity (turned with
     * it). `false` when it doesn't turn that way (it doesn't turn, or turns only `horizontal` and this is
     * up or down), or once what's here isn't one of the project's blocks.
     */
    fun setFacing(self: LuaHandle.CustomBlock, facing: String): Boolean
}

/**
 * A project block from `blocks/<id>/block.json`: `this` in the block's script, and what
 * `nf.blocks.get` hands out. It's the kind of block, not one placed in the world: its handlers hear
 * what players do with any block of it (see `CustomBlock` for one placed). If the block is deleted,
 * its methods answer `nil` or `false` and its handlers go.
 */
interface ProjectBlockApi {
    /** Its id: its folder name under `blocks/`. */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.ProjectBlock): String

    /**
     * Its `properties` from its `block.json`: the project's own facts about it, any JSON (`{ damage = 12,
     * class = "melee" }`), as a table. A new table each call, so keep it rather than ask in a loop. An
     * empty table when the file has none, `nil` once the block is gone.
     */
    fun properties(self: LuaHandle.ProjectBlock): Any?

    /** Whether the project still has it. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.ProjectBlock): Boolean

    /**
     * Places one at the block position a location is in, as `world:set_block` would: whatever is there is
     * replaced, and nothing drops. Its `place` handlers are not asked (a script placing a block isn't a
     * player placing one), and it isn't a `block_place` either. If the block has a centity, it's spawned
     * over the block. `nil` when the chunk isn't loaded, or when the game has no state of its carrier left
     * to hold the block in (the project has more blocks of that carrier than that).
     */
    fun place(self: LuaHandle.ProjectBlock, location: LuaLocation, options: BlockPlaceOptions?): LuaHandle.CustomBlock?
}

/**
 * A real inventory: a player's (`player:inventory()`), their ender chest, a storage of the project's
 * that they keep (`player:storage(name, size)`), a container block's (`block:inventory()`: a chest, a
 * barrel, a hopper, a furnace) or an entity's (a chest boat, a donkey, a villager). Its item methods
 * work like `Menu`'s. The handle is where the inventory is, so it reads live; once that's gone (a
 * chest broken, a player offline, a chunk unloaded) its methods answer `nil`, `false` or nothing.
 * Slots are numbered from 0. A player's: the hotbar 0 to 8, the rest of the main inventory 9 to 35,
 * armour 36 (feet), 37 (legs), 38 (chest) and 39 (head), the offhand 40, then body armour 41 and a
 * saddle 42 (what the game keeps there for any entity). A double chest's inventory is both halves, 0
 * to 53.
 */
interface InventoryApi {
    /**
     * Whether it's there now: the player online, the container still a container, the entity in the world
     * and its chunk loaded.
     */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Inventory): Boolean

    /**
     * What kind of inventory it is: `"player"`, `"ender_chest"`, `"storage"`, `"chest"`, `"barrel"`,
     * `"shulker_box"`, `"hopper"`, `"dispenser"`, `"dropper"`, `"furnace"`, `"brewing"`, … `nil` once it's
     * gone.
     */
    fun kind(self: LuaHandle.Inventory): String?

    /** How many slots it has: 43 for a player, 27 for a chest, 54 for a double chest. 0 once it's gone. */
    fun size(self: LuaHandle.Inventory): Long

    /**
     * Whose it is: the `Player` (for a player's inventory, ender chest and storage), the `Entity`, or the
     * `Block`. `nil` once it's gone.
     */
    fun holder(self: LuaHandle.Inventory): LuaHandle?

    /** What is in a slot, or `nil` for an empty one (or once it's gone). A slot outside it is an error. */
    fun item(self: LuaHandle.Inventory, index: Long): ItemData?

    /**
     * Puts an item in a slot, replacing what was there, or empties it. A slot outside it, an item table
     * with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun setItem(self: LuaHandle.Inventory, index: Long, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /** Every item in it, keyed by slot. Empty slots are left out, so `pairs` visits only what is there. */
    fun items(self: LuaHandle.Inventory): Map<Long, ItemData>

    /**
     * Puts an item wherever it fits, topping up matching stacks first, the way picking something up does.
     * In a player's inventory only the hotbar and main inventory take it, never armour or the offhand.
     */
    fun addItem(self: LuaHandle.Inventory, item: ItemData): Long

    /** Takes away items that match, from the first slot on, until `count` are gone or none are left. */
    fun removeItem(self: LuaHandle.Inventory, match: StringOrItemMatch, count: Long?): Long

    /** How many items match, across every slot. */
    fun countItem(self: LuaHandle.Inventory, match: StringOrItemMatch): Long

    /** Whether at least `count` items match. */
    fun hasItem(self: LuaHandle.Inventory, match: StringOrItemMatch, count: Long?): Boolean

    /** The first slot holding an item that matches, or `nil` when none does. */
    fun firstSlot(self: LuaHandle.Inventory, match: StringOrItemMatch): Long?

    /** Empties every slot. To empty one, `set_item(index, nil)`. */
    fun clear(self: LuaHandle.Inventory): Boolean

    /**
     * The players who have it open on their screen now: for a storage, those looking at a window with a
     * slot bound to it. (A player's own inventory, open in their own inventory screen, doesn't count: the
     * server can't tell.)
     */
    fun viewers(self: LuaHandle.Inventory): List<LuaHandle.Player>
}

/**
 * A bar across the top of players' screens, made by `nf.bossbars.create`. It shows to the players it's
 * shown to (`show_to`), and stays theirs when they leave and come back. It belongs to the script that
 * made it and goes when that script unloads (or `remove()`): from then on its methods answer `nil` or
 * `false`.
 */
interface BossBarApi {
    /** Whether it's still there: `false` once it's removed or its script has unloaded. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): Boolean

    /** Shows it to a player, now if they're online and again whenever they join. */
    fun showTo(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Stops showing it to a player. */
    fun hideFrom(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Everyone it's shown to, online or not: by `show_to`, and for an attached bar, whoever is near its
     * centity now. Empty once it's gone.
     */
    fun viewers(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): List<LuaHandle.Player>

    /** What it says, as MiniMessage, or `nil` once it's gone. */
    fun text(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): String?

    /** Changes what it says. */
    fun setText(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, text: String): Boolean

    /** The glyph drawn at its left end, or `nil` when it has none (or it's gone). */
    fun icon(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): String?

    /** Draws a glyph at its left end, or none with `nil`. A glyph no resource pack has is an error. */
    fun setIcon(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, icon: String?): Boolean

    /** How full it is, 0 to 1, or `nil` once it's gone. */
    fun progress(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): Double?

    /** Fills it to a fraction. Outside 0 to 1 is an error. */
    fun setProgress(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, progress: Double): Boolean

    /** Its colour, or `nil` once it's gone. */
    fun color(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): String?

    /** Changes its colour. */
    fun setColor(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, color: String): Boolean

    /** Whether it's one bar or split into notches, or `nil` once it's gone. */
    fun style(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): String?

    /** Makes it one bar, or splits it into 6, 10, 12 or 20 notches. */
    fun setStyle(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, style: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Ties it to a centity: it shows to every player within `range` of it (in its world) and hides from
     * them as they go, and with `health` its progress follows the centity's health: what a boss's bar
     * does. Players it was shown to with `show_to` keep it. A bar attached again moves to the new centity.
     */
    fun attach(self: LuaHandle.BossBar, centity: LuaHandle.Centity, options: BossBarAttachOptions?): Boolean

    /** Unties it from its centity: the players it showed to by being near it stop seeing it. */
    fun detach(self: LuaHandle.BossBar)

    /** The centity it's attached to, or `nil`. */
    fun attached(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): LuaHandle.Centity?

    /** Takes it off every screen for good. */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.BossBar): Boolean
}

/**
 * The panel of lines at the right of one player's screen, from `player:sidebar()`: up to 15 lines of
 * MiniMessage under a title, without the score numbers vanilla shows. Setting its title or lines shows
 * it. A sidebar needs a scoreboard of the player's own, so while it shows NetherForge gives them one
 * that copies the server's teams and the lines under name tags (the project's own changes at once,
 * anyone else's within a second), keeping team colours and name tags working; hiding it gives them the
 * server's back. It's cleared when they leave. For someone offline its methods answer `nil` or
 * `false`.
 */
interface SidebarApi {
    /** Its title, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when it has none (or they're offline). */
    fun title(self: LuaHandle.Sidebar): String?

    /** Changes its title, and shows it. */
    fun setTitle(self: LuaHandle.Sidebar, text: String): Boolean

    /** Its lines, top first, as MiniMessage. Empty when it has none (or they're offline). */
    fun lines(self: LuaHandle.Sidebar): List<String>

    /** Replaces its lines, and shows it. More than 15 is an error. */
    fun setLines(self: LuaHandle.Sidebar, lines: List<String>): Boolean

    /** Whether they see it now. `false` when they're offline. */
    fun isVisible(self: LuaHandle.Sidebar): Boolean

    /**
     * Shows it, or hides it and gives them the server's scoreboard back, keeping its title and lines for
     * next time.
     */
    fun setVisible(self: LuaHandle.Sidebar, visible: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Takes its title and lines away and hides it, giving them the server's scoreboard back. */
    fun clear(self: LuaHandle.Sidebar): Boolean
}

/**
 * A scoreboard team, made by `nf.teams.create`: what goes before and after its members' names (over
 * their heads and in the player list), the colour of those names and of their glow, whether members
 * can hurt each other, see each other while invisible, see name tags and push each other. Members are
 * players and other entities. Teams live on the server's scoreboard, so every player sees them (while
 * a player's sidebar shows, its scoreboard copies them). A team belongs to the script that made it and
 * goes when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping), members and all, so the script's body
 * makes it again; from then on its methods answer `nil` or `false`. On the server's scoreboard it's
 * called `nf.<name>` (what `/team` commands name it), so it never collides with a team made with
 * `/team` or by another plugin, and NetherForge never touches those.
 */
interface TeamApi {
    /** Its name, as `nf.teams.create` was given it (without the `nf.` the scoreboard adds). */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.Team): String

    /** Whether it's still there: `false` once it's removed or its script has unloaded. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Team): Boolean

    /**
     * Puts a player or entity in it, taking them out of any other team they're in (one team at a time is
     * Minecraft's rule). A player stays in it when they leave and come back, and can be added while
     * offline.
     */
    fun addMember(self: LuaHandle.Team, member: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Takes a player or entity out of it. */
    fun removeMember(self: LuaHandle.Team, member: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /** Whether a player or entity is in it. `false` once it's gone. */
    fun hasMember(self: LuaHandle.Team, member: LuaHandle.Entity): Boolean

    /**
     * Its members that are here: players online (as `Player`) and entities in loaded chunks. Empty once it
     * has gone.
     */
    fun members(self: LuaHandle.Team): List<LuaHandle.Entity>

    /**
     * What it's called where Minecraft names a team (the `/team list` output), as MiniMessage; `nil` once
     * it's gone.
     */
    fun displayName(self: LuaHandle.Team): String?

    /** Changes what it's called. */
    fun setDisplayName(self: LuaHandle.Team, text: String): Boolean

    /**
     * What goes before each member's name, over their head and in the player list, as MiniMessage; `nil`
     * once it's gone.
     */
    fun prefix(self: LuaHandle.Team): String?

    /** Changes what goes before each member's name. */
    fun setPrefix(self: LuaHandle.Team, text: String): Boolean

    /** What goes after each member's name, as MiniMessage; `nil` once it's gone. */
    fun suffix(self: LuaHandle.Team): String?

    /** Changes what goes after each member's name. */
    fun setSuffix(self: LuaHandle.Team, text: String): Boolean

    /**
     * The colour of its members' names, and of their outline when they glow; `nil` when it has none (or
     * once it's gone).
     */
    fun color(self: LuaHandle.Team): String?

    /**
     * Colours its members' names, and their outline when they glow (a glowing entity's outline is white
     * otherwise). A prefix's own colours still win over it for the prefix.
     */
    fun setColor(self: LuaHandle.Team, color: String?): Boolean

    /** Whether members can hurt each other. `false` once it's gone. */
    fun hasFriendlyFire(self: LuaHandle.Team): Boolean

    /** Lets members hurt each other, or stops them. */
    fun setFriendlyFire(self: LuaHandle.Team, enabled: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Whether members see each other, faintly, while invisible. `false` once it's gone. */
    fun canSeeInvisibleTeammates(self: LuaHandle.Team): Boolean

    /** Lets members see each other while invisible, or stops them. */
    fun setCanSeeInvisibleTeammates(self: LuaHandle.Team, enabled: Boolean): Boolean

    /** Who sees its members' name tags; `nil` once it's gone. */
    fun nametags(self: LuaHandle.Team): String?

    /** Changes who sees its members' name tags. */
    fun setNametags(self: LuaHandle.Team, visibility: String): Boolean

    /** Who its members push, and are pushed by; `nil` once it's gone. */
    fun collision(self: LuaHandle.Team): String?

    /** Changes who its members push, and are pushed by. */
    fun setCollision(self: LuaHandle.Team, rule: String): Boolean

    /** Takes it off the scoreboard for good; its members are in no team any more. */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.Team): Boolean
}

/**
 * A database, from `nf.db()` or `nf.db('network')`. `nf.db()` is this package's own: one SQLite file
 * per package, kept across restarts, which every other package (a dependency, or the project using
 * one) never sees; its tables are made by the package's `migrations/NNN_name.sql` files, applied in
 * order when the project loads. `nf.db('network')` (a name) is a connection to a MySQL or PostgreSQL
 * server that the server's owner set up under `databases:` in the plugin's `config.yml`: shared by
 * every package the owner lists for it, so give tables a name no other package would (`shop_scores`),
 * create them yourself (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`: migrations belong to the package's own database
 * only), and write the SQL in the server's dialect. The handle is the same either way: the same calls,
 * `?` and `params`, rows and `value, err` answers. Every function is asynchronous (see `nf.task`): the
 * work runs on a background thread, so a slow query never holds up the server, and one database runs
 * its work in the order it was given, so a task's `db:execute` is always done before its next
 * `db:query`. Use it from a task (`local rows, err = db:query(...)`) or give a callback. A mistake in
 * the SQL, a constraint a row breaks or a database that couldn't be migrated is `nil, err`, never a
 * thrown error.
 */
interface DatabaseApi {
    /**
     * Runs a statement that reads rows and gives them all. Each row is a table keyed by column name; a
     * `NULL` column is left out of its row (so `row.nickname` is `nil`), integers come as `integer`s,
     * reals as `number`s and text as `string`s; what a MySQL or PostgreSQL server has beyond those arrives
     * as one of them (a boolean as `1` or `0`, a `DECIMAL` as a `number`, a date or time as its text, an
     * integer too big for an `integer` as text; `CAST(column AS CHAR)` keeps a decimal exact). A column
     * that holds a blob is an error: `hex(column)` it in the SQL (`encode(column, 'hex')` on PostgreSQL).
     * One SQL statement in the database's own dialect (SQLite's for the package's own database; MySQL's or
     * PostgreSQL's for a connection the server owner set up), with `?` where a value goes: the values come
     * in `params`, never inside the text, so a name a player typed can't become SQL. A second statement
     * after a `;` is an error: use `db:transaction` for several. On PostgreSQL a literal `?` (the JSON
     * operators) is written `??`. It works off the main thread, so its rows arrives later: give
     * `callback`, which is called with `rows, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`)
     * without one, and it waits and returns `rows, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error.
     * When it fails, `rows` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun query(self: LuaHandle.Database, sql: String, params: LuaValue?): CompletionStage<List<Map<String, Any?>>>

    /**
     * Runs a statement that changes data (`INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`) and says what it changed. It's
     * committed on its own. One SQL statement in the database's own dialect (SQLite's for the package's
     * own database; MySQL's or PostgreSQL's for a connection the server owner set up), with `?` where a
     * value goes: the values come in `params`, never inside the text, so a name a player typed can't
     * become SQL. A second statement after a `;` is an error: use `db:transaction` for several. On
     * PostgreSQL a literal `?` (the JSON operators) is written `??`. It works off the main thread, so its
     * result arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `result, err` once it's done, or call it
     * inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `result, err`. Without a callback
     * outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `result` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun execute(self: LuaHandle.Database, sql: String, params: LuaValue?): CompletionStage<DatabaseResult>

    /**
     * Runs several statements as one: all of them happen, or, when one fails, none do (the ones before it
     * are undone) and the error says which failed. Other work on the database waits until it is done, so
     * nothing sees it half-way. It works off the main thread, so its results arrives later: give
     * `callback`, which is called with `results, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`)
     * without one, and it waits and returns `results, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an
     * error. When it fails, `results` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun transaction(self: LuaHandle.Database, statements: List<DatabaseStatement>): CompletionStage<List<DatabaseResult>>
}

/**
 * A live menu window from `menus/<id>/`: `this` in the menu's script, and what `player:open_menu`,
 * `nf.menus.shared` and every menu event hand out. One that isn't `shared` exists from the moment it's
 * opened for someone until its last viewer closes it; after that its methods return `nil` or `false`
 * rather than erroring, and its handlers (and its slots') are gone. A shared one lives as long as the
 * project.
 */
interface MenuApi {
    /**
     * This window's id: the menu's own id for a shared one, a fresh one for each window of a menu that
     * isn't.
     */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.Menu): String

    /**
     * Which menu it is: its folder name under `menus/`. Nil once the window is gone, and for a window made
     * from a `MenuTemplate` (see `template()`).
     */
    fun kind(self: LuaHandle.Menu): String?

    /**
     * The `MenuTemplate` this window was made from (`player:open_menu(template)`), or `nil` for a window
     * of a menu file, and once the window or the template is gone.
     */
    fun template(self: LuaHandle.Menu): LuaHandle.MenuTemplate?

    /** Whether the window is still there. One that isn't shared goes when its last viewer closes it. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Boolean

    /** Whether this is the one window the whole server shares. */
    fun isShared(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Boolean

    /**
     * What the script that opened this window passed along: `player:open_menu(menu, { context = value })`.
     * The very value, not a copy, so a table can carry anything (the item being bought, a callback, the
     * centity that was clicked). Every event on the window carries it too, as `event.context`. `nil` when
     * nothing was passed, and always for a shared window, which nobody opens with a context.
     */
    fun context(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Any?

    /** How many slots it has. Slots are numbered from 0, left to right, then down. */
    fun size(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Long

    /**
     * How many rows of slots it has: a chest's 1 to 6, a hopper's 1, a dispenser's 3. `size() / rows()` is
     * how many are in a row.
     */
    fun rows(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Long

    /** The title, as MiniMessage, without the skin drawn in front of it. */
    fun title(self: LuaHandle.Menu): String?

    /**
     * Renames the window. Minecraft can't rename a window that's open, so everyone looking sees it close
     * and open again: fine between clicks, wrong every tick.
     */
    fun setTitle(self: LuaHandle.Menu, text: String): Boolean

    /** The resource pack skin drawn behind it, as `<pack>/<key>`, or `nil` for the vanilla look. */
    fun skin(self: LuaHandle.Menu): String?

    /**
     * Changes the background. A skin is drawn in the title, so this costs the same close-and-open
     * `set_title` does. A skin the server's packages don't have is an error.
     */
    fun setSkin(self: LuaHandle.Menu, skin: String?): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether clicks are kept from moving items into or out of it: such a click reaches handlers already
     * cancelled. Scripts hear the clicks either way.
     */
    fun isLocked(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Boolean

    /** Locks or unlocks this window. Not saved: a reload goes back to what the file says. */
    fun setLocked(self: LuaHandle.Menu, locked: Boolean)

    /**
     * Where a shift-click from the player's own inventory goes: into the slots of this window bound to
     * `storage`, from `first` to `last` (storage slot indexes), in order. Stacks top up partial stacks
     * first, then take empty slots, as vanilla does. Each slot's `click` handlers hear the move as a click
     * on it (`event.item` is the stack moving); `event:cancel()` refuses it there, and the item goes to
     * the next slot. Nothing else is tried: an item that no slot takes stays in the player's inventory,
     * and a window's other slots never get it. Applies whether or not the window is locked, and each call
     * replaces the last. A reload forgets it, so the script's body sets it again. The window must be bound
     * to the storage (`slot:bind`), and not be `shared`.
     */
    fun shiftInto(self: LuaHandle.Menu, storage: LuaHandle.Inventory, first: Long?, last: Long?): Boolean

    /**
     * One of its slots, as a handle. A slot outside the window is an error. A handle is the window's id
     * and the index, so it's fine to keep while the window lasts.
     */
    fun slot(self: LuaHandle.Menu, index: Long): LuaHandle.Slot

    /** What is in a slot, or `nil` for an empty one. A slot outside the window is an error. */
    fun item(self: LuaHandle.Menu, index: Long): ItemData?

    /**
     * Puts an item in a slot, replacing what was there, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled
     * field, or an item id the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun setItem(self: LuaHandle.Menu, index: Long, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /** Puts an item wherever it fits, topping up matching stacks first, the way picking something up does. */
    fun addItem(self: LuaHandle.Menu, item: ItemData): Long

    /** Every item in it, keyed by slot. Empty slots are left out, so `pairs` visits only what is there. */
    fun items(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Map<Long, ItemData>

    /**
     * Puts several items in at once, by slot, replacing what was in those slots; the others are left
     * alone. Every item is checked before any slot changes, so a mistake (a slot outside the window, a
     * misspelled field, an item the server doesn't have) is an error that leaves the window as it was.
     */
    fun setItems(self: LuaHandle.Menu, items: Map<Long, ItemData>): Boolean

    /**
     * Puts a copy of one item in every empty slot, or in each of the listed slots (empty or not): a border
     * of glass panes, say. A slot outside the window is an error.
     */
    fun fill(self: LuaHandle.Menu, item: ItemData, indices: List<Long>?): Boolean

    /** Empties every slot. To empty one, `set_item(index, nil)`. */
    fun clear(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Boolean

    /** Everyone looking at it now. */
    fun viewers(self: LuaHandle.Menu): List<LuaHandle.Player>

    /**
     * What a viewer is carrying on their cursor while they have this window open, or `nil` for nothing
     * (and when they aren't looking at it).
     */
    fun cursorItem(self: LuaHandle.Menu, player: LuaHandle.Player): ItemData?

    /**
     * Puts an item on a viewer's cursor, replacing what they were carrying, or empties it. What's on the
     * cursor goes back to their inventory (or drops) when they close the window.
     */
    fun setCursorItem(self: LuaHandle.Menu, player: LuaHandle.Player, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /**
     * Sends the whole window to everyone looking at it again. Changes are sent by themselves, so this is
     * only for when a viewer's screen may be out of step: after a cancelled click left a ghost item on it,
     * say.
     */
    fun refresh(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Boolean

    /**
     * Shows this very window to someone too: a second pair of eyes on one menu. Its `open` handlers hear
     * it. To give someone a window of their own, use `player:open_menu`.
     */
    fun openFor(self: LuaHandle.Menu, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Closes it for one viewer. */
    fun closeFor(self: LuaHandle.Menu, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Closes it for everyone looking at it. */
    fun closeAll(self: LuaHandle.Menu): Boolean
}

/**
 * One slot of one menu window, from `menu:slot(index)` (`this:slot(index)` in the menu's script).
 * After the window goes, its methods return `nil` or `false` rather than erroring, and its handlers
 * are gone.
 */
interface SlotApi {
    /** Its number in the window, from 0. */
    fun index(self: LuaHandle.Slot): Long

    /** The window it belongs to. */
    fun menu(self: LuaHandle.Slot): LuaHandle.Menu

    /** What is in it, or `nil` when it's empty or the window is gone. */
    fun item(self: LuaHandle.Slot): ItemData?

    /**
     * Puts an item in it, replacing what was there, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled field,
     * or an item id the server doesn't have, is an error.
     */
    fun setItem(self: LuaHandle.Slot, item: ItemData?): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes this slot a view of a slot of a player's `storage`: it shows what's there, and whatever goes
     * in or out of it, by a click, a drag, a shift-click or a script's `set_item` on either, is saved in
     * the storage at once, as the game moves it. So the item is only ever in one place, whatever happens
     * next: the window closing, the player leaving or dying, the server stopping or the project reloading.
     * In a locked window players can still put items in and take them out of a bound slot by hand
     * (double-click gathering stays locked, and a shift-click from their own inventory goes only where
     * `shift_into` says); its `click` handlers can still refuse what doesn't belong (`event:cancel()`).
     * Binding a slot again moves it to the new place. Only a window of a menu that isn't `shared` can be
     * bound, and only to a storage (`player:storage`): another inventory, or a slot outside the storage,
     * is an error.
     */
    fun bind(self: LuaHandle.Slot, storage: LuaHandle.Inventory, index: Long?): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes this slot a view of what a player wears in one place, as `bind` does a storage's slot: what
     * goes in or out of it is put on or taken off at once, and when what they wear changes some other way
     * (a right-click with armour, a script's `set_equipment`) the slot shows it by the next tick. Locked
     * windows let players use it by hand, as a bound slot. The game doesn't check what's put on: give the
     * slot a rule (`set_rule({ wearable = "head", max = 1 })`). Only a window of a menu that isn't
     * `shared` can be bound.
     */
    fun bindEquipment(self: LuaHandle.Slot, player: LuaHandle.Player, equipment: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Says what this slot takes, or with `nil` lets it take anything again. Whatever a player puts in, by
     * a click, a drag, a number key or a shift-click from their own inventory into `shift_into`'s slots,
     * is held to it: a stack it doesn't take stays where it was, and of one it does, only `max` goes in
     * (the rest stays on the cursor). Its `click` handlers still hear what's let through. Scripts' own
     * `set_item` aren't held to it. A shift-click from below that the game moves itself (no `shift_into`)
     * isn't either, in a window that isn't locked.
     */
    fun setRule(self: LuaHandle.Slot, rule: SlotRule?): Boolean

    /** What this slot takes, or `nil` when it takes anything (or the window is gone). */
    fun rule(self: LuaHandle.Slot): SlotRule?

    /**
     * Whether this slot's rule takes this item (any count of it). `true` for a slot without a rule;
     * `false` once the window is gone.
     */
    fun accepts(self: LuaHandle.Slot, item: ItemData): Boolean

    /**
     * Stops showing a storage's slot (or a player's equipment) here. The slot is emptied, since what it
     * showed is still where it was. Its player leaving unbinds every slot bound to their storage or
     * equipment.
     */
    fun unbind(self: LuaHandle.Slot): Boolean

    /** Which storage slot this slot shows, or `nil` when it shows none. */
    fun binding(self: LuaHandle.Slot): SlotBinding?
}

/**
 * A menu made by a script with `nf.menus.create` rather than read from `menus/<id>/`: what a window
 * opened from it starts as. Open a window of it with `player:open_menu(template)`, as many as you
 * like, each a `Menu` of its own; handlers on the template hear every one of its windows (after the
 * window's own handlers). It lives as long as the script that created it: when that script unloads, or
 * `remove()` is called, its windows close and its handlers go, and its methods answer `nil` or `false`
 * rather than erroring.
 */
interface MenuTemplateApi {
    /**
     * Its id, made up when it was created. It never names a folder under `menus/`, and it's only good
     * while the template lasts.
     */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): String

    /** Whether it's still there: until `remove()`, or until the script that created it unloads. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): Boolean

    /** How many slots its windows have. */
    fun size(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): Long

    /** How many rows of slots its windows have: a chest's 1 to 6, a hopper's 1, a dispenser's 3. */
    fun rows(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): Long

    /** The title its windows start with, as MiniMessage, without the skin. */
    fun title(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): String?

    /** The resource pack skin its windows start with, as `<pack>/<key>`, or `nil` for the vanilla look. */
    fun skin(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): String?

    /**
     * Whether its windows start locked: clicks that would move items into or out of them reach handlers
     * already cancelled.
     */
    fun isLocked(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): Boolean

    /**
     * What a slot of a new window starts with, or `nil` for an empty one. A slot outside its windows is an
     * error.
     */
    fun item(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate, index: Long): ItemData?

    /** Every window made from it that is still open. */
    fun windows(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): List<LuaHandle.Menu>

    /** Takes it away before its script unloads: its windows close, and its handlers go. */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.MenuTemplate): Boolean
}

/**
 * A dialog from `dialogs/<id>/`: `this` in the dialog's script, and what `nf.dialogs.get` returns. A
 * dialog holds nothing, so there is one of each for the whole server and showing it is all there is to
 * do with it. Every button runs Lua with the answers to the inputs in hand; none of them is a command.
 * Handlers on it last while the project has the dialog, across reloads of it. One made by
 * `nf.dialogs.create` works the same way, and lasts as long as the script that made it.
 */
interface DialogApi {
    /** Its folder name under `dialogs/`, or for one from `nf.dialogs.create`, an id made up for it. */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.Dialog): String

    /**
     * Whether the project still has this dialog (a reload can take it away), or, for one from
     * `nf.dialogs.create`, whether the script that made it is still running.
     */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Dialog): Boolean

    /** Its title, as MiniMessage. */
    fun title(self: LuaHandle.Dialog): String?

    /** The button keys, in the order they are drawn: what `event.key` can be. */
    fun buttons(self: LuaHandle.Dialog): List<String>

    /** The input keys, in the order they are asked: the keys of `event.values`. */
    fun inputs(self: LuaHandle.Dialog): List<String>

    /** One of its buttons, as a handle. A key the dialog has no button for is an error. */
    fun button(self: LuaHandle.Dialog, key: String): LuaHandle.Button

    /**
     * Puts it on someone's screen. It's built fresh each time, so what they see is the dialog as it is
     * now, with whatever `options` change for this opening. A project dialog already on their screen is
     * closed (its `close` handlers hear it).
     */
    fun openFor(self: LuaHandle.Dialog, player: LuaHandle.Player, options: DialogOpenOptions?): Boolean
}

/**
 * One button of a dialog, from `dialog:button(key)` (`this:button(key)` in the dialog's script). If a
 * reload takes the button away, its methods return `nil` rather than erroring, and its handlers are
 * gone.
 */
interface ButtonApi {
    /** Its `key` in `dialog.json`: what `event.key` is when it's pressed. */
    fun key(self: LuaHandle.Button): String

    /** The dialog it belongs to. */
    fun dialog(self: LuaHandle.Button): LuaHandle.Dialog

    /** What it says, as MiniMessage, or `nil` once the dialog no longer has it. */
    fun label(self: LuaHandle.Button): String?
}

/**
 * A project item from `items/<id>/item.json`: `this` in the item's script, and what `nf.items.get`
 * hands out. It's the item itself, not one stack of it: its handlers hear what players do with any
 * stack of it, wherever the stack came from. A stack of it is an `Item` table whose `item` is this id.
 * If the item is deleted, its methods answer `nil` or `false` and its handlers go.
 */
interface ProjectItemApi {
    /** Its id: its folder name under `items/`. */
    fun id(self: LuaHandle.ProjectItem): String

    /** Whether the project still has it. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.ProjectItem): Boolean

    /**
     * A stack of it, as an `Item` table: the same as `nf.items.create(this:id(), overrides)`. `nil` once
     * the item is gone.
     */
    fun create(self: LuaHandle.ProjectItem, overrides: LuaValue?): ItemData?

    /**
     * Its `properties` from its `item.json`: the project's own facts about it, any JSON (`{ damage = 12,
     * class = "melee" }`), as a table. A new table each call, so keep it rather than ask in a loop. An
     * empty table when the file has none, `nil` once the item is gone.
     */
    fun properties(self: LuaHandle.ProjectItem): Any?
}

/**
 * One playing particle effect, from `nf.particles.play`. Once it has ended (it finished, was stopped,
 * lost what it followed, its file was deleted, or the script that played it unloaded) its methods
 * answer `nil` or `false` rather than erroring, except `kind()`. An effect is ephemeral: it has no
 * `id()` and can't be saved in a `data()` table.
 */
interface EffectApi {
    /** Which effect it is: its folder name under `particles/`, even after it has ended. */
    fun kind(self: LuaHandle.Effect): String

    /**
     * Whether it's still playing. `false` once it has ended, for any reason (its `end` handlers still see
     * `true`).
     */
    fun isActive(self: LuaHandle.Effect): Boolean

    /**
     * Ends it now, with reason `"stopped"`. Particles already spawned live out their short lives on the
     * players' screens.
     */
    fun stop(self: LuaHandle.Effect): Boolean

    /** Where it is now, facing the way it faces: in world space. `nil` once it has ended. */
    fun location(self: LuaHandle.Effect): LuaLocation?

    /**
     * Moves it, and stops it following anything. A `Location`'s `yaw` and `pitch` become its facing (nil
     * is 0); a `Vec3` keeps its world and its facing.
     */
    fun teleport(self: LuaHandle.Effect, locationOrPosition: LocationOrVec3): Boolean

    /**
     * Makes it follow something, from the next tick on. Each tick, before it spawns anything, the effect
     * moves to the target: its position (a node's `world_position()`), facing the target's yaw (a node's
     * centity's), pitch 0. When the target stops existing (a removed centity, a player who left, an entity
     * that died or was removed, a centity or entity whose chunk unloaded) the effect ends with reason
     * `"target_gone"`. Calling it again switches targets; `teleport` stops following.
     */
    fun follow(self: LuaHandle.Effect, target: LuaHandle, offset: Vec3?): Boolean
}

/**
 * A streamed HTTP response, from `nf.http.stream`: its `open`, each `message` and its one `end` are
 * events on it. It belongs to the script that opened it and ends with reason `"unloaded"` when that
 * script unloads (a reload, the server stopping). Once it has ended its methods answer as an ended
 * stream does rather than erroring. Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
 */
interface HttpStreamApi {
    /** The URL it was opened with, even after it has ended. */
    fun url(self: LuaHandle.HttpStream): String

    /**
     * `"connecting"` until its `open` event, then `"open"`, and `"ended"` once its `end` event has been
     * heard.
     */
    fun state(self: LuaHandle.HttpStream): String

    /**
     * The response's status code once it has opened (`200`, `404`); `nil` before, and once it has ended
     * (its `end` event has it).
     */
    fun status(self: LuaHandle.HttpStream): Long?

    /**
     * Ends it now, with reason `"closed"`: the connection is dropped and messages that arrived but weren't
     * handed over yet are thrown away.
     */
    fun close(self: LuaHandle.HttpStream): Boolean
}

/**
 * A WebSocket, from `nf.http.websocket`: `send` text on it, and hear its `open`, each `message` and
 * its one `end` as events. It belongs to the script that opened it and ends with reason `"unloaded"`
 * when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping). Once it has ended its methods answer as an
 * ended socket does rather than erroring. Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
 */
interface WebSocketApi {
    /** The URL it was opened with, even after it has ended. */
    fun url(self: LuaHandle.WebSocket): String

    /**
     * `"connecting"` until its `open` event, then `"open"`, and `"ended"` once its `end` event has been
     * heard.
     */
    fun state(self: LuaHandle.WebSocket): String

    /**
     * The subprotocol the other side chose from `options.protocols`, while it's open; `nil` before and
     * after, or when it chose none.
     */
    fun protocol(self: LuaHandle.WebSocket): String?

    /**
     * Sends a text message. Before the socket opens it waits and goes, in order, once it does. A message
     * over `connections.max-message-bytes` is an error at the script's line; one past the package's
     * `connections.bytes-per-minute` ends the socket (reason `"limit"`) instead of going.
     */
    fun send(self: LuaHandle.WebSocket, text: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Ends it now, with reason `"closed"`: the other side is told with `code` and `reason`, and messages
     * that arrived but weren't handed over yet are thrown away.
     */
    fun close(self: LuaHandle.WebSocket, code: Long?, reason: String?): Boolean
}

/**
 * A route a script answers, from `nf.web.route`. It goes when its script unloads or `remove` is
 * called.
 */
interface RouteApi {
    /** Stops answering: later requests to it are `404`, and requests still waiting are answered `503`. */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.Route): Boolean

    /** Whether it still answers. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Route): Boolean
}

/**
 * How a route's handler answers its request: once, with `send`, `json` or `redirect`, during the
 * handler or later (from a task, or a callback). Every answer also says `X-Content-Type-Options:
 * nosniff`, and `Cache-Control: no-store` unless you set one. One not answered within
 * `web.handler-timeout-seconds` is answered `504`; one whose route's script unloads first, `503`.
 * Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
 */
interface WebResponseApi {
    /**
     * Answers with `status`, `body` and `headers`. The `Content-Type` is `text/plain; charset=utf-8`
     * unless `headers` sets one (`["content-type"] = "text/html; charset=utf-8"` for a page). A body over
     * `web.max-response-bytes`, a status that isn't one, or a header that can't be sent (one the server
     * sets, like `Content-Length`) is an error at the script's line.
     */
    fun send(self: LuaHandle.WebResponse, status: Long, body: String?, headers: Map<String, String>?): Boolean

    /**
     * Answers with `status` and `value` written as JSON (`application/json`), as `nf.json.encode` writes
     * it.
     */
    fun json(self: LuaHandle.WebResponse, status: Long, value: LuaValue): Boolean

    /** Answers with a redirect to `url` (`302`, or `status`: `301`, `303`, `307` or `308`). */
    fun redirect(self: LuaHandle.WebResponse, url: String, status: Long?): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it still waits for an answer: `false` once it's answered, timed out, or its route's script
     * unloaded.
     */
    fun isPending(self: LuaHandle.WebResponse): Boolean
}

/**
 * A "log in with…" flow, from `nf.web.oauth`: `begin` a login for a player, and hear its `complete` or
 * `fail`. It belongs to the script that made it.
 */
interface OAuthFlowApi {
    /**
     * Begins a login for `player`: the address of the service's page to send them to (as a link in chat),
     * with a new state and code challenge that only this login's callback can use, for 10 minutes. `nil`
     * while the server owner hasn't turned web routes on, or once the flow has gone.
     */
    fun begin(self: LuaHandle.OAuthFlow, player: LuaHandle.Player): String?

    /** Ends the flow: its callback route goes and logins under way are forgotten. */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.OAuthFlow): Boolean
}

/**
 * An instance of a WebAssembly module, from `nf.wasm.instantiate`: its own memory, kept from call to
 * call, so a function can build state another reads. Its calls run off the main thread one at a time,
 * in the order they were made. It belongs to the script that made it and is closed when that script
 * unloads. Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
 */
interface WasmInstanceApi {
    /** The module it is an instance of, as the calling script names it. */
    fun module(self: LuaHandle.WasmInstance): String

    /**
     * Calls one of the functions the module exports and gives its results. It's stopped past
     * `wasm.max-call-seconds`; a call that traps (an out-of-bounds read, `unreachable`, a division by
     * zero) or is stopped fails with `nil, err`, and the instance is closed after it (its memory may be
     * half-written). It works off the main thread, so its results arrives later: give `callback`, which is
     * called with `results, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it
     * waits and returns `results, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails,
     * `results` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun call(self: LuaHandle.WasmInstance, name: String, arguments: LuaValue?): CompletionStage<List<Any?>>

    /**
     * Calls a function that answers with text: it returns two `i32`s (where the text starts and how many
     * bytes it is) or one `i64` holding both (the start in its high 32 bits). The text is read from the
     * module's memory as UTF-8, then freed as `call` frees a string argument. Otherwise as `call`: for
     * text in and text out, JSON included. It works off the main thread, so its text arrives later: give
     * `callback`, which is called with `text, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`)
     * without one, and it waits and returns `text, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error.
     * When it fails, `text` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun callText(self: LuaHandle.WasmInstance, name: String, arguments: LuaValue?): CompletionStage<String>

    /**
     * Closes it now, freeing its memory: calls still waiting fail, and later ones do at once. Unloading
     * the script that made it closes it too.
     */
    fun close(self: LuaHandle.WasmInstance): Boolean
}

/**
 * A program started with `nf.wasm.start`: `send` it lines and hear each line it prints as `output`,
 * then its one `end`. It runs on a thread of its own, waiting while it reads input nobody has sent. It
 * belongs to the script that started it and ends with reason `"unloaded"` when that script unloads (a
 * reload, the server stopping). Once it has ended its methods answer as an ended program does rather
 * than erroring. Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
 */
interface WasmProcessApi {
    /** The module it runs, as the calling script names it, even after it has ended. */
    fun module(self: LuaHandle.WasmProcess): String

    /** `"running"` until its `end` event, then `"ended"`. */
    fun state(self: LuaHandle.WasmProcess): String

    /**
     * Writes a line to its input, with a line ending after it: the program reads it when it next reads. A
     * line over `wasm.max-line-bytes` is an error at the script's line.
     */
    fun send(self: LuaHandle.WasmProcess, line: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Ends its input: once it has read what was sent, it reads the end of the file, which most programs
     * answer by finishing what they were doing and exiting.
     */
    fun closeInput(self: LuaHandle.WasmProcess): Boolean

    /** Ends it now, with reason `"stopped"`: lines it printed that weren't handed over yet are thrown away. */
    fun stop(self: LuaHandle.WasmProcess): Boolean
}

/**
 * A channel of the network, from `nf.network.channel`: publish on it with `publish`, ask one server
 * with `request`, and hear messages and requests as its `message` and `request` events. A server
 * listens to a channel while any of its scripts has a handler on it; handlers go with their scripts,
 * as any handler does.
 */
interface NetworkChannelApi {
    /**
     * Its name, as the calling package names it: bare when it's the caller's own, `namespace:name`
     * otherwise.
     */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.NetworkChannel): String

    /**
     * Publishes `value` on the channel: every server listening hears it as a `message` event, this one
     * included, or only `options.server`. It goes at once, through the hub; nothing waits for it. Anything
     * a `data()` table can hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans,
     * `Vec3`, `Location` (its world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which
     * another server may not have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64
     * KiB unless its owner says otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an
     * error at the script's line.
     */
    fun publish(self: LuaHandle.NetworkChannel, value: LuaValue, options: NetworkPublishOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Asks `server` on this channel: its `request` handlers answer, and the answer comes back here. It
     * fails (`nil, err`) when this server isn't connected, that server isn't, nothing there handles
     * requests on the channel, a handler there errored, or no answer came within
     * `network.request-timeout-seconds`. A handler that answers `nil` gives `nil` with no `err`. Anything
     * a `data()` table can hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans,
     * `Vec3`, `Location` (its world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which
     * another server may not have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64
     * KiB unless its owner says otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an
     * error at the script's line. It works off the main thread, so its reply arrives later: give
     * `callback`, which is called with `reply, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`)
     * without one, and it waits and returns `reply, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an
     * error. When it fails, `reply` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun request(self: LuaHandle.NetworkChannel, server: String, data: LuaValue): CompletionStage<Any?>
}

/**
 * A map the whole network shares, from `nf.network.map`: string keys, any values. Reads (`get`, `all`)
 * answer at once from this server's copy. Writes go to the hub, which makes them in the order they
 * arrive and tells every server that has the map: `set` and `delete` don't wait, and this server's
 * copy has the new value once the hub has made the change (the next tick or so, when `change` is
 * heard); `add` and `insert` wait for the hub's answer, so they're safe when two servers write one key
 * at once. A key may expire. The hub keeps them for the whole network, saved on the proxy, within the
 * limits of its `hub.properties` (how many keys and bytes each package may keep).
 */
interface NetworkMapApi {
    /**
     * Its name, as the calling package names it: bare when it's the caller's own, `namespace:name`
     * otherwise.
     */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap): String

    /**
     * Whether this server's copy has arrived from the hub: until then `get` answers `nil` and `all` an
     * empty table. Its `ready` event says when.
     */
    fun isReady(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap): Boolean

    /**
     * A key's value in this server's copy, or `nil` when it has none (or the copy hasn't arrived). A table
     * comes back new each call: changing it changes nothing.
     */
    fun get(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap, key: String): Any?

    /** Every key and its value in this server's copy. */
    fun all(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap): Map<String, Any?>

    /**
     * Sets a key, for the whole network: through the hub, without waiting. Anything a `data()` table can
     * hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans, `Vec3`, `Location` (its
     * world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which another server may not
     * have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64 KiB unless its owner says
     * otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an error at the script's line.
     * A key is 1 to 256 characters. What the hub refuses (a package over its limits) is logged.
     */
    fun set(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap, key: String, value: LuaValue, options: NetworkMapWriteOptions?): Boolean

    /** Deletes a key, for the whole network: through the hub, without waiting. */
    fun delete(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap, key: String): Boolean

    /**
     * Adds `amount` to a key's number, made by the hub in one step: two servers adding at once both count.
     * A key with no value starts at 0; one holding something that isn't a number fails. It keeps the
     * expiry it had, unless `options.expires` gives a new one. It works off the main thread, so its total
     * arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `total, err` once it's done, or call it inside
     * a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `total, err`. Without a callback outside a
     * task, it's an error. When it fails, `total` is `nil` and `err` says why.
     */
    fun add(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap, key: String, amount: Double, options: NetworkMapWriteOptions?): CompletionStage<Double>

    /**
     * Sets a key to `data` only if it has no value, made by the hub in one step: of two servers inserting
     * one key at once, exactly one gets `true`. A claim, a lock or a reservation: give it
     * `options.expires` so a server that stops holding it can't keep it forever. It works off the main
     * thread, so its inserted arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `inserted, err` once
     * it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `inserted,
     * err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `inserted` is `nil` and `err`
     * says why.
     */
    fun insert(self: LuaHandle.NetworkMap, key: String, data: LuaValue, options: NetworkMapWriteOptions?): CompletionStage<Boolean>
}

/**
 * One cutscene playing for one player, from `nf.cutscenes.play`. Once it has ended (it finished, was
 * stopped or skipped, was replaced by another, its player left, or the script that played it unloaded)
 * its methods answer `nil` or `false` rather than erroring, except `kind()` and `length()`. A cutscene
 * is ephemeral: it has no `id()` and can't be saved in a `data()` table.
 */
interface CutsceneApi {
    /** Which cutscene it is: its file name under `cutscenes/`, even after it has ended. */
    fun kind(self: LuaHandle.Cutscene): String

    /** How long it lasts, in seconds, even after it has ended. */
    fun length(self: LuaHandle.Cutscene): Double

    /** Who is watching. `nil` once it has ended. */
    fun player(self: LuaHandle.Cutscene): LuaHandle.Player?

    /**
     * Whether it's still playing. `false` once it has ended, for any reason (its `end` handlers still see
     * `true`).
     */
    fun isActive(self: LuaHandle.Cutscene): Boolean

    /** Seconds played so far. `nil` once it has ended. */
    fun time(self: LuaHandle.Cutscene): Double?

    /** Ends it now, with reason `"stopped"`, and puts the player back. */
    fun stop(self: LuaHandle.Cutscene): Boolean
}

/**
 * A screen: maps (each 128 by 128 pixels) side by side, which scripts draw on and play videos on,
 * shown in item frames (`place`, or the file's `place`) or as map items in players' hands (`item`).
 * Pixel `0, 0` is its top left. What it shows is the server's to send and isn't saved: draw it
 * whenever its script starts. Once a screen is gone (a script's screen whose script unloaded, a
 * project screen whose file was deleted) its drawing does nothing and its getters answer `nil` or
 * `false`. A screen is drawn for every player near it (`viewers = "near"`), or only for those a script
 * shows it to. `for_player` gives a player a view of their own: the same maps, drawn differently for
 * them alone.
 */
interface ScreenApi {
    /**
     * The project's screen it is (its folder name under `screens/`), or the `key` a script made it with;
     * `nil` for a script's screen made without one.
     */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.Screen): String?

    /** Whether it's still there. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Screen): Boolean

    /** How wide it is, in pixels: 128 a map. */
    fun width(self: LuaHandle.Screen): Long

    /** How tall it is, in pixels: 128 a map. */
    fun height(self: LuaHandle.Screen): Long

    /** Fills the whole screen with one colour. Nothing happens once the screen is gone. */
    fun clear(self: LuaHandle.Screen, color: String?)

    /**
     * One pixel's colour, as the map draws it: `#rrggbb`, or `"clear"`. `nil` for a pixel off the screen,
     * or once the screen is gone.
     */
    fun pixel(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long): String?

    /** Sets one pixel. One off the screen is left alone. Nothing happens once the screen is gone. */
    fun setPixel(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, color: String?)

    /**
     * A block of pixels' colours, row by row (`#rrggbb`, or `"clear"`): what `set_pixels` takes. Pixels
     * off the screen are `"clear"`; an empty list once the screen is gone. A block of more than 65536
     * pixels is an error.
     */
    fun pixels(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, width: Long, height: Long): List<String>

    /**
     * Sets a block of pixels from a list of colours, row by row, `width` to a row: the quick way to draw
     * many pixels at once. What's off the screen is left alone. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
     */
    fun setPixels(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, width: Long, colors: List<String>)

    /** Fills a rectangle with one colour. Nothing happens once the screen is gone. */
    fun fill(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, width: Long, height: Long, color: String)

    /**
     * Draws a rectangle: filled, outlined, or both, with rounded corners if `style.radius` says. Nothing
     * happens once the screen is gone.
     */
    fun rectangle(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, width: Long, height: Long, style: ShapeStyle)

    /** Draws a straight line between two pixels, both included. Nothing happens once the screen is gone. */
    fun line(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x1: Long, y1: Long, x2: Long, y2: Long, color: String, thickness: Long?)

    /** Draws a circle round a pixel: filled, outlined, or both. Nothing happens once the screen is gone. */
    fun circle(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, radius: Long, style: ShapeStyle)

    /** Draws an ellipse round a pixel. Nothing happens once the screen is gone. */
    fun ellipse(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, radiusX: Long, radiusY: Long, style: ShapeStyle)

    /**
     * Draws a closed shape through points, `{ x1, y1, x2, y2, … }` (at least three points). Fewer than
     * three, or an odd count of numbers, is an error. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
     */
    fun polygon(self: LuaHandle.Screen, points: List<Long>, style: ShapeStyle)

    /**
     * Writes text with its top left at a pixel. Lines break at `\n`, and at spaces past `options.width`
     * when it's given. MiniMessage colours, `<b>`, `<u>` and `<st>` are drawn; glyphs and other tags
     * aren't. An unknown font is an error. Returns how big it was, as `nf.screens.measure_text` would say
     * (`0` by `0` once the screen is gone).
     */
    fun text(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, text: String, options: ScreenTextOptions?): ScreenTextSize

    /**
     * Draws a picture: a texture of a resource pack, as `<pack>/<path>` (`ui/logo` is
     * `resource_packs/ui/textures/logo.png`). Its colours become map colours as `options.dither` says;
     * pixels less than half opaque draw nothing. A picture the project doesn't have is an error. Returns
     * `false` when the picture can't be read. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
     */
    fun image(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, image: String, options: ScreenImageOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Copies a rectangle of the screen to another place on it (overlapping is fine): a quick way to
     * scroll. What's off the screen is left alone. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
     */
    fun copy(self: LuaHandle.Screen, x: Long, y: Long, width: Long, height: Long, toX: Long, toY: Long)

    /**
     * Hangs it on a wall: an invisible, fixed item frame a map, its top left map at `location`'s block
     * (the air in front of the wall) and the rest going right and down as a player facing it sees them.
     * The frames need no wall behind them. A screen already hanging somewhere is moved. A project screen
     * hangs where its file says again when the server restarts. Returns `false` when the world isn't
     * loaded.
     */
    fun place(self: LuaHandle.Screen, location: LuaLocation, facing: String): Boolean

    /** Takes its frames down. It can still be drawn on, and its map items still show it. */
    fun takeDown(self: LuaHandle.Screen): Boolean

    /** Where its top left map hangs; `nil` when it hangs nowhere. */
    fun location(self: LuaHandle.Screen): LuaLocation?

    /** Which way it faces where it hangs; `nil` when it hangs nowhere. */
    fun facing(self: LuaHandle.Screen): String?

    /**
     * A map item showing one of its maps, to give a player or put in a frame: `column` and `row` count
     * from 0 at its top left. A map off the screen is an error. A screen made without a `key` gets new map
     * numbers each time it's made, so its items show nothing after a restart.
     */
    fun item(self: LuaHandle.Screen, column: Long?, row: Long?): ItemData

    /**
     * Draws it for a player, whoever it's drawn for otherwise (a `viewers = "script"` screen is drawn only
     * for those it's shown to). Lasts until `hide_from`, or the screen goes.
     */
    fun showTo(self: LuaHandle.Screen, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Stops drawing it for a player (they see blank maps, and can't click it), until `show_to`. */
    fun hideFrom(self: LuaHandle.Screen, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /**
     * Whether it is drawn for a player now: shown to them, or near them on a `"near"` screen, and not
     * hidden from them.
     */
    fun isShownTo(self: LuaHandle.Screen, player: LuaHandle.Player): Boolean

    /** Everyone it is drawn for now. */
    fun viewers(self: LuaHandle.Screen): List<LuaHandle.Player>

    /**
     * A player's own view of the screen: drawing on it changes what they see alone (the same maps, the
     * same frames), starting from what everyone sees now. It lasts until `release`, they leave, or the
     * screen goes; drawing on the shared screen meanwhile doesn't reach them. Called on a player's own
     * view, it's that view.
     */
    fun forPlayer(self: LuaHandle.Screen, player: LuaHandle.Player): LuaHandle.Screen

    /** Whose own view this is; `nil` for the screen everyone sees. */
    fun player(self: LuaHandle.Screen): LuaHandle.Player?

    /** Ends a player's own view (see `for_player`): they see what everyone sees again. */
    fun release(self: LuaHandle.Screen): Boolean

    /**
     * Plays a video (`videos/<id>/`) on the screen, from its top left (`options.x`, `options.y` move it),
     * replacing any video playing on it (that one ends, reason `"replaced"`). Its sound plays to the
     * players it's drawn for who are near where it hangs. It belongs to the script that played it: when
     * that script unloads, it ends. A video the project doesn't have is an error; one not yet converted in
     * the editor gives `nil`.
     */
    fun play(self: LuaHandle.Screen, video: String, options: VideoPlayOptions?): LuaHandle.Playback?

    /** The video playing on it now; `nil` when none is. */
    fun playback(self: LuaHandle.Screen): LuaHandle.Playback?

    /**
     * Takes a script's screen away for good: its frames come down, and its video ends. A screen made with
     * a `key` gives up its maps' numbers too: one made again with that key gets new ones, and an item or
     * frame of the old ones shows nothing. A project screen can't be removed (its file says it's there):
     * that's an error.
     */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.Screen): Boolean
}

/**
 * A video playing on a screen, from `screen:play`. Once it has ended its methods answer `nil` or
 * `false`, except `video()` and `length()`; `exists()` says whether it has.
 */
interface PlaybackApi {
    /** Which video it is: its folder name under `videos/`, even after it has ended. */
    fun video(self: LuaHandle.Playback): String

    /** The screen it plays on; `nil` once it has ended. */
    fun screen(self: LuaHandle.Playback): LuaHandle.Screen?

    /** How long the video lasts, in seconds, even after it has ended. */
    fun length(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Double

    /** Whether it hasn't ended: paused counts. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Boolean

    /** Whether it is paused. */
    fun isPaused(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Boolean

    /** Holds it on the frame it shows, its sound stopped. */
    fun pause(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Boolean

    /** Plays it on from where it was paused, its sound from there too. */
    fun resume(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Boolean

    /** Ends it now, with reason `"stopped"`. The screen keeps its last frame. */
    fun stop(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Boolean

    /** Where it is, in seconds from the video's start; `nil` once it has ended. */
    fun time(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Double?

    /**
     * Jumps to a time in the video, in seconds (held to 0 and its length); its sound starts again from
     * there.
     */
    fun seek(self: LuaHandle.Playback, seconds: Double): Boolean

    /** How fast it plays: 1 is as recorded. `nil` once it has ended. */
    fun speed(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Double?

    /** Plays it faster or slower, 0.25 to 4 (held to that). Its sound plays only at speed 1. */
    fun setSpeed(self: LuaHandle.Playback, speed: Double?): Boolean

    /** Its sound's volume, 0 to 1; `nil` once it has ended. */
    fun volume(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Double?

    /** Changes its sound's volume, 0 to 1 (held to that); its sound starts again at the new volume. */
    fun setVolume(self: LuaHandle.Playback, volume: Double?): Boolean

    /** Whether it starts again when it reaches its end. */
    fun isLooping(self: LuaHandle.Playback): Boolean

    /** Whether it starts again when it reaches its end. */
    fun setLooping(self: LuaHandle.Playback, looping: Boolean): Boolean
}

/**
 * A path in the scripts' data directory (`plugins/NetherForge/data/`, on the editor's dev server too).
 * The only state a script has that survives a restart. Files are capped at 1 MiB; writes past it are
 * refused, not truncated.
 */
interface FileApi {
    /** The path from the data directory; `""` for the directory itself. */
    fun path(self: LuaHandle.File): String

    /** The last part of the path. */
    fun name(self: LuaHandle.File): String

    /** The folder it's in, or `nil` for the data directory itself. */
    fun parent(self: LuaHandle.File): LuaHandle.File?

    /** Something inside this folder, whether or not it exists yet. `nil` for a name that isn't allowed. */
    fun child(self: LuaHandle.File, name: String): LuaHandle.File?

    /** Whether anything is there. */
    fun exists(self: LuaHandle.File): Boolean

    /** Whether it's a folder. */
    fun isFolder(self: LuaHandle.File): Boolean

    /** Its size in bytes, or `nil` when it isn't a file. */
    fun size(self: LuaHandle.File): Long?

    /** What's directly inside, sorted by name; empty for a file or nothing. */
    fun children(self: LuaHandle.File): List<LuaHandle.File>

    /** The contents, or `nil` when there's no file. */
    fun read(self: LuaHandle.File): String?

    /** Replaces the contents, creating folders above it as needed. */
    fun write(self: LuaHandle.File, text: String): Boolean

    /** Adds to the end. */
    fun append(self: LuaHandle.File, text: String): Boolean

    /**
     * The contents parsed as JSON, as `nf.json.decode` does, or `nil` when there's no file or it isn't
     * JSON.
     */
    fun readJson(self: LuaHandle.File): Any?

    /** Writes a value as JSON, as `nf.json.encode` does. */
    fun writeJson(self: LuaHandle.File, value: LuaValue): Boolean

    /**
     * When it last changed, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`), or `nil` when
     * there's nothing there.
     */
    fun modifiedTime(self: LuaHandle.File): Long?

    /**
     * Moves it (a file, or a folder and everything in it) to another path in the data directory, creating
     * folders above it as needed. Nothing already there is overwritten.
     */
    fun rename(self: LuaHandle.File, path: String): LuaHandle.File?

    /**
     * Copies a file to another path in the data directory, creating folders above it as needed. Nothing
     * already there is overwritten. Folders can't be copied.
     */
    fun copyTo(self: LuaHandle.File, path: String): LuaHandle.File?

    /** Creates the folder, and any above it. */
    fun createFolder(self: LuaHandle.File): Boolean

    /** Deletes a file or an empty folder. */
    fun delete(self: LuaHandle.File): Boolean
}

/**
 * What `nf.schedule.daily`, `weekly` and `cron` return: a schedule that will run at its next time,
 * until it is cancelled or its script unloads.
 */
interface ScheduleApi {
    /**
     * Ends it: it doesn't run again. What it last ran is kept, so a schedule made again with the same `id`
     * doesn't catch up a run it already made. Cancelling one that has ended does nothing.
     */
    fun cancel(self: LuaHandle.Schedule)

    /** Whether it will still run: `false` once it's cancelled or its script has unloaded. */
    fun isActive(self: LuaHandle.Schedule): Boolean

    /**
     * When it runs next, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`; `nf.time.format`
     * writes it as a date). `nil` once it has ended.
     */
    fun nextRun(self: LuaHandle.Schedule): Long?
}

/**
 * One player's HUD: the elements of the project's resource packs (`hud` in `pack.json`: bars, rows of
 * icons, lines of text, each at its place on the screen), shown with what a script sets here. An
 * element nobody set isn't drawn. It's drawn in their action bar, sent again whenever something
 * changes and every second, so `player:send_actionbar` shows its line alongside it while the HUD is up
 * (on its own for a moment, when the server can't measure it). What it shows is the server's to
 * remember, not saved: set it again when they join. A name that isn't one of the packs' elements, or
 * is another kind of element, is an error.
 */
interface HudApi {
    /** Whose HUD it is. */
    fun player(self: LuaHandle.Hud): LuaHandle.Player

    /**
     * Fills a bar: `value` out of `max`, shown as that much of its segments (to the nearest half segment,
     * for a bar with a half picture). A `value` past `max` or below 0 is shown full or empty; a `max` of 0
     * or less is an error. With `segments`, only that many of the bar's segments are drawn (1 to the bar's
     * own), placed by the bar's `align`.
     */
    fun fillBar(self: LuaHandle.Hud, name: String, value: Double, max: Double, options: HudFillOptions?): Boolean

    /**
     * Fills several bars as one long bar, each `per_segment` of `max` a segment: as many segments are
     * drawn as `max` needs, filling the first bar's, then the next's (rows of hearts, ten to a row). A bar
     * no segment reaches is removed. Every name must be a bar; `per_segment` and `max` more than 0.
     */
    fun fillBars(self: LuaHandle.Hud, names: List<String>, value: Double, max: Double, perSegment: Double): Long

    /** What a bar shows, or `nil` when nothing set it (or they're offline). */
    fun bar(self: LuaHandle.Hud, name: String): HudBar?

    /**
     * Shows these of a row's icons, in this order: status effects, buffs, keys held. An empty list shows
     * none. An icon the row doesn't have is an error.
     */
    fun setIcons(self: LuaHandle.Hud, name: String, icons: List<String>): Boolean

    /** The icons a row shows, in order, or `nil` when nothing set it (or they're offline). */
    fun icons(self: LuaHandle.Hud, name: String): List<String>?

    /**
     * Sets a line of text, on the action bar's own line where the line's `x` and `align` put it. The
     * server measures it with the game's font (`fonts/default.json`) to put it there; one it can't measure
     * isn't drawn.
     */
    fun setText(self: LuaHandle.Hud, name: String, text: String): Boolean

    /** A line's text, or `nil` when nothing set it (or they're offline). */
    fun text(self: LuaHandle.Hud, name: String): String?

    /** Stops drawing one element (any kind), until something sets it again. */
    fun remove(self: LuaHandle.Hud, name: String): Boolean

    /** Stops drawing every element: their action bar is the game's again. */
    fun clear(self: LuaHandle.Hud)
}
